/* Word breakdowns (roots, origin, memory hook, example sentences) prepared in a Claude session. Merged onto cards that have none; keyed by card id and checked against the definition. */
const WORD_ANALYSIS = {"2026||eschatology":{"def":"the branch of theology concerned with final matters—death, judgment, the afterlife, and the end of the world; a system of beliefs about such matters","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"eschato","type":"root","meaning":"last, final"},{"part":"-logy","type":"suffix","meaning":"study of"}],"origin":"From Greek eschatos (last) + -logia (study).","mnemonic":"Eschaton means last: the study of humanity's last day, the final chapter.","sentences":["The seminar on Christian eschatology examined how different traditions imagine judgment and the end of the world.","Her grandfather's eschatology shaped every sermon he gave, always pointing toward the final reckoning and what comes after death.","Scholars compared Norse and Zoroastrian eschatology to see how each culture pictured the last battle and the world's renewal."]},"2026||supplicate":{"def":"to ask or beg humbly and earnestly; to make a humble plea","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"sub-","type":"prefix","meaning":"under"},{"part":"plic","type":"root","meaning":"fold, bend"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin supplicare, to kneel or beg humbly (sub + plicare, to fold).","mnemonic":"Folding your knees under you to supply a plea: you supplicate on bended knee.","sentences":["The villagers gathered at the shrine to supplicate the gods for rain after months of punishing drought.","He had to supplicate before the council, hat in hand, to keep his small farm from seizure.","Rather than supplicate for forgiveness, the proud general simply turned and walked out of the throne room."]},"2026||extrajudicial":{"def":"occurring or done outside legal or judicial authority; not sanctioned by a court process","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"extra-","type":"prefix","meaning":"outside"},{"part":"judic","type":"root","meaning":"judgment, law"},{"part":"-ial","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin extra (outside) + judicialis, from judex (judge).","mnemonic":"Extra judge-ial: action taken outside the judge's courtroom.","sentences":["Human rights groups condemned the extrajudicial killings carried out without any trial or charge against the victims.","The governor's extrajudicial punishment of the protesters bypassed the courts entirely and sparked a legal challenge.","In the novel, a secret militia imposes extrajudicial justice on the town because nobody trusts the corrupt court."]},"2026||harbinger":{"def":"a person or thing that signals or foreshadows what is to come; a forerunner","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"harbinger","type":"root","meaning":"lodging forerunner"}],"origin":"From Old French herbergeor, one who arranges lodging ahead of an army; related to Germanic root for shelter.","mnemonic":"A harbor-ginger arrives early, announcing the ships behind it.","sentences":["The first robin of the year is often called a harbinger of spring, arriving before the warm weather does.","Economists saw the sudden jump in unemployment claims as a harbinger of a deeper recession ahead.","Dark clouds on the horizon were a harbinger of the storm that would flatten the fishing village by midnight."]},"2026||sundry":{"def":"various; several; diverse","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sundry","type":"root","meaning":"separate, various"}],"origin":"From Old English syndrig, meaning separate or distinct; related to sunder.","mnemonic":"Sundry items are sundered into many separate kinds, like a junk drawer.","sentences":["The drawer was stuffed with sundry odds and ends: batteries, rubber bands, loose keys, and a dried-out marker.","She thanked sundry friends and relatives who had helped with the move, naming each one in her speech.","The report listed sundry expenses, including postage, parking fees, and a handful of small office supplies."]},"2026||gaffe":{"def":"an embarrassing mistake, especially a social blunder or tactless remark","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"gaffe","type":"root","meaning":"blunder"}],"origin":"From French gaffe, a blunder, originally a boat hook; exact development uncertain.","mnemonic":"A gaffe makes you gasp and laugh at the same time.","sentences":["The candidate's gaffe about his opponent's hometown went viral and dominated the news cycle for two days.","Calling the bride by her ex-fiancé's name was a terrible gaffe, and the whole table fell silent.","Even seasoned diplomats occasionally commit a gaffe, such as mistaking one nation's flag for another's during a ceremony."]},"2026||diffident":{"def":"lacking self-confidence; shy, reserved, or modest in manner","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, not"},{"part":"fid","type":"root","meaning":"trust"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin diffidere, to mistrust (dis- + fidere, to trust); originally meant distrusting oneself.","mnemonic":"Di-FIDent: you have no FIDelity in yourself, so you speak softly.","sentences":["The diffident young intern rarely spoke in meetings, even when she knew the answer to the question.","He gave a diffident smile and hesitated at the door, unsure whether he was truly welcome at the party.","Despite his brilliance, the professor was diffident about his work and always downplayed his many accomplishments."]},"2026||incommensurable":{"def":"impossible to measure by the same standard; not comparable because of fundamental differences","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"com-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"mensur","type":"root","meaning":"measure"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"capable of"}],"origin":"From Latin incommensurabilis (in- + com- + mensurabilis, measurable).","mnemonic":"Cannot be measured with the same ruler: apples and, say, symphonies.","sentences":["Philosophers argue that the two moral frameworks are incommensurable, since no shared standard exists to judge between them.","Comparing the grief of losing a parent to the thrill of a promotion seemed incommensurable to her.","The diagonal and side of a square are incommensurable lengths, because their ratio cannot be written as a fraction."]},"2026||impiety":{"def":"lack of reverence or respect toward religion, God, or sacred things; irreverence","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"piety","type":"root","meaning":"dutiful reverence"}],"origin":"From Latin impietas (in- + pietas, dutifulness); via Old French impieté.","mnemonic":"Im-piety: the opposite of pious, with no prayer in sight.","sentences":["The preacher accused the townspeople of impiety for skipping worship and mocking the sacred rituals openly.","In ancient Athens, Socrates was charged with impiety for allegedly rejecting the city's gods and corrupting its youth.","Laughing during the funeral rites struck the elders as an act of shocking impiety."]},"2026||monolithic":{"def":"massive, uniform, rigid, or appearing as a single large and indivisible whole","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"mono-","type":"prefix","meaning":"single"},{"part":"lith","type":"root","meaning":"stone"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek monolithos, made of a single stone (monos + lithos).","mnemonic":"One giant stone, like a monolith: solid, uniform, immovable.","sentences":["Critics described the company as a monolithic bureaucracy where every decision had to pass through one rigid chain of command.","The monolithic block of granite loomed over the quarry, carved from a single enormous piece of rock.","Voters are rarely a monolithic group; even neighbors in the same district often disagree sharply on key issues."]},"2026||dispositive":{"def":"serving to settle, determine, or dispose of a matter; decisive in effect","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart"},{"part":"pos","type":"root","meaning":"place, put"},{"part":"-itive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin dispositivus, from disponere, to arrange or settle (dis- + ponere, to place).","mnemonic":"Disposes of the case: the evidence that puts the argument to rest.","sentences":["The judge ruled that the signed contract was dispositive, ending the dispute without any need for a full trial.","Her fingerprints on the weapon were not dispositive, since the defense offered a plausible innocent explanation.","The lawyers filed a dispositive motion hoping to settle the whole lawsuit before it reached a jury."]},"2026||senescence":{"def":"the process of aging; gradual deterioration associated with growing older","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"sen","type":"root","meaning":"old"},{"part":"-esc","type":"suffix","meaning":"becoming"},{"part":"-ence","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin senescere, to grow old, from senex (old).","mnemonic":"Senior-essence: the slow fade of growing old, like a senator's gray hair.","sentences":["Biologists studying cellular senescence want to understand why cells stop dividing as an organism grows older.","The gardener noted the senescence of the maple leaves as they yellowed and dropped in October.","Some researchers hope that therapies could slow senescence and keep people healthy well into their nineties."]},"2026||insouciance":{"def":"casual unconcern; lighthearted indifference; lack of worry","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"souci","type":"root","meaning":"care, worry"},{"part":"-ance","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From French insouciance (in- + soucier, to care), from Latin sollicitare.","mnemonic":"In-SOUCI-ance: no souci, no worries, shrugging while the ship sinks.","sentences":["He met the news of his layoff with such insouciance that his coworkers wondered if he had even heard it.","The teenager strolled into the exam with cheerful insouciance, as though nothing important was at stake.","Her insouciance in the face of danger either inspired the crew or terrified them, depending on the hour."]},"2026||mendicant":{"def":"a beggar","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"mend","type":"root","meaning":"fault, beg"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"}],"origin":"From Latin mendicare, to beg, from mendicus, needy.","mnemonic":"A mend-I-cant: I can't afford to mend my shoes, so I beg.","sentences":["A wandering mendicant sat outside the temple gates, holding out a wooden bowl for any passing coin or crust.","The monk, a mendicant by vow, owned nothing and relied on the kindness of villagers for every meal.","Medieval cities were full of mendicant friars who preached in the squares and lived entirely on donations."]},"2026||abridge":{"def":"to reduce or lessen in duration, scope, authority, etc.","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"bridge","type":"root","meaning":"shorten"}],"origin":"From Old French abregier, from Late Latin abbreviare, to shorten (related to brevis, short).","mnemonic":"Abridge: a short bridge, a quicker crossing than the full novel.","sentences":["The publisher decided to abridge the thousand-page novel so that younger readers could finish it in a weekend.","Critics warned that the new law would abridge citizens' freedom of speech during times of unrest.","The editor had to abridge the interview heavily to fit the transcript into a single magazine page."]},"2026||neologism":{"def":"a new word, meaning, usage, or phrase","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"neo-","type":"prefix","meaning":"new"},{"part":"log","type":"root","meaning":"word"},{"part":"-ism","type":"suffix","meaning":"practice, result"}],"origin":"From French néologisme, from Greek neos (new) + logos (word).","mnemonic":"NEO from The Matrix, plus LOGos: a brand-new word.","sentences":["The word 'selfie' began as a neologism but is now so common that dictionaries list it without comment.","Tech writers often invent a neologism to describe a product category that did not exist before.","The poet's neologism, a blend of two old words, confused critics at first and delighted readers later."]},"2026||adumbrate":{"def":"to produce a faint image or resemblance of; to outline or sketch","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"umbr","type":"root","meaning":"shadow"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin adumbrare, to shade or sketch (ad- + umbra, shadow).","mnemonic":"Ad-UMBRA-te: an umbrella casts a shadow, a faint outline of the real thing.","sentences":["In his opening remarks, the senator sought to adumbrate the major reforms he would propose in the coming session.","The early chapters adumbrate the tragedy to come, hinting at it through small ominous details.","The architect used charcoal to adumbrate the building's shape, giving the client a rough idea before the real drawings."]},"2026||unctuous":{"def":"excessively flattering or ingratiating; oily","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"unct","type":"root","meaning":"oil, anoint"},{"part":"-uous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin unctuosus, oily, from unctum (ointment), from unguere, to anoint.","mnemonic":"Un-CTUOUS: so much oil, he slides into conversation with slick flattery.","sentences":["The unctuous salesman praised every choice the couple made, hoping flattery would close the sale before lunch.","His unctuous greeting, all smiles and syrupy compliments, made the manager distrust him immediately.","The waiter's unctuous manner grew tiresome as he hovered, complimenting each diner's taste and refilling water constantly."]},"2026||fissiparous":{"def":"tending to break up into factions or groups; inclined to split or divide","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"fiss","type":"root","meaning":"split"},{"part":"-i-","type":"suffix","meaning":"connecting vowel"},{"part":"par","type":"root","meaning":"produce"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin fissus (split) + -parus (bearing), literally producing by splitting.","mnemonic":"FISSion plus parent: a group that keeps splitting into smaller offspring.","sentences":["The fissiparous opposition coalition broke into five rival factions within a month of the election.","Historians note the fissiparous nature of the movement, which divided over doctrine every few years.","Student clubs on the campus can be fissiparous, with one disagreement producing two competing societies overnight."]},"2026||divination":{"def":"the practice of seeking knowledge of the future or the unknown by supernatural means","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"divin","type":"root","meaning":"divine, god-inspired"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or process"}],"origin":"From Latin divinatio, from divinare, to foresee, from divinus (divine).","mnemonic":"Divine-ation: asking the divine, via cards or bones, what tomorrow brings.","sentences":["Ancient Romans practiced divination by studying the flight of birds before launching any major military campaign.","She treated tarot as a playful form of divination, not a serious way to predict her future.","The priest's divination with animal bones was meant to reveal whether the harvest would be rich or poor."]},"2026||coterie":{"def":"a small group of people with shared interests or tastes, especially one that is exclusive or cliquish","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"coterie","type":"root","meaning":"tenant group"}],"origin":"From French coterie, originally a group of tenant farmers; later a close social circle.","mnemonic":"A COzy-TEa-RIght circle: a tight clique sipping tea behind closed doors.","sentences":["The novelist surrounded herself with a small coterie of loyal friends who read every draft before publication.","Access to the senator required getting past his coterie of aides, who guarded his schedule fiercely.","A coterie of wealthy collectors dominated the gallery's openings, shutting out newcomers who lacked the right connections."]},"2026||retinue":{"def":"a group of advisers, assistants, or other attendants accompanying an important person","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"tin","type":"root","meaning":"hold"},{"part":"-ue","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Old French retenue, from retenir, to retain, from Latin retinere (hold back).","mnemonic":"Retinue: those retained, kept on staff to follow the VIP.","sentences":["The queen arrived with a large retinue of servants, guards, and ladies-in-waiting trailing behind her carriage.","The pop star's retinue filled an entire floor of the hotel, from stylists to bodyguards and assistants.","A retinue of reporters followed the minister down the corridor, shouting questions at every step."]},"2026||luddite":{"def":"a person opposed to new technology or ways of working, especially one who resists technological change","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"Luddite","type":"root","meaning":"after Ned Ludd"}],"origin":"From the Luddites, English textile workers (1811-16) who smashed machines, named after Ned Ludd.","mnemonic":"Ned Ludd smashed looms; now a luddite smashes (or refuses) your smartphone.","sentences":["My grandfather is a proud luddite who refuses to own a smartphone and still pays every bill by check.","Calling the union leader a luddite was unfair, since she supported automation as long as workers were retrained.","The village elder, a self-declared luddite, argued that the new machines would destroy the craft his family had practiced for generations."]},"2026||acolyte":{"def":"a person assisting a priest in a religious service; more generally, a devoted follower or assistant of an important person","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"acolyte","type":"root","meaning":"follower, attendant"}],"origin":"From Greek akolouthos, a follower or attendant, via Latin acolytus.","mnemonic":"A-CO-light: a candle-carrying helper trailing behind the priest.","sentences":["The young acolyte carried the candles down the aisle and stood quietly beside the priest during the ceremony.","The professor's acolytes hung on his every word and defended his theories at every conference.","Surrounded by acolytes who praised his every idea, the founder lost touch with what customers actually wanted."]},"2026||chimera":{"def":"a thing that is hoped or wished for but in fact is illusory or impossible to achieve","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"chimera","type":"root","meaning":"mythical fire-breather"}],"origin":"From Greek khimaira, a mythical fire-breathing monster of lion, goat, and serpent parts.","mnemonic":"A monster stitched from parts of several beasts: an impossible fantasy.","sentences":["Peace in the region proved a chimera, as every ceasefire collapsed within days of being signed.","Chasing the chimera of overnight riches, he sank his savings into one risky scheme after another.","For decades the perfect, bug-free program remained a chimera that engineers pursued but never actually achieved."]},"2026||imbibe":{"def":"to drink, especially alcohol; to absorb or assimilate ideas, knowledge, or information","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in"},{"part":"bib","type":"root","meaning":"drink"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin imbibere, to drink in (in- + bibere, to drink).","mnemonic":"I-BIBE: wearing a bib, you drink in drinks, or ideas.","sentences":["Guests were encouraged to imbibe freely at the wedding, and the bar stayed busy until midnight.","As a child, she would imbibe every story her grandmother told, soaking up folklore and old family history.","The students imbibed the professor's teachings so thoroughly that his phrases appeared in their essays for years."]},"2026||callous":{"def":"showing or having an insensitive and cruel disregard for others","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"call","type":"root","meaning":"hard skin"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin callosus, hardened, from callum (hard skin).","mnemonic":"A callus on the heart: skin so hard it feels no pain.","sentences":["His callous remark about the flood victims shocked the audience and drew immediate calls for an apology.","Years in the emergency room had left her anything but callous; she still cried after the hardest nights.","The landlord's callous refusal to fix the heating in January left the elderly tenants shivering in their coats."]},"2026||dialectic":{"def":"the art or practice of investigating or discussing the truth of opinions through logical argument; a process in which opposing ideas or forces interact and lead to the development of a new or different understanding","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"dia-","type":"prefix","meaning":"through, between"},{"part":"lect","type":"root","meaning":"speak, choose"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"art, relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek dialektike (techne), the art of discourse, from dialegesthai, to converse.","mnemonic":"Dia-LECT-ic: two voices talking through an argument until a new truth emerges.","sentences":["Plato's dialogues use the dialectic, with Socrates questioning each claim until a contradiction or deeper truth appears.","Marx described history as a dialectic of conflicting classes, each struggle producing a new social order.","The debate team practiced dialectic by arguing both sides, hoping that clashing views would sharpen their understanding."]},"2026||bricolage":{"def":"something constructed or created from a diverse range of available things; the process of constructing or creating something in this way","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"bricol","type":"root","meaning":"tinker, make do"},{"part":"-age","type":"suffix","meaning":"result, process"}],"origin":"From French bricolage, from bricoler, to tinker or do odd jobs.","mnemonic":"BRICks and COLLAGE: building something from whatever pieces lie around.","sentences":["The sculpture was a bricolage of bottle caps, bicycle chains, and scrap metal gathered from the junkyard.","Her fashion style is pure bricolage, mixing thrift-store jackets, family heirlooms, and homemade accessories into one outfit.","The band's sound was a bricolage of folk melodies, electronic loops, and recorded street noise."]},"2026||synoptic":{"def":"presenting or taking a comprehensive or general view of something; affording an overall summary or perspective","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"syn-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"opt","type":"root","meaning":"see"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek synoptikos, seeing everything together (syn- + opsis, view).","mnemonic":"SYN-OPTic: optics that see everything together in one glance.","sentences":["The textbook gave a synoptic overview of the war, covering causes, battles, and consequences in a single chapter.","Meteorologists draw a synoptic chart to show weather conditions across a whole region at one moment.","Her synoptic view of the company's problems let her spot patterns that specialists focused on single departments missed."]},"2025||nonsectarian":{"def":"not limited to or associated with a particular religious denomination or political group","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"non-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"sect","type":"root","meaning":"faction, sect"},{"part":"-arian","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From non- + sectarian, from Late Latin secta (sect, following).","mnemonic":"Non-sect: open to everyone, no sect gets a special seat.","sentences":["The hospital was founded as a nonsectarian institution, treating patients of every faith without preference.","The school's nonsectarian charter meant that no single religion could shape the curriculum or the daily assemblies.","Organizers kept the charity dinner nonsectarian so Christians, Muslims, Jews, and atheists could all attend comfortably."]},"2025||mendacious":{"def":"not truthful; lying or prone to deception","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"mend","type":"root","meaning":"lie, fault"},{"part":"-acious","type":"suffix","meaning":"prone to"}],"origin":"From Latin mendax, lying, related to mendum (fault).","mnemonic":"MEN-DAcious: men who dash to say something false; liars.","sentences":["The mendacious witness changed his story three times, and the jury stopped believing anything he said.","Critics called the advertisement mendacious, since its claims about the product's benefits were simply untrue.","A mendacious rumor spread through the office, started by someone who gained from the false story."]},"2025||streamlined":{"def":"made more efficient or simple by eliminating unnecessary parts","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"stream","type":"root","meaning":"flow"},{"part":"-line","type":"root","meaning":"line"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"past participle"}],"origin":"From stream + line + -ed; streamline originally described shapes that reduce drag in fluid flow.","mnemonic":"Lined up like a stream: nothing sticks out to slow you down.","sentences":["The company introduced a streamlined application process that cut the wait from six weeks to three days.","The streamlined body of the dolphin lets it glide through water with very little resistance.","After the redesign, the kitchen felt streamlined, with every pan and spice in reach and no wasted steps."]},"2025||vaunt":{"def":"to boast about or praise something, often excessively","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"vaunt","type":"root","meaning":"boast"}],"origin":"From Old French vanter, from Late Latin vanitare, to boast, related to Latin vanus (empty).","mnemonic":"VAUNT sounds like flaunt and haunt: loudly show off, empty talk.","sentences":["The mayor liked to vaunt the city's new stadium at every opportunity, whether or not anyone asked.","Rather than vaunt his wealth, the billionaire drove an old truck and wore the same jacket for years.","The brochure vaunted the resort's golf course and spa in glowing terms that the actual visit never matched."]},"2025||contravene":{"def":"to act against or violate a rule, law, or agreement","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"contra-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"ven","type":"root","meaning":"come"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin contravenire, to come against (contra- + venire, to come).","mnemonic":"Contra-VENE: you come against the rule, like walking against traffic.","sentences":["The new policy would contravene international law, according to the legal advisers who reviewed the draft.","Any employee who tries to contravene the safety rules risks immediate suspension from the factory floor.","The company's secret deal appeared to contravene the terms of its agreement with regulators."]},"2025||bustle":{"def":"to move in a hurried or energetic manner; a state of busy activity","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"bustle","type":"root","meaning":"hurry"}],"origin":"Probably from Middle English busken, to prepare oneself; origin uncertain.","mnemonic":"Bustle sounds like hustle: rush around busily with muscle.","sentences":["Shoppers bustle through the market on Saturday mornings, weaving between stalls piled with fruit and flowers.","Commuters bustle along the platforms every morning, and the noise overwhelmed him after a quiet week in the mountains.","Waiters bustled between tables as the restaurant filled, carrying trays high and calling orders to the kitchen."]},"2025||ensconce":{"def":"to settle oneself securely or comfortably in a place","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, make"},{"part":"sconce","type":"root","meaning":"shelter, fort"}],"origin":"From en- + sconce, a small fort or shelter, from Dutch schans.","mnemonic":"En-SCONCE: tucked in like a candle inside its wall sconce.","sentences":["She ensconced herself in the armchair by the fire with a blanket, a novel, and a pot of tea.","The senator was ensconced in his office, refusing calls while aides scrambled to manage the scandal.","After years of traveling, the writer finally ensconced himself in a quiet cottage by the sea."]},"2025||disconsolate":{"def":"deeply unhappy or without comfort; inconsolable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"without"},{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"with"},{"part":"sol","type":"root","meaning":"comfort"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin disconsolatus (dis- + consolatus, comforted), from consolari.","mnemonic":"Dis-CONSOLE-ate: so sad that no one can console you.","sentences":["The disconsolate child sat on the curb, sobbing, after her ice cream fell to the ground.","Fans left the stadium disconsolate after their team lost the final in the closing seconds.","He wandered the empty house, disconsolate, unable to find any comfort after his dog's death."]},"2025||sardonic":{"def":"grimly mocking or cynical","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sardonic","type":"root","meaning":"bitterly mocking"}],"origin":"From Greek sardonios, via French; linked to a Sardinian plant said to twist the face into a grin.","mnemonic":"A SARcastic DONkey: grimly grinning and jeering at everyone.","sentences":["His sardonic smile suggested he found the manager's inspirational speech both ridiculous and somewhat insulting.","The comedian's sardonic wit, dry and biting, turned every corporate cliché into a punchline.","She gave a sardonic laugh when told the project would be easy, having seen this promise before."]},"2025||autarky":{"def":"economic independence or self-sufficiency, especially at a national level","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"aut","type":"root","meaning":"self"},{"part":"-arky","type":"suffix","meaning":"rule, sufficiency"}],"origin":"From Greek autarkeia, self-sufficiency (autos, self + arkein, to suffice).","mnemonic":"AUTo-ARKy: a nation self-driving, needing no imports.","sentences":["The dictator pursued autarky, banning most imports in an attempt to make the country entirely self-reliant.","Economists warn that autarky usually leaves a nation poorer, because it gives up the benefits of trade.","North Korea's official ideology of self-reliance has long aimed at a degree of autarky, though it still depends on aid."]},"2025||contrition":{"def":"the feeling of sincere remorse or guilt for wrongdoing","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"trit","type":"root","meaning":"rub, grind"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin contritio, a grinding down, from conterere (to crush); a heart crushed by guilt.","mnemonic":"Con-TRITE: a heart ground down by guilt, begging forgiveness.","sentences":["He expressed deep contrition for the harm he caused, apologizing in person to every family affected.","The judge reduced the sentence after seeing the defendant's genuine contrition and his efforts to make amends.","Her contrition was obvious; she stood with tears in her eyes, unable to meet her friend's gaze."]},"2025||spurn":{"def":"to reject or refuse with disdain or contempt","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"spurn","type":"root","meaning":"kick away"}],"origin":"From Old English spurnan, to kick; related to spur.","mnemonic":"Spurn sounds like spur: kick someone away with your heel.","sentences":["She chose to spurn the generous job offer, convinced the company's values clashed with her own.","The prince's proposal was spurned by the duchess, who laughed and left the room without a word.","Villagers spurn strangers who ignore local customs, turning their backs when the visitors approach."]},"2025||supercilious":{"def":"behaving or looking as though one thinks one is superior to others","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"super-","type":"prefix","meaning":"above"},{"part":"cili","type":"root","meaning":"eyelid, eyebrow"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin superciliosus, from supercilium, eyebrow (super + cilium); the raised brow of disdain.","mnemonic":"Super-CILIA: raised eyebrows above everyone, looking down at you.","sentences":["The supercilious waiter sneered at our order and sighed as if our tastes offended him personally.","Her supercilious tone made every compliment sound like an insult, and colleagues avoided her at lunch.","With a supercilious glance at their cheap coats, the host directed the guests to the back table."]},"2025||blather":{"def":"talk long-windedly without making very much sense","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"blather","type":"root","meaning":"babble"}],"origin":"From Old Norse blathra, to talk nonsense; imitative in origin.","mnemonic":"Blather rhymes with bladder: lots of hot air and no point.","sentences":["The speaker continued to blather for an hour, and the audience slowly lost track of any actual point.","Politicians tend to blather on during interviews, avoiding the question while filling the allotted time.","Please do not blather about your weekend plans while I am trying to finish this report."]},"2025||timorous":{"def":"showing or suffering from nervousness, fear, or a lack of confidence","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"timor","type":"root","meaning":"fear"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin timorosus, fearful, from timor (fear).","mnemonic":"TIMid + horror: a mouse that trembles at every noise.","sentences":["The timorous puppy hid under the couch whenever thunder rumbled or a stranger knocked at the door.","He made a timorous request for a raise, whispering it so quietly that his boss asked him to repeat it.","Her timorous nature kept her from speaking up, though she often had the best ideas in the room."]},"2025||torpor":{"def":"a state of physical or mental inactivity; lethargy","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"torp","type":"root","meaning":"numb, stiff"},{"part":"-or","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin torpor, numbness, from torpere, to be stiff or numb.","mnemonic":"Torpor sounds like torpedo-ed: struck down and sluggish, unable to move.","sentences":["After the huge meal, a pleasant torpor settled over the table, and nobody wanted to move from their chairs.","Hibernating bears fall into a torpor in which their heartbeat and body temperature drop sharply.","The heat wave left the whole town in a listless torpor, with shops closed and streets empty by noon."]},"2025||casuistry":{"def":"the use of clever but unsound reasoning, especially in relation to moral questions; sophistry","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"casu","type":"root","meaning":"case"},{"part":"-ist","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"},{"part":"-ry","type":"suffix","meaning":"practice"}],"origin":"From Latin casus (case) via French casuistique; the practice of applying general rules to particular cases.","mnemonic":"CASE-uistry: twisting each case with clever excuses until wrong looks right.","sentences":["The lawyer's casuistry turned a plain act of fraud into a tangled debate about technical definitions.","Critics dismissed his defense as casuistry, a clever string of arguments meant to excuse the clearly wrong.","Moral casuistry can help judge hard cases, but it can also be abused to rationalize nearly anything."]},"2025||inchoate":{"def":"not yet completed or fully developed; rudimentary","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"on, at"},{"part":"cho","type":"root","meaning":"harness, begin"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin inchoare, to begin (originally to hitch up, from cohum, a yoke strap).","mnemonic":"In-CHOate: ideas just begun, still half-formed in the cradle.","sentences":["The movement was still inchoate, with vague goals and no leadership or clear plan for the future.","Her inchoate feelings of unease had not yet formed into any clear thought she could put into words.","The architect showed an inchoate sketch of the building, with only rough shapes and no details."]},"2025||incipient":{"def":"beginning to exist or appear; in an initial stage","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"into, on"},{"part":"cip","type":"root","meaning":"take, begin"},{"part":"-ient","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin incipiens, present participle of incipere, to begin (in- + capere, to take).","mnemonic":"IN-CIPient: the initial sip, just starting to taste it.","sentences":["The doctor caught the incipient infection early, before any serious symptoms had a chance to develop.","An incipient smile crept across her face as she began to recognize the old song.","Signs of an incipient revolt appeared in the streets, with small protests growing larger each week."]},"2025||anodyne":{"def":"not likely to provoke dissent or offense; inoffensive, often deliberately so","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"an-","type":"prefix","meaning":"without"},{"part":"odyn","type":"root","meaning":"pain"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"ending"}],"origin":"From Greek anodynos, painless (an- + odyne, pain).","mnemonic":"AN-ODYNE: without pain, bland enough that no one is bothered.","sentences":["The politician gave an anodyne speech full of safe phrases, carefully avoiding anything that could offend voters.","The hotel's anodyne decor, all beige walls and generic art, was pleasant enough but instantly forgettable.","Critics found the film anodyne, a mild comedy that never risked saying anything bold or controversial."]},"2025||bonhomie":{"def":"cheerful friendliness; geniality","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"bon","type":"root","meaning":"good"},{"part":"homme","type":"root","meaning":"man"},{"part":"-ie","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From French bonhomie, from bonhomme, a good-natured man (bon + homme).","mnemonic":"BON HOMMIE: a good guy who greets everyone with warmth.","sentences":["The host's easy bonhomie put every guest at ease within minutes of walking through the door.","A spirit of bonhomie filled the pub as strangers shared stories and bought each other drinks.","His natural bonhomie made him a favorite coach, always joking and slapping players on the back."]},"2025||torrid":{"def":"full of difficulty or tribulation","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"torr","type":"root","meaning":"parch, dry with heat"},{"part":"-id","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin torridus, parched, from torrere, to burn or dry.","mnemonic":"TORRID like torrent of trouble: a scorching, brutal stretch.","sentences":["The couple endured a torrid year of lawsuits, layoffs, and illness that nearly broke their marriage.","The team had a torrid time in the playoffs, losing key players and suffering one heartbreaking defeat after another.","After a torrid week of deadlines and angry clients, she finally took a long weekend to recover."]},"2025||contradistinction":{"def":"distinction made by contrasting the different qualities of two things","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"contra-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart"},{"part":"tinct","type":"root","meaning":"mark, distinguish"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From contra- + distinction, from Latin distinctio, from distinguere (to separate).","mnemonic":"Contra-distinction: set two things against each other to see how they differ.","sentences":["The author defined freedom in contradistinction to license, stressing that responsible choice marked the real difference.","In contradistinction to his cautious brother, Marco leapt at every risky opportunity that came along.","The museum placed ancient pottery in contradistinction to modern ceramics so visitors could see how the craft evolved."]},"2025||pecuniary":{"def":"relating to or consisting of money","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pecun","type":"root","meaning":"money, wealth"},{"part":"-iary","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin pecuniarius, from pecunia, money, from pecu (cattle, an early form of wealth).","mnemonic":"Pecu means cattle: when herds were your cash, money was pecuniary.","sentences":["The judge fined the company for pecuniary damages, requiring it to pay back the money customers had lost.","He had no pecuniary interest in the deal, so he could give unbiased advice about the purchase.","Her pecuniary troubles began when the business failed, leaving a long list of unpaid bills."]},"2025||enervate":{"def":"cause (someone) to feel drained of energy or vitality; weaken","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"nerv","type":"root","meaning":"sinew, strength"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin enervare, to weaken (e- + nervus, sinew).","mnemonic":"E-NERVE-ate: your nerve and energy are drained out of you; not energize!","sentences":["The oppressive humidity can enervate even the fittest hikers, leaving them sluggish within an hour.","Months of endless meetings began to enervate the team, who lost both enthusiasm and sharpness.","The long illness had enervated him so much that climbing the stairs left him gasping and shaky."]},"2025||peevish":{"def":"easily irritated, especially by unimportant things","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"peev","type":"root","meaning":"irritable"},{"part":"-ish","type":"suffix","meaning":"like, tending to"}],"origin":"From Middle English pevish, meaning foolish or spiteful; origin uncertain.","mnemonic":"PEEVE-ish: always peeved, like a toddler missing a nap.","sentences":["The peevish customer complained about the tiny crack in the cup, the music, and the temperature of the room.","Tired and hungry, the children grew peevish and snapped at each other over the smallest things.","Her peevish reply to the harmless question made everyone wonder what had put her in such a mood."]},"2025||rote":{"def":"mechanical or habitual repetition of something to be learned","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"rote","type":"root","meaning":"routine repetition"}],"origin":"From Middle English rote, meaning habitual course; origin uncertain, possibly Latin rota (wheel).","mnemonic":"Rote like ROuTinE: repeating over and over like a wheel.","sentences":["He learned the multiplication tables by rote, repeating them aloud until they came without thinking.","Learning by rote may help with memorizing dates, but it rarely builds deep understanding of a subject.","She recited the oath by rote, her mind wandering far from the words she spoke."]},"2025||cinch":{"def":"an extremely easy task","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"cinch","type":"root","meaning":"saddle girth"}],"origin":"From Spanish cincha, a saddle girth, from Latin cingula (belt); later meaning a sure thing.","mnemonic":"A tightened saddle strap: a sure grip, a sure win, nothing to it.","sentences":["After weeks of practice, the driving test was a cinch, and she passed without making a single mistake.","He assumed beating the weaker team would be a cinch, but the match proved far harder.","Assembling the bookshelf turned out to be a cinch, taking barely twenty minutes with the clear instructions."]},"2025||apposite":{"def":"apt in the circumstances or in relation to something","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ap-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"pos","type":"root","meaning":"place, put"},{"part":"-ite","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin appositus, put near, from apponere (ad- + ponere, to place).","mnemonic":"AP-POSITE: positioned right at the spot, perfectly placed.","sentences":["Her apposite quotation from Shakespeare captured the mood of the moment better than a long speech could.","The lawyer cited an apposite case, one whose facts closely matched those of the dispute before the court.","It was an apposite remark, and the room fell quiet because everyone knew it was exactly right."]},"2025||agglomerate":{"def":"collect or form into a mass or group","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"glomer","type":"root","meaning":"ball, mass"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin agglomerare, to wind into a ball (ad- + glomus, ball of yarn).","mnemonic":"Add a GLOM: stick things together into one big lump.","sentences":["Over time, small villages agglomerate into larger towns as farmland gives way to roads and shops.","Dust particles in space agglomerate slowly, clumping together until they form the seeds of new planets.","Media companies agglomerate by merging, until a handful of firms control most of what people watch."]},"2025||hypothecate":{"def":"pledge (money) by law to a specific purpose","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"hypo-","type":"prefix","meaning":"under"},{"part":"thec","type":"root","meaning":"place, put"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Medieval Latin hypothecare, from Greek hypotheke, a pledge or deposit (hypo- + tithenai, to place).","mnemonic":"HYPO-theCATE: put money under a pledge, locked to one purpose.","sentences":["Some governments hypothecate the fuel tax, spending it only on roads rather than adding it to general funds.","The bank allowed the shipowner to hypothecate his vessel as security for the loan.","Critics say it is unwise to hypothecate tax revenue, since it limits flexibility when priorities change."]},"2025||devolution":{"def":"the transfer or delegation of power to a lower level, especially by central government to local or regional administration","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down, away"},{"part":"volut","type":"root","meaning":"roll, turn"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Late Latin devolutio, from devolvere, to roll down (de- + volvere, to roll).","mnemonic":"De-VOLUTION: power rolls downhill from the capital to the regions.","sentences":["Scotland gained its own parliament through devolution, receiving control of education and health from London.","Supporters of devolution argue that local officials understand community needs better than distant national ministers.","The treaty promised devolution of powers to the provinces, giving them more say over taxes and schools."]},"2025||coterminous":{"def":"having the same boundaries or extent in space, time, or meaning","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"co-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, with"},{"part":"termin","type":"root","meaning":"boundary, end"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin conterminus, bordering (con- + terminus, boundary).","mnemonic":"CO-TERMINus: sharing the same terminus, same end line.","sentences":["The county's borders are coterminous with the old kingdom, matching its edges almost exactly.","The two lease agreements are coterminous, both expiring on the last day of December.","In his mind, patriotism and loyalty to the party were coterminous, and he could not separate them."]},"2025||entrepot":{"def":"a port, city, or other center to which goods are brought for import and export, and for collection and distribution","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"entre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"between"},{"part":"pot","type":"root","meaning":"put, place"}],"origin":"From French entrepôt, a warehouse, from entreposer, to store (entre- + poser, to place).","mnemonic":"ENTRE-POT: a pot between buyers and sellers, where goods pass through.","sentences":["Singapore grew rich as an entrepot, receiving goods from across Asia and shipping them on to the world.","The medieval city served as an entrepot where merchants from east and west exchanged silk, spices, and silver.","Hong Kong's role as an entrepot declined as mainland ports built their own direct trade links."]},"2025||obstreperous":{"def":"noisy and difficult to control","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ob-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"strep","type":"root","meaning":"make noise"},{"part":"-erous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin obstreperus, clamorous, from obstrepere (ob- + strepere, to make noise).","mnemonic":"OB-STREP: strep-loud and stubborn, noisy against every order.","sentences":["The obstreperous crowd shouted down the speaker and refused to quiet down despite repeated requests from the police.","A teacher must keep calm with obstreperous pupils who talk, tease, and rebel against every instruction.","The obstreperous toddler screamed in the grocery aisle and refused to let anyone put him in the cart."]},"2025||revanchism":{"def":"a policy of seeking to retaliate, especially to recover lost territory","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"revanche","type":"root","meaning":"revenge"},{"part":"-ism","type":"suffix","meaning":"policy, doctrine"}],"origin":"From French revanche (revenge) + -ism; first used of French desire to retake Alsace-Lorraine after 1871.","mnemonic":"REVenge plus CHANGE hands: a policy of winning back lost land.","sentences":["Historians link the rise of revanchism after the war to the humiliating loss of territory and national pride.","The president's revanchism, his constant talk of reclaiming lost lands, alarmed neighboring countries.","Postwar revanchism fueled calls to retake the border provinces, even at the risk of another conflict."]},"2025||distend":{"def":"swell or cause to swell by pressure from inside","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart"},{"part":"tend","type":"root","meaning":"stretch"}],"origin":"From Latin distendere, to stretch apart (dis- + tendere, to stretch).","mnemonic":"Dis-TEND: tension stretches your belly apart like a balloon.","sentences":["Gas can distend the stomach, causing painful bloating and a tight, swollen feeling after a heavy meal.","Her stomach was distended from hunger, a grim sign of the famine that had gripped the region.","The balloon began to distend as he blew into it, the rubber thinning as it stretched."]},"2025||cogitate":{"def":"think deeply about something; meditate or reflect","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"co-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"agit","type":"root","meaning":"drive, shake, turn over"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin cogitare, to think (co- + agitare, to turn over).","mnemonic":"CO-GIT-ate: gears grinding together in the head.","sentences":["He sat by the window for an hour to cogitate on the job offer before giving a final answer.","Philosophers cogitate on questions of existence, sometimes spending years on a single puzzling idea.","Let me cogitate on your proposal over the weekend, and I will reply on Monday."]},"2025||pertinacious":{"def":"holding firmly to an opinion or a course of action","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"per-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"tenac","type":"root","meaning":"holding"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin pertinax, holding fast (per- + tenax, tenacious, from tenere, to hold).","mnemonic":"PER-TENACious: a very tenacious grip that never lets go.","sentences":["The pertinacious reporter kept calling the official for weeks until he finally agreed to answer her questions.","Her pertinacious defense of the old plan frustrated colleagues who had long since accepted the new direction.","The pertinacious climber refused to turn back despite the storm, holding firmly to her goal of reaching the summit."]},"2024||transpose":{"def":"cause (two or more things) to change places with each other","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"trans-","type":"prefix","meaning":"across"},{"part":"pose","type":"root","meaning":"place, put"}],"origin":"From Old French transposer, from Latin trans- (across) + French poser (to place).","mnemonic":"TRANS-POSE: put things across, swapping their places like a game of chairs.","sentences":["The typist managed to transpose the digits in the phone number, so every caller reached the wrong person.","Musicians often transpose a song into a lower key so the singer can reach the notes comfortably.","In the equation, you can transpose the two terms across the equals sign if you change their signs."]},"2024||truancy":{"def":"the action of staying away from school without good reason; absenteeism","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"tru-","type":"root","meaning":"idle beggar"},{"part":"-ancy","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Old French truant, 'vagabond, beggar', of Celtic origin.","mnemonic":"A truck-driving kid ditches school, leaving the classroom desk empty.","sentences":["The principal warned that repeated truancy would lead to a meeting with the boy's parents.","City officials launched a campaign to reduce truancy after attendance dropped sharply last spring.","Her truancy began quietly, with a skipped homeroom, and soon she was missing entire weeks of class."]},"2024||efface":{"def":"to rub out, erase, or obliterate (outlines, traces, inscriptions, etc.)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ef-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"face","type":"root","meaning":"face, form"}],"origin":"From French effacer, from Latin ex- 'out' + facies 'face'.","mnemonic":"Wipe the face off a coin until nothing remains.","sentences":["Centuries of rain had begun to efface the names carved into the old gravestones.","He tried to efface every trace of his mistake before his boss returned.","Time and weather may efface the lettering on the stone, but the monument itself will stand for centuries."]},"2024||peremptory":{"def":"leaving no opportunity for denial or refusal; imperative","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"per-","type":"prefix","meaning":"completely"},{"part":"empt","type":"root","meaning":"take, destroy"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin peremptorius 'destructive, decisive', from perimere 'to cut off'.","mnemonic":"A per-emptory order empties your options; just obey.","sentences":["The general issued a peremptory command, and no soldier dared question it.","Her peremptory tone made it clear that the discussion was over and that no one should argue further.","The judge gave a peremptory wave of the hand and dismissed the objection."]},"2024||aleatory":{"def":"dependent on luck or chance","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"alea","type":"root","meaning":"dice, chance"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin aleatorius, from alea 'a die, game of chance'.","mnemonic":"Alea sounds like 'a lay-dice': roll them and see.","sentences":["The composer used aleatory methods, letting dice decide the order of the musical phrases.","Many gamblers forget that the outcomes of roulette are purely aleatory and cannot be predicted.","The novel's aleatory structure meant each reader encountered the chapters in a different order."]},"2024||transfiguration":{"def":"a complete change of form or appearance into a more beautiful or spiritual state","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"trans-","type":"prefix","meaning":"across"},{"part":"figur","type":"root","meaning":"form, shape"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or result"}],"origin":"From Latin transfiguratio, from transfigurare 'change shape'.","mnemonic":"Trans-figure: your figure transforms into something glowing.","sentences":["Pilgrims gathered to celebrate the transfiguration, when the teacher's face and clothes shone like light.","After months of renovation, the dingy warehouse underwent a startling transfiguration into a gleaming gallery.","The poem describes the transfiguration of a gray winter field into gold at sunrise."]},"2024||manifold":{"def":"many and various","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"mani-","type":"root","meaning":"many"},{"part":"-fold","type":"suffix","meaning":"times over"}],"origin":"From Old English manigfeald, from manig 'many' + -feald '-fold'.","mnemonic":"Many folds: a map with manifold creases.","sentences":["The company faced manifold problems, from broken equipment to unpaid suppliers and angry customers.","Her reasons for leaving were manifold, and she listed them one by one.","The manifold wonders of the rainforest kept the scientists busy for decades."]},"2024||depravity":{"def":"moral corruption; wickedness","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"completely"},{"part":"prav","type":"root","meaning":"crooked, bad"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin pravus 'crooked, perverse' via depravare 'to corrupt'.","mnemonic":"De-pray-ity: a person who never prays sinks into wickedness.","sentences":["The novel exposes the depravity of a ruling class that tortured people for amusement.","Witnesses at the trial were shocked by the sheer depravity of a crime committed with such cold cruelty.","Moralists of the Victorian era preached that idleness and drink would lead inevitably to depravity and ruin."]},"2024||mire":{"def":"a situation or state of difficulty, distress, or embarrassment from which it is hard to extricate oneself","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"mire","type":"root","meaning":"swamp, bog"}],"origin":"From Old Norse mýrr 'bog, swamp'.","mnemonic":"Mire sounds like 'mud': you get stuck in it.","sentences":["The failed merger left the firm stuck in a legal mire for years.","Soldiers trudged through the mire, their boots sinking deeper with each step.","He tried to pull his family out of the financial mire, but debts kept piling up."]},"2024||uppish":{"def":"arrogantly self-assertive","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"up","type":"root","meaning":"raised, high"},{"part":"-ish","type":"suffix","meaning":"somewhat like"}],"origin":"From English up + -ish, early 18th century, meaning 'putting oneself up'.","mnemonic":"Nose stuck up in the air, he is uppish.","sentences":["The uppish new manager lectured veteran staff about things they had known for years.","Nobody liked the uppish young clerk who corrected everyone's grammar in public.","Her uppish manner at dinner left the other guests rolling their eyes."]},"2024||solemn":{"def":"formal and dignified","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"solemn","type":"root","meaning":"yearly, ceremonial"}],"origin":"From Latin sollemnis 'established, ceremonial', from sollus 'whole' + annus 'year'.","mnemonic":"Sol-emn sounds like 'solo hymn': quiet, serious, dignified.","sentences":["The mourners stood in solemn silence as the coffin was lowered, and no one dared to whisper.","He made a solemn promise before the court to tell the whole truth.","The president's solemn expression and quiet voice showed how seriously he took the occasion of the memorial."]},"2024||concomitant":{"def":"existing or occurring with something else, as a related feature or circumstance","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"comit","type":"root","meaning":"accompany"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin concomitari, from con- 'with' + comes 'companion'.","mnemonic":"A con-comrade: it comes along with something else.","sentences":["Rapid growth in the town brought concomitant problems, including traffic jams, rising rents, and overcrowded schools.","The new tax rise and its concomitant paperwork frustrated small business owners.","Fame and its concomitant loss of privacy weighed heavily on the young actress."]},"2024||ire":{"def":"intense anger","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ire","type":"root","meaning":"anger"}],"origin":"From Latin ira 'anger'.","mnemonic":"Ire rhymes with fire: anger that burns.","sentences":["The mayor's decision drew the ire of residents who wanted the park saved.","His face reddened with ire as he read the insulting letter, and he crumpled it in his fist.","Fans vented their ire on social media for hours after the referee's baffling call cost their team the match."]},"2024||contumacious":{"def":"stubbornly perverse or rebellious; willfully and obstinately disobedient","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"intensive"},{"part":"tum","type":"root","meaning":"swell with pride"},{"part":"-acious","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"}],"origin":"From Latin contumax 'stubborn, insolent', possibly from tumere 'to swell'.","mnemonic":"Con-TUM-acious: a tumultuous kid who never obeys.","sentences":["The contumacious witness refused to answer the judge's questions despite repeated warnings.","Teachers found the contumacious student impossible to discipline, since he openly defied every rule and mocked every warning.","The contumacious council defied the king's orders and kept meeting in secret."]},"2024||malignant":{"def":"disposed to cause harm, suffering, or distress deliberately","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"mal-","type":"prefix","meaning":"bad"},{"part":"ign","type":"root","meaning":"born, kind"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin malignans, from malignus 'evil-natured', from male 'badly' + gignere 'to beget'.","mnemonic":"Mal-ignant: born bad, wants to harm you.","sentences":["The villain's malignant glare, full of cold spite, made the children shrink behind their mother in fear.","Critics condemned the malignant rumors, spread on purpose by rivals, that were meant to ruin her career.","He cast a malignant look at his rival and muttered a threat."]},"2024||precipitate":{"def":"cause (an event or situation, typically one that is bad or undesirable) to happen suddenly, unexpectedly, or prematurely","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before, forward"},{"part":"cipit","type":"root","meaning":"head"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin praecipitare 'throw headlong', from praeceps 'headfirst'.","mnemonic":"Pre-cip-itate: pitch headfirst into events.","sentences":["One careless remark by the ambassador could precipitate a sudden crisis between the two nations.","Economists fear that the sudden rate hike may precipitate a wave of bankruptcies among small businesses this year.","Her angry outburst precipitated the committee's collapse far sooner than anyone expected."]},"2024||rescission":{"def":"the revocation, cancellation, or repeal of a law, order, or agreement","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"sciss","type":"root","meaning":"cut"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin rescissio, from rescindere 'to cut back, annul'.","mnemonic":"Re-scissors: cut the contract back to nothing.","sentences":["The buyer demanded rescission of the contract after discovering the house had hidden damage.","After months of protest, Congress voted for the rescission of the unpopular law, canceling it entirely.","The company announced the rescission of its earlier offer, leaving the candidate stunned."]},"2024||adduce":{"def":"to bring forward in argument or as evidence; cite as pertinent or conclusive","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"duc","type":"root","meaning":"lead, bring"}],"origin":"From Latin adducere 'to lead to', from ad- + ducere 'to lead'.","mnemonic":"Ad-duce: lead the evidence to the judge.","sentences":["The lawyer rose to adduce a new witness whose testimony could clear her client.","Scholars adduce several letters as proof that the author wrote the manuscript.","In his speech, he adduced statistics from three different studies to support his claim about falling crime."]},"2024||incontrovertible":{"def":"not able to be denied or disputed","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"contra-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"vert","type":"root","meaning":"turn"},{"part":"-ible","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin in- 'not' + controversus 'disputed', from contra + versus 'turned against'.","mnemonic":"In-contra-vert: you cannot turn it against a counterargument.","sentences":["DNA analysis provided incontrovertible evidence that the suspect had been at the scene.","The scientist insisted that her findings were incontrovertible and invited anyone to test them.","Few facts are truly incontrovertible, but the sunrise is one of them."]},"2024||execrable":{"def":"extremely bad or unpleasant","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out, utterly"},{"part":"secr","type":"root","meaning":"sacred, curse"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"worthy of"}],"origin":"From Latin execrabilis, from exsecrari 'to curse', from ex- + sacer 'sacred'.","mnemonic":"Ex-crable: so bad it makes you crawl out of the room.","sentences":["The restaurant's execrable service left diners waiting two hours for cold food.","Critics called the film execrable, with a script so terrible that several of them walked out halfway through.","He wrote execrable poetry that made his friends wince at every reading."]},"2024||pugilistic":{"def":"inclined or eager to fight; aggressively hostile; belligerent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pugil","type":"root","meaning":"boxer, fist"},{"part":"-istic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin pugil 'boxer', related to pugnus 'fist'.","mnemonic":"Pug dogs snarl; pugilistic people punch.","sentences":["The senator's pugilistic style, always looking for a fight, made every committee hearing a shouting match.","He had a pugilistic temper and often looked for a fight at the bar.","The coach praised the team's pugilistic spirit after they refused to back down."]},"2024||jubilant":{"def":"feeling or expressing great happiness and triumph","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"jubil","type":"root","meaning":"shout of joy"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin jubilare 'to shout for joy'.","mnemonic":"Jubilee party: everyone is joyfully shouting.","sentences":["Jubilant fans poured into the streets after the team won the championship.","She was jubilant when the acceptance letter finally arrived in the mail.","The jubilant crowd cheered and hugged strangers as the election results were announced late that night."]},"2024||supererogatory":{"def":"beyond the requirements of duty; performed to an extent not demanded","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"super-","type":"prefix","meaning":"above, beyond"},{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"rogat","type":"root","meaning":"ask, pay"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin supererogare 'to pay out beyond what is owed'.","mnemonic":"Super-extra: more than the job asked.","sentences":["Staying until midnight to polish the slides was supererogatory; nobody had required it.","Philosophers debate whether a supererogatory act, such as donating a kidney, is ever truly obligatory.","His supererogatory efforts impressed the boss, though they were never part of his job."]},"2024||asseverate":{"def":"to declare earnestly or solemnly; affirm positively","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"sever","type":"root","meaning":"serious, strict"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin asseverare 'to act with earnestness', from severus 'serious'.","mnemonic":"As-sever-ate: swear with severe seriousness.","sentences":["The defendant continued to asseverate his innocence even after the jury returned its verdict.","She would asseverate that the story was true, swearing on her mother's name.","He asseverated that he had never seen the document before that day."]},"2024||actuate":{"def":"to put into action; start a process","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"act","type":"root","meaning":"do, drive"},{"part":"-uate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Medieval Latin actuare, from Latin actus 'a doing'.","mnemonic":"Act-uate: make something act.","sentences":["Pressing the lever will actuate the valve and start the flow of water.","What could actuate a quiet clerk to commit such a daring theft?","Fear of failure actuated her to study late every night, and by spring she had mastered the material."]},"2024||obscurantist":{"def":"a person who strives to prevent the increase and spread of knowledge","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"obscur","type":"root","meaning":"dark, hidden"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"},{"part":"-ist","type":"suffix","meaning":"person who"}],"origin":"From Latin obscurare 'to darken', via French obscurantiste.","mnemonic":"An obscurant-ist hides the light bulb under a bucket.","sentences":["The obscurantist censored the library, convinced that ordinary people should not read dangerous books.","Reformers denounced the obscurantist who wanted to close the new school so the villagers would never learn to read.","Galileo faced obscurantist opponents who refused even to look through his telescope."]},"2024||upbraid":{"def":"find fault with (someone); scold","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"up-","type":"prefix","meaning":"up"},{"part":"braid","type":"root","meaning":"twitch, reproach"}],"origin":"From Old English upbregdan 'to bring forth as a charge', from up- + bregdan 'to pull'.","mnemonic":"Up-braid: yank someone up by the hair and scold.","sentences":["The coach did not upbraid his players but quietly asked what had gone wrong.","She would upbraid her brother whenever he forgot to call their mother.","The editor upbraided the reporter in front of the whole newsroom for missing the deadline for the third time."]},"2024||pilfer":{"def":"to steal, especially in small quantities","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pilfer","type":"root","meaning":"to rob, plunder"}],"origin":"From Old French pelfre 'booty', of uncertain origin; related to 'pelf'.","mnemonic":"A pill-fur: sneaky hands steal little things.","sentences":["Employees were caught trying to pilfer pens, tape, and paper from the supply cupboard.","The magpie would pilfer shiny objects such as spoons and rings from the open kitchen window each morning.","He pilfered a few coins each day from the jar until it was nearly empty."]},"2024||euphemism":{"def":"An indirect, less offensive way of saying something that is considered unpleasant","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"eu-","type":"prefix","meaning":"well, good"},{"part":"phem","type":"root","meaning":"speech"},{"part":"-ism","type":"suffix","meaning":"practice of"}],"origin":"From Greek euphemismos, from eu- 'good' + pheme 'speech'.","mnemonic":"Eu-phem: speaking pleasantly about a harsh truth.","sentences":["'Passed away' is a common euphemism for 'died' that many people find gentler.","The company used the euphemism 'rightsizing' to describe laying off hundreds of workers.","Calling a garbage collector a 'sanitation worker' is a euphemism meant to sound more respectful."]},"2024||epigone":{"def":"a less distinguished follower or imitator of someone, especially an artist or philosopher","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"epi-","type":"prefix","meaning":"after, upon"},{"part":"gon","type":"root","meaning":"born"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Greek epigonos 'born after', from epi- + gignesthai 'to be born'.","mnemonic":"Epi-gone: came after the great one has gone, only imitating.","sentences":["The critic dismissed him as an epigone who merely copied the style of the great painter.","Every master attracts epigones who repeat the form without grasping the spirit.","She did not want to be an epigone of her famous mentor."]},"2024||propound":{"def":"to suggest for consideration; to propose","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forward"},{"part":"pound","type":"root","meaning":"put, place"}],"origin":"From Latin proponere 'to set forth', altered from 'propone'.","mnemonic":"Pro-pound: pound your idea onto the table.","sentences":["The professor will propound a new theory of language at tomorrow's conference.","Philosophers have long propounded the idea that real knowledge begins with doubt, and the debate continues today.","At the town hall meeting, she propounded a bold plan to double the city's parks within five years."]},"2024||requisition":{"def":"the act of requiring or demanding","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again, back"},{"part":"quis","type":"root","meaning":"seek, ask"},{"part":"-ition","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin requisitio, from requirere 'to seek, require'.","mnemonic":"Re-quest + position: a formal demand for supplies.","sentences":["The army issued a requisition for blankets and food from the nearby villages.","Teachers must submit a requisition before the school will order any new equipment.","The hospital's urgent requisition for more beds was approved within a day."]},"2024||ineluctable":{"def":"incapable of being evaded; inescapable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"luct","type":"root","meaning":"struggle"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin ineluctabilis, from in- + eluctari 'to struggle out of'.","mnemonic":"In-e-luctable: you can't wrestle your way out.","sentences":["Death is the ineluctable end of every living thing, however much we resist.","The old family feared the ineluctable decline of their once great fortune.","Scientists spoke of the ineluctable rise in sea levels over the coming century."]},"2024||appellation":{"def":"a name, title, or designation","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ap-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"pell","type":"root","meaning":"call, name"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or result"}],"origin":"From Latin appellatio, from appellare 'to address, name'.","mnemonic":"A-pell-ation: the label someone calls you.","sentences":["The wine carried the appellation 'Bordeaux', which guaranteed where its grapes were grown.","The king preferred the appellation 'Defender of the Faith' to any other title.","Her appellation as 'the iron lady', first used by a hostile newspaper, stuck with her for decades afterward."]},"2024||otiose":{"def":"useless; futile","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"oti","type":"root","meaning":"leisure, ease"},{"part":"-ose","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin otiosus 'at leisure, idle', from otium 'leisure'.","mnemonic":"Oh-tea-oh-se: sipping tea idly, achieving nothing.","sentences":["The committee deleted the otiose paragraph because it added nothing to the argument.","His otiose complaints changed nothing, since the decision had already been made.","Editors cut the otiose adjectives from the draft until the story read clearly."]},"2024||invective":{"def":"insulting, abusive, or highly critical language","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"into, against"},{"part":"vect","type":"root","meaning":"carry"},{"part":"-ive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Late Latin invectivus 'abusive', from invehi 'to attack with words'.","mnemonic":"In-vective: words carried in to attack you.","sentences":["The politician unleashed a stream of invective against his opponents during the debate.","Her letter to the newspaper was full of invective, calling her neighbors names, and it left the editor appalled.","The coach's invective echoed across the locker room after the humiliating loss."]},"2024||interstice":{"def":"an intervening space, especially a very small one","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"inter-","type":"prefix","meaning":"between"},{"part":"stice","type":"root","meaning":"standing"}],"origin":"From Latin interstitium, from inter- 'between' + sistere 'to stand'.","mnemonic":"Inter-stice: the tiny gap standing between two things.","sentences":["Moss grew in every interstice between the old paving stones, softening the cracks along the garden path.","Light slipped through the interstice in the curtains and fell across the bed.","A tiny interstice between the planks was enough for the mouse to squeeze through."]},"2024||disavow":{"def":"to deny responsibility or support for","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not, away"},{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"vow","type":"root","meaning":"promise, declare"}],"origin":"From Old French desavouer, from des- 'not' + avouer 'to avow'.","mnemonic":"Dis-a-vow: take back your vow.","sentences":["The party moved quickly to disavow the candidate's offensive remarks and said they did not reflect its values.","When questioned by auditors, he tried to disavow any knowledge of the missing money and blamed the accountant.","Years later, the author disavowed his early novels as immature, and refused to let publishers reprint them."]},"2024||arrogate":{"def":"to claim without justification; to claim for oneself without right","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"rog","type":"root","meaning":"ask"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin arrogare 'to claim for oneself', from ad- + rogare 'to ask'.","mnemonic":"Arrogant people arrogate: grab what they have no right to.","sentences":["The new manager tried to arrogate powers that belonged to the board.","He would arrogate credit for every success while blaming others for failures.","The king arrogated the right to tax his subjects without consulting parliament, an act that angered the nobles."]},"2024||atavistic":{"def":"relating to or characterized by reversion to something ancient or ancestral","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"atav","type":"root","meaning":"ancestor"},{"part":"-istic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin atavus 'great-great-grandfather's father', ancestor.","mnemonic":"At-a-vis-tic: a visit from your ancestors' instincts.","sentences":["The crowd's atavistic fear of the dark rose as the lights went out.","He felt an atavistic urge to protect his family that he could not explain.","Biologists described the tail-like bone as an atavistic trait inherited from distant ancestors."]},"2024||spurious":{"def":"not being what it purports to be; false or fake","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"spuri","type":"root","meaning":"illegitimate, false"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin spurius 'illegitimate, false'.","mnemonic":"A spurious claim is a spooky imposter in a costume.","sentences":["Investigators discovered that the painting's signature was spurious and the work a forgery.","The study drew a spurious link between ice cream sales and shark attacks.","She dismissed the spurious rumor as nonsense invented by her rivals, since there was not a shred of truth behind it."]},"2024||variegate":{"def":"alter in appearance, especially by adding different colors","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"vari","type":"root","meaning":"different, varied"},{"part":"-eg-","type":"root","meaning":"make"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin variegare 'to make different in appearance', from varius 'varied' + agere 'to do'.","mnemonic":"Vari-egg-ate: paint many colours on an egg.","sentences":["The gardener planted tulips and lilies to variegate the plain green lawn.","Light through stained glass will variegate the stone floor with reds and blues.","The artist variegated the plain white wall with bold streaks of orange and violet until it looked like a sunset."]},"2024||talisman":{"def":"anything whose presence exercises a remarkable or powerful influence on human feelings or actions","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"talisman","type":"root","meaning":"charm, consecrated object"}],"origin":"From Arabic tilsam, from Greek telesma 'consecration, ritual'.","mnemonic":"Tal-is-man: a man carries a charm that brings luck.","sentences":["The soldier carried a small coin as a talisman to protect him in battle.","Many athletes treat an old pair of socks as a talisman for good luck.","The family heirloom served as a talisman, and she touched it before every exam."]},"2024||inane":{"def":"lacking sense, significance, or ideas; silly","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"inane","type":"root","meaning":"empty, void"}],"origin":"From Latin inanis 'empty, void'.","mnemonic":"In-ane: insane, but just empty-headed.","sentences":["His inane comments during the meeting wasted everyone's time and added nothing.","She rolled her eyes at the inane questions the interviewer kept asking.","The sitcom's inane plot centered on a man who loses his sock."]},"2024||redoubtable":{"def":"formidable, especially as an opponent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"intensive"},{"part":"doubt","type":"root","meaning":"fear"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"worthy of"}],"origin":"From Old French redouter 'to fear greatly', from re- + douter 'to fear'.","mnemonic":"Re-doubt-able: you doubt you could beat him.","sentences":["The redoubtable champion had not lost a match in seven years, and rivals dreaded having to face him.","Everyone in the army feared the redoubtable old general, whose clever tactics had never once failed on the battlefield.","In the debate final, she faced a redoubtable opponent, a seasoned lawyer who had never lost an argument."]},"2024||vitiate":{"def":"spoil or impair the quality or efficiency of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"viti","type":"root","meaning":"fault, vice"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin vitiare 'to spoil', from vitium 'fault'.","mnemonic":"Vice-iate: vice spoils everything.","sentences":["Hidden bias could vitiate the entire study and make its results worthless.","A single dishonest juror, swayed by a bribe, may vitiate the whole trial and force the court to begin again.","Poor wiring vitiated the machine's efficiency, wasting power at every step and driving up the factory's electricity bills."]},"2024||tact":{"def":"a keen sense of what to say or do to avoid giving offense; skill in dealing with difficult or delicate situations","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"tact","type":"root","meaning":"touch"}],"origin":"From Latin tactus 'touch', from tangere 'to touch'.","mnemonic":"Tact: a delicate touch with words.","sentences":["She handled the awkward meeting with great tact, never mentioning his mistake.","A good diplomat needs tact in order to deliver bad news without causing offense or damaging a delicate relationship.","He lacked tact, and at the party he blurted out in front of everyone that the host's cake looked burnt."]},"2024||obviate":{"def":"to anticipate and prevent or eliminate (difficulties, disadvantages, etc.) by effective measures; render unnecessary","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ob-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in the way"},{"part":"vi","type":"root","meaning":"way, road"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin obviare 'to meet in the way, withstand', from ob- + via 'way'.","mnemonic":"Ob-via-te: step in the way and the problem's gone.","sentences":["A good map will obviate the need to ask strangers for directions.","The new software obviates the tedious step of typing figures by hand.","Her early warning about the cracked beam obviated a disaster that would otherwise have cost the builders millions of dollars."]},"2024||ethereal":{"def":"extremely delicate and light in a way that seems too perfect for this world","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ether","type":"root","meaning":"upper sky, air"},{"part":"-eal","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin aetherius, from Greek aither 'upper air, bright sky'.","mnemonic":"Ether floats like a wisp of air: light as heaven.","sentences":["The singer's ethereal voice seemed to float over the audience like mist.","At the garden party she wore an ethereal white gown so light and delicate that it shimmered in the moonlight.","Dawn lit the valley with an ethereal glow that felt almost unreal."]},"2024||ebullient":{"def":"cheerful and full of energy","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"bull","type":"root","meaning":"bubble, boil"},{"part":"-ient","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin ebullire 'to bubble up', from e- + bullire 'to boil'.","mnemonic":"E-bull-ient: bubbling over like a boiling pot.","sentences":["The ebullient host greeted every guest with a hug and a joke.","He was ebullient after the promotion, grinning at everyone and practically bouncing down the hall with energy.","Her ebullient personality, always cheerful and full of energy, filled even the dullest meetings with laughter."]},"2024||jockey":{"def":"struggle by every available means to gain or achieve something","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"jock","type":"root","meaning":"Jock, nickname for Jack"},{"part":"-ey","type":"suffix","meaning":"diminutive"}],"origin":"From 'Jockey', a Scots diminutive of Jock (John); later a horse rider.","mnemonic":"Jockeys elbow rivals aside on the track to win.","sentences":["Several firms began to jockey for position as the merger deadline approached.","Candidates jockey for the party's nomination long before the first vote is cast.","The two sisters jockeyed for their father's attention at every holiday dinner."]},"2024||fester":{"def":"become worse or more intense, especially through long-term neglect or indifference","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"fester","type":"root","meaning":"ulcer, sore"}],"origin":"From Old French festre 'ulcer', from Latin fistula 'pipe, ulcer'.","mnemonic":"A sore left alone will fester like old leftovers.","sentences":["If you ignore the anger, it will fester and poison your friendship.","Because nobody cleaned it properly, the untreated wound on his leg began to fester and swell painfully.","Resentments festered in the team for months, growing worse through neglect, before anyone finally spoke up about them."]},"2024||remit":{"def":"the area of authority of a person or group","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"mit","type":"root","meaning":"send"}],"origin":"From Latin remittere 'send back', from re- + mittere 'to send'.","mnemonic":"Re-mit: what was sent back to you to handle.","sentences":["Setting the company's yearly budget falls squarely within the finance director's remit, not the marketing manager's.","The inquiry's remit was limited to the events of that single week.","That question is outside our remit, because our small committee has no authority to investigate it."]},"2024||pareidolia":{"def":"the illusory perception of meaningful patterns or images of familiar things in random or amorphous data.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"para-","type":"prefix","meaning":"beside, beyond"},{"part":"eidol","type":"root","meaning":"image, shape"},{"part":"-ia","type":"suffix","meaning":"condition"}],"origin":"From Greek para 'beside' + eidolon 'image'.","mnemonic":"Para-idol: seeing an idol's face beside the clouds.","sentences":["Seeing a smiling face in a wall socket is a classic case of pareidolia.","Pareidolia explains why people claim to spot the Virgin Mary in a piece of toast.","Psychologists say pareidolia helped early humans quickly recognize faces, even in rocks and clouds."]},"2023||derelict":{"def":"in a very poor condition as a result of disuse and neglect","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"completely"},{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"lict","type":"root","meaning":"leave"}],"origin":"From Latin derelinquere 'to abandon', from de- + relinquere 'to leave behind'.","mnemonic":"De-relict: left behind like an old relic.","sentences":["The derelict factory had broken windows and a roof full of holes.","Kids avoided the derelict house at the end of the street, whose roof had collapsed years ago from neglect.","City planners wanted to turn the derelict waterfront, full of rusting sheds and broken piers, into a public park."]},"2023||condign":{"def":"well deserved; fitting; adequate","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"fully"},{"part":"dign","type":"root","meaning":"worthy"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin condignus 'fully worthy', from con- + dignus 'worthy'.","mnemonic":"Con-dign: you got what you were fully worth.","sentences":["The thief received condign punishment, a sentence that fairly matched the seriousness of his crime and satisfied the victims.","Critics agreed that the author's condign reward was a major literary prize.","She believed that a condign penalty, neither too harsh nor too light, would teach the polluting company a lesson."]},"2023||charlatan":{"def":"a person falsely claiming to have a special knowledge or skill; a fraud","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"charlatan","type":"root","meaning":"quack, babbler"}],"origin":"From Italian ciarlatano 'quack', from ciarlare 'to babble'.","mnemonic":"Char-la-tan: a smooth talker with fake tonics.","sentences":["The charlatan sold bottles of colored water as a miracle cure, and then quietly left town with the money.","Investors realized too late that their advisor was a charlatan with no real training.","Critics called the self-styled guru a charlatan who preyed on the desperate."]},"2023||veritable":{"def":"being truly or very much so","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"verit","type":"root","meaning":"truth"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"fit for"}],"origin":"From Old French, from Latin veritas 'truth'.","mnemonic":"Veri-table: a table that is a truly a table.","sentences":["After the storm the street was a veritable river, with water up to the curbs.","When we opened the door, the attic was a veritable treasure trove of forgotten toys, books, and old photographs.","She is a veritable genius at chess, beating every opponent at the club without ever seeming to try hard."]},"2023||privity":{"def":"a relation between two parties that is recognized by law, such as that of blood, lease, or service","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"priv","type":"root","meaning":"private, own"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Old French privité 'secrecy, private matter', from Latin privatus.","mnemonic":"Privity: a privy, private link between two parties.","sentences":["Only parties in privity of contract may sue to enforce its terms.","The law recognizes privity between landlord and tenant, giving each rights against the other.","A stranger to the agreement lacked privity and could not claim damages."]},"2023||rapacious":{"def":"aggressively greedy or grasping","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"rap","type":"root","meaning":"seize, snatch"},{"part":"-acious","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"}],"origin":"From Latin rapax 'grasping', from rapere 'to seize'.","mnemonic":"Rap-acious: snatches everything like a raptor.","sentences":["The rapacious landlord raised the rent again and again to squeeze his tenants.","Hungry wolves descended in a rapacious pack on the helpless herd, taking far more than they could eat.","Rapacious executives, grabbing bonuses and perks, drained the company of cash long before it finally collapsed."]},"2023||mar":{"def":"impair the appearance of; disfigure","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"mar","type":"root","meaning":"hinder, damage"}],"origin":"From Old English merran 'to hinder, damage'.","mnemonic":"Mar: a scratch on the car's paint job.","sentences":["Even a single scratch could mar the beautiful polish of the brand new piano that stood in the hall.","The organizers feared that an outbreak of violence would mar the otherwise peaceful celebration in the town square.","The ugly graffiti sprayed overnight marred the fine old stone wall that had stood unspoiled for two centuries."]},"2023||lambaste":{"def":"to criticize harshly","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"lam","type":"root","meaning":"beat"},{"part":"baste","type":"root","meaning":"beat, thrash"}],"origin":"From lam 'to beat' + baste 'to thrash', of Scandinavian origin.","mnemonic":"Lamb gets basted; a critic gets lambasted.","sentences":["Critics lambaste the film for its clumsy script and wooden acting, calling it the worst release of the year.","The coach will lambaste the team in the locker room, shouting for ten minutes, if they play carelessly again.","The following morning, newspapers lambasted the mayor harshly for ignoring the flood warnings that experts had issued."]},"2023||protract":{"def":"to prolong","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forward"},{"part":"tract","type":"root","meaning":"draw, pull"}],"origin":"From Latin protrahere 'to draw forth', from pro- + trahere 'to drag'.","mnemonic":"Pro-tract: drag it out like a tractor pulling forever.","sentences":["Lawyers tried to protract the trial in hopes the witnesses would forget.","Endless amendments and procedural objections served only to protract the debate, which dragged on for several more weeks.","The negotiations were protracted for months by petty disputes, leaving everyone at the table frustrated and exhausted."]},"2023||indemnify":{"def":"compensate for loss, damage, or injury","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"demn","type":"root","meaning":"damage, loss"},{"part":"-ify","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin indemnis 'unhurt', from in- + damnum 'loss'.","mnemonic":"In-damage-ify: make you undamaged again.","sentences":["The insurer promised to indemnify the owner for any fire damage, paying for the repairs in full.","Standard leases often indemnify the landlord against claims from injured visitors, so the tenant must cover the costs.","The shipping company agreed to indemnify customers whose parcels were lost in transit by refunding the full value of each item."]},"2023||corrugate":{"def":"contract or cause to contract into wrinkles or folds","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"cor-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"rug","type":"root","meaning":"wrinkle, fold"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin corrugare 'to wrinkle', from con- + rugare 'to wrinkle'.","mnemonic":"Cor-rug-ate: crumple like a rug.","sentences":["Intense heat can corrugate thin metal sheets, bending them into rippled ridges and folds like a washboard.","At the factory, the machine will corrugate flat paper into ridged sheets so the cardboard boxes become stronger.","Years of constant worry and little sleep corrugated his forehead into deep lines and creases."]},"2023||interminable":{"def":"endless (often used hyperbolically)","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"termin","type":"root","meaning":"end"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin interminabilis, from in- + terminare 'to end'.","mnemonic":"In-terminal: a train station you never reach.","sentences":["The interminable meeting dragged on and on, with no sign of ending, until the sun went down.","Students groaned at the interminable lecture that seemed to have no end.","After an interminable wait at the airport, which felt like days, they finally boarded the delayed flight."]},"2023||sordid":{"def":"involving ignoble actions and motives; arousing moral distaste and contempt","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sord","type":"root","meaning":"dirty, filthy"},{"part":"-id","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin sordidus 'dirty, mean', from sordere 'to be dirty'.","mnemonic":"Sordid: a dirty, shady secret.","sentences":["Reporters uncovered a sordid scandal involving bribes, blackmail, and secret deals that shocked and disgusted the public.","He lived in a sordid room with peeling wallpaper and a leaking roof.","The film dwells on the sordid details of the family's feud, including lies, betrayal, and petty greed."]},"2023||interpose":{"def":"place or insert between one thing and another","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"inter-","type":"prefix","meaning":"between"},{"part":"pos","type":"root","meaning":"place, put"}],"origin":"From Old French interposer, from Latin inter- + Old French poser 'to place'.","mnemonic":"Inter-pose: strike a pose between two people.","sentences":["She tried to interpose herself physically between the two arguing brothers before their shouting turned into a fight.","The judge refused to let the lawyer interpose objections during the closing speech.","A cloud interposed itself between the earth and the sun, dimming the afternoon."]},"2023||discountenance":{"def":"to disconcert, embarrass or abash; to show disapproval of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not, away"},{"part":"counten","type":"root","meaning":"face, bearing"},{"part":"-ance","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Old French descontenancier 'to put out of countenance', from des- + contenance 'face'.","mnemonic":"Dis-count-tenance: take away someone's face and favor.","sentences":["The sudden question seemed to discountenance the speaker, who stumbled over his notes.","The church would discountenance such behavior, and the bishop publicly criticized it from the pulpit on Sunday.","The school's teachers strongly discountenanced cheating of any kind and punished every student they caught at once."]},"2023||vapid":{"def":"offering nothing that is stimulating or challenging","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"vap","type":"root","meaning":"flat, spent"},{"part":"-id","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin vapidus 'flat, insipid', related to vappa 'flat wine'.","mnemonic":"Vapid: like stale vapor with no flavor.","sentences":["The vapid talk show offered viewers nothing but gossip and giggles, with no real ideas to think about.","His vapid speech, full of stale slogans and no real ideas, bored the audience and left no impression.","She found the party vapid, full of small talk with no real ideas."]},"2023||fulminate":{"def":"express vehement protest","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"fulmin","type":"root","meaning":"lightning"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin fulminare 'to hurl lightning', from fulmen 'lightning'.","mnemonic":"Full-mine-ate: you explode like lightning.","sentences":["Angry editors fulminate against the new law in column after column, demanding that parliament repeal it at once.","Grandpa would fulminate at the television, shouting and waving his fists, every time the senator began to speak.","The preacher fulminated from the pulpit about the corrupting influence of money."]},"2023||sinecure":{"def":"a position requiring little or no work but giving the holder status or financial benefit","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"sine-","type":"prefix","meaning":"without"},{"part":"cure","type":"root","meaning":"care, charge"}],"origin":"From Latin sine cura, 'without care', a church post with no duties.","mnemonic":"Sine cure: a job with 'no cure' needed, no worries, just the paycheck.","sentences":["The senator's nephew landed a sinecure at the port authority, collecting a handsome salary while rarely showing up.","Critics called the ceremonial title a sinecure, since the office came with a large stipend and no actual duties.","After years of exhausting work, she dreamed of a sinecure where she could be paid just to sit and read."]},"2023||anathema":{"def":"something or someone that one vehemently dislikes","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ana-","type":"prefix","meaning":"up, back"},{"part":"thema","type":"root","meaning":"thing set, placed"}],"origin":"From Greek anathema, 'thing devoted to evil'; later a curse in church Latin.","mnemonic":"Anathema sounds like 'a-NATH-ema': I'd rather eat nails, it's my enemy.","sentences":["Censorship is anathema to a free press, and editors across the country condemned the proposed law.","To the lifelong vegetarian, the idea of a steakhouse dinner was pure anathema, and she refused to go.","Corporate waste was anathema to the founder, who turned off lights and reused paper clips religiously."]},"2023||perfunctory":{"def":"carried out with a minimum of effort or reflection","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"per-","type":"prefix","meaning":"through"},{"part":"funct","type":"root","meaning":"perform, do"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin perfunctorius, 'done in a careless way', from perfungi 'to get through with'.","mnemonic":"Per-FUNCTION-ory: just going through the function, no heart in it.","sentences":["He gave the report a perfunctory glance, signed it without reading, and hurried off to lunch.","The guard's perfunctory check of our bags missed the obvious prohibited items in plain sight.","Her perfunctory thank-you note, three words long, made the host feel the gift had been unwelcome."]},"2023||motley":{"def":"incongruously varied in appearance or character; disparate","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"motley","type":"root","meaning":"of mixed colors"}],"origin":"From Middle English motley, a mixed-color cloth; further origin uncertain.","mnemonic":"A jester's motley coat: every patch a different color and clash.","sentences":["A motley crew of sailors, farmers, and runaway students signed on for the long voyage.","The antique shop displayed a motley collection of clocks, chipped teapots, and mismatched chairs.","Her motley wardrobe of plaid, polka dots, and neon somehow worked as a signature style."]},"2023||terse":{"def":"sparing in the use of words; abrupt","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"terse","type":"root","meaning":"wiped, neat, brief"}],"origin":"From Latin tersus, 'wiped clean, neat', past participle of tergere.","mnemonic":"Terse sounds like 'purse': a purse-lipped answer, tightly closed with few words.","sentences":["His terse reply, a single 'No', ended the negotiation before it started.","The manager's terse email gave the time and place and nothing more, not even a greeting.","Annoyed by the questions, the witness gave terse answers and stared at the clock."]},"2023||abrogation":{"def":"the repeal or abolition of a law, right, or agreement.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ab-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away"},{"part":"rog","type":"root","meaning":"ask, propose a law"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin abrogare, 'to repeal a law', from ab- + rogare 'to ask'.","mnemonic":"Ab-ROGATE: a law gets 'rogue' and is thrown away.","sentences":["Lawmakers debated the abrogation of the old trade treaty, which had constrained the nation for decades.","Activists feared that the abrogation of the privacy law would leave citizens exposed to surveillance.","The king's sudden abrogation of the charter shocked the nobles who had relied on its protections."]},"2023||interlocutor":{"def":"someone who participates in a conversation or dialogue","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"inter-","type":"prefix","meaning":"between"},{"part":"locut","type":"root","meaning":"speak"},{"part":"-or","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"}],"origin":"From Latin interlocutor, from interloqui 'to speak between'.","mnemonic":"Inter + locutor: someone who speaks between two sides, a talking partner.","sentences":["The diplomat was a skilled interlocutor, able to keep both sides talking through the tense negotiations.","In the interview, the journalist treated her interlocutor with respect, never interrupting the long answers.","Plato's dialogues feature Socrates and a series of interlocutors who gradually have their assumptions dismantled."]},"2023||invidious":{"def":"likely to arouse or incur resentment or anger in others","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against, upon"},{"part":"vid","type":"root","meaning":"look"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin invidiosus, 'envious', from invidia 'envy', from invidere 'to look upon with ill will'.","mnemonic":"Invidious sounds like 'envy-ous': a comparison that breeds envy and hard feelings.","sentences":["The principal faced an invidious choice, since promoting either teacher would anger the other and divide the staff.","Ranking the siblings by income was an invidious comparison that left the family dinner in tense silence.","Critics argued that the invidious distinction between workers would breed resentment across the whole company."]},"2023||virulent":{"def":"extremely poisonous; hateful","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"vir","type":"root","meaning":"poison"},{"part":"-ulent","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin virulentus, 'full of poison', from virus 'poison'.","mnemonic":"Virulent contains 'virus': a poisonous, spreading, nasty thing.","sentences":["Doctors raced to contain the virulent strain, which spread faster and killed more patients than earlier versions.","The columnist launched a virulent attack on the mayor, filled with bitter insults and open contempt.","Her virulent hatred of the rival firm poisoned every meeting where its name came up."]},"2023||rive":{"def":"split or tear apart violently","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"rive","type":"root","meaning":"to tear, split"}],"origin":"From Old Norse rifa, 'to tear apart'.","mnemonic":"Rive rhymes with 'dive': dive an axe in and the log splits.","sentences":["The lightning seemed to rive the old oak down its center, leaving two smoking halves.","Grief threatened to rive the family apart after the argument over the inheritance.","Storm waves rive the cliffs each winter, hurling great slabs of rock into the sea."]},"2023||enjoin":{"def":"instruct or urge (someone) to do something","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"cause to"},{"part":"join","type":"root","meaning":"join, bind"}],"origin":"From Old French enjoindre, from Latin injungere, 'to attach, impose'.","mnemonic":"En-JOIN: you 'join' someone to a duty and press them to do it.","sentences":["The judge moved to enjoin the company from dumping waste into the river until a full review concluded.","Her mentor would enjoin her to double-check every figure before presenting anything to the board.","The priest would enjoin the villagers to keep the fast and give alms to the poor."]},"2023||transmute":{"def":"change in form, nature, or substance","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"trans-","type":"prefix","meaning":"across, change"},{"part":"mut","type":"root","meaning":"change"}],"origin":"From Latin transmutare, 'to change from one form to another'.","mnemonic":"Trans + mute: mutate across forms, like lead into gold.","sentences":["Medieval alchemists tried in vain to transmute lead into gold using furnaces and secret recipes.","The chef could transmute humble leftovers into an elegant dish that guests praised for hours.","Over years of practice, she learned to transmute her nervous energy into a commanding stage presence."]},"2023||rapprochement":{"def":"an establishment or resumption of harmonious relations","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"ap-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"proch","type":"root","meaning":"near"},{"part":"-ment","type":"suffix","meaning":"result of"}],"origin":"From French rapprocher, 'to bring near again', from Latin prope 'near'.","mnemonic":"Rap-pro-SHMOH: the two sides approach again, and the chill thaws.","sentences":["After years of hostility, the two countries announced a rapprochement and reopened their embassies.","The sisters' rapprochement began with a short phone call after a decade of silence.","Analysts watched for any sign of rapprochement between the rival unions before the deadline for strikes."]},"2023||sententious":{"def":"given to moralizing in a pompous or affected manner","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sentent","type":"root","meaning":"opinion, maxim"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin sententiosus, 'full of meaning', from sententia 'opinion, maxim'.","mnemonic":"Sentence-ious: lecturing in long, preachy sentences.","sentences":["The old professor grew sententious at dinner, delivering moral lectures on thrift to bored guests.","His sententious advice, packed with proverbs, made the young interns roll their eyes behind his back.","The novel's narrator is sententious and pompous, lecturing readers on virtue between plot events."]},"2023||forbear":{"def":"politely or patiently restrain an impulse to do something; refrain","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"for-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, against"},{"part":"bear","type":"root","meaning":"carry, endure"}],"origin":"From Old English forberan, 'to endure, abstain', from for- + beran 'to bear'.","mnemonic":"For-BEAR: bear it, and hold back instead of snapping.","sentences":["She chose to forbear from replying to the insult, knowing a quarrel would only make matters worse.","Could you forbear from smoking in the car, please, since the baby has a cold?","The mediator asked both parties to forbear until the investigation was finished and the facts were known."]},"2023||reprove":{"def":"reprimand or censure (someone)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again, back"},{"part":"prove","type":"root","meaning":"test, judge"}],"origin":"From Old French reprover, from Latin reprobare, 'to disapprove'.","mnemonic":"Re-PROVE: prove someone wrong and scold them for it.","sentences":["The teacher had to reprove the class for talking through the fire drill instructions.","He gently reproved his friend for lying, hoping honesty would restore trust.","Her grandmother would reprove her sharply for any rude remark at the dinner table."]},"2023||repudiate":{"def":"refuse to accept or be associated with","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, back"},{"part":"pud","type":"root","meaning":"shame"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin repudiare, 'to reject, divorce', from repudium 'divorce'.","mnemonic":"Re-PUDIATE: shove it away like a bad pudding.","sentences":["The senator moved to repudiate the extremist group's endorsement, calling its views disgraceful.","She would repudiate any claim that she had ever approved the plan, though the emails said otherwise.","The company refused to repudiate its contract, even after the new owners urged it to walk away."]},"2023||extirpate":{"def":"root out and destroy completely","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"stirp","type":"root","meaning":"root, stock"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin exstirpare, 'to root out', from stirps 'stem, root'.","mnemonic":"Ex-STIR-pate: stir up the roots and pull the weed out entirely.","sentences":["Conservationists hope to extirpate the invasive snake from the island before it wipes out native birds.","The new regime vowed to extirpate corruption from every level of government.","Gardeners struggled to extirpate the stubborn bindweed, whose roots ran deep beneath the flower beds."]},"2023||truculent":{"def":"eager or quick to argue or fight","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"truc","type":"root","meaning":"fierce, savage"},{"part":"-ulent","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin truculentus, 'fierce', from trux 'fierce'.","mnemonic":"Truculent contains 'truck': a big truck of a person barreling into a fight.","sentences":["The truculent customer shouted at the clerk, demanding a refund and daring anyone to refuse.","His truculent attitude in meetings made colleagues dread every disagreement, since he turned each into a brawl.","The two truculent drivers climbed out of their cars, ready to settle the fender-bender with fists."]},"2023||languid":{"def":"displaying or having a disinclination for physical exertion or effort; slow and relaxed","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"langu","type":"root","meaning":"be faint, weary"},{"part":"-id","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin languidus, 'faint, listless', from languere 'to be faint'.","mnemonic":"Languid sounds like 'languish': lying limp in a hammock all afternoon.","sentences":["On the hot afternoon, a languid breeze barely stirred the curtains as the cat stretched out lazily.","She gave a languid wave from her chair, too relaxed to bother standing up.","The languid river drifted past the village, as slow and unhurried as the people along its banks."]},"2023||remuneration":{"def":"payment for work done","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"muner","type":"root","meaning":"gift, reward"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin remunerari, 'to reward', from munus 'gift, duty'.","mnemonic":"Re-MONEY-ration: money returned to you for your effort.","sentences":["The contract specified generous remuneration for the translators, including a bonus for finishing ahead of schedule.","Volunteers receive no remuneration, though the charity covers their travel costs and meals.","She considered the remuneration inadequate for such long hours and asked her boss for a raise."]},"2023||tawdry":{"def":"showy but cheap and of poor quality","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"tawdry","type":"root","meaning":"cheap lace, from St Audrey"}],"origin":"Shortened from 'St Audrey's lace', cheap trinkets sold at a fair in Ely, England.","mnemonic":"Tawdry sounds like 'tacky and dreary': glitter that looks cheap.","sentences":["The tawdry carnival, with its peeling paint and plastic prizes, looked sad in the harsh daylight.","She wore a tawdry necklace of gaudy glass beads that turned her neck green by evening.","Critics dismissed the film as a tawdry spectacle, all flashy costumes and no substance."]},"2023||querulous":{"def":"complaining in a petulant or whining manner","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"quer","type":"root","meaning":"complain"},{"part":"-ulous","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"}],"origin":"From Latin querulus, 'complaining', from queri 'to complain'.","mnemonic":"Querulous: always 'queryin' and whining about something.","sentences":["The querulous old man complained about the soup, the noise, the draft, and the service within five minutes.","In a querulous voice, the toddler whined that the cookie was too small and the milk too cold.","Her querulous letters to the editor, always grumbling about trivial matters, were rarely published."]},"2023||opprobrium":{"def":"the disgrace or the reproach incurred by conduct considered outrageously shameful; infamy","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ob-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"probr","type":"root","meaning":"disgrace, reproach"},{"part":"-ium","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin opprobrium, 'reproach, disgrace', from ob- + probrum 'infamy'.","mnemonic":"Op-PROBE-rium: the public probes your shame and scorns you.","sentences":["The company faced public opprobrium after the report revealed it had hidden safety defects for years.","The athlete endured worldwide opprobrium for cheating, and sponsors abandoned him within days.","The treaty's violation drew opprobrium from allies and rivals alike, leaving the country diplomatically isolated."]},"2023||recompense":{"def":"make amends to (someone) for loss or harm suffered; compensate","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again, back"},{"part":"compens","type":"root","meaning":"weigh, balance"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Old French recompenser, from Late Latin recompensare, 'to balance out'.","mnemonic":"Re-COMPENSE: compensate again to even the scales.","sentences":["The airline promised to recompense passengers for the lost luggage and the long delay.","How can I ever recompense you for saving my life, she asked the firefighter?","The court ordered the contractor to recompense the family for the damage caused by the faulty wiring."]},"2023||evanescent":{"def":"tending to become imperceptible; scarcely perceptible","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"vanesc","type":"root","meaning":"vanish"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin evanescere, 'to vanish', from e- + vanescere 'to disappear'.","mnemonic":"E-VANISH-ent: it starts to vanish as you watch.","sentences":["The evanescent glow of the sunset faded from the sky before we could take a photograph.","Fame can be evanescent: here today, forgotten by next season.","She tried to capture the evanescent scent of rain in her poem, but it kept slipping away."]},"2023||disinterested":{"def":"not influenced by considerations of personal advantage","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"interest","type":"root","meaning":"personal stake"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From dis- + interested, 'having no personal stake'.","mnemonic":"Dis-interested: no stake in the game, a fair referee.","sentences":["A disinterested mediator, with no stake in either company, helped the rivals reach a fair settlement.","Judges must be disinterested, ruling on the evidence rather than on which side might benefit them.","He offered disinterested advice, since he stood to gain nothing from whichever job she chose."]},"2023||impel":{"def":"to drive or urge forward","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward, on"},{"part":"pel","type":"root","meaning":"drive, push"}],"origin":"From Latin impellere, 'to push against, drive on'.","mnemonic":"Im-PEL: a pellet fired forward, pushed to act.","sentences":["A sense of duty seemed to impel her to stay late, even though no one had asked.","Hunger can impel a person to take jobs they would normally refuse.","What could impel a quiet man to leap into the freezing river after a stranger's dog?"]},"2023||travail":{"def":"painful or laborious effort","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"travail","type":"root","meaning":"toil, painful labor"}],"origin":"From Old French travail, 'toil', perhaps from Latin tripalium, an instrument of torture; origin partly uncertain.","mnemonic":"Travail sounds like 'travel' and trial: a long, hard, painful journey.","sentences":["After years of travail in the mines, the family finally saved enough to buy a small farm.","The novel describes the travail of immigrants crossing the ocean in cramped, disease-ridden ships.","Writing her thesis was a travail of sleepless nights, rewrites, and endless self-doubt."]},"2023||ennui":{"def":"a feeling of utter weariness and discontent resulting from satiety or lack of interest; boredom","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ennui","type":"root","meaning":"boredom, annoyance"}],"origin":"From French ennui, from Old French enui, from Latin in odio 'in hatred'.","mnemonic":"Ennui sounds like 'on we...': on and on, so bored I could sigh.","sentences":["A deep ennui settled over the resort town in winter, when the tourists left and nothing happened.","He drifted through his days in ennui, hardly caring whether the phone rang or the sun rose.","The poet wrote of the ennui of wealthy aristocrats who had everything and wanted nothing."]},"2023||facile":{"def":"appearing neat and comprehensive only by ignoring the true complexities of an issue; superficial","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"facil","type":"root","meaning":"easy to do"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin facilis, 'easy to do', from facere 'to do'.","mnemonic":"Facile contains 'facil-' like 'facilitate': too easy, an answer that glosses over problems.","sentences":["The politician offered a facile solution to poverty, ignoring the many complicated forces that cause it.","Critics dismissed the film's facile ending, in which every conflict was neatly erased by a single apology.","It is facile to blame one person for the failure when the whole system was flawed."]},"2023||tract":{"def":"an expanse, stretch, or period of time","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"tract","type":"root","meaning":"drawn out, stretched"}],"origin":"From Latin tractus, 'a drawing out, stretch', from trahere 'to draw'.","mnemonic":"A tract is a stretch pulled out across land, like a tractor drags across a field.","sentences":["The developer bought a vast tract of forest north of town and planned to build a thousand homes.","A tract of empty highway stretched ahead, with not a single car for miles.","Over a tract of several years, the village slowly recovered from the flood."]},"2023||emolument":{"def":"profit, salary, or fees from office or employment","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"mol","type":"root","meaning":"grind"},{"part":"-ument","type":"suffix","meaning":"result of"}],"origin":"From Latin emolumentum, originally 'miller's gain', from molere 'to grind'.","mnemonic":"E-MOLE-ument: the profit from milling, now the pay from office.","sentences":["The senator disclosed the emolument he received from the board, along with his regular government salary.","Critics argued that the emolument of the position was far too high for such light duties.","The old statute forbade officials from accepting any emolument from foreign governments without legislative permission."]},"2023||compunction":{"def":"a feeling of uneasiness or anxiety caused by regret for doing wrong or causing pain","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"com-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"punct","type":"root","meaning":"prick, sting"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin compunctio, 'a pricking (of conscience)', from compungere 'to prick sharply'.","mnemonic":"Com-PUNCTURE: your conscience gets punctured and stings.","sentences":["He lied to his friend without compunction, then slept soundly that night.","She felt a twinge of compunction after snapping at the waiter, and left a larger tip.","The ruthless executive fired hundreds of workers with no compunction whatsoever about the families affected."]},"2023||hyphenate":{"def":"a person who is active in more than one occupation or sphere","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"hyphen","type":"root","meaning":"joined by a mark"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who is"}],"origin":"From hyphen, from Greek huphen 'together'; the noun sense arose from titles like 'actor-director' in 20th-century American usage.","mnemonic":"A hyphenate wears two job titles linked by a hyphen, like writer-director.","sentences":["The film's star is a true hyphenate: an actor, director, and screenwriter all at once.","As a hyphenate, she balances a career as a poet and a pastry chef.","Hollywood loves a hyphenate who can write the script, direct the film, and play the lead."]},"2023||fatuous":{"def":"silly and pointless","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"fatu","type":"root","meaning":"foolish"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin fatuus, 'foolish, silly'.","mnemonic":"Fatuous sounds like 'fat head': a silly, empty-headed remark.","sentences":["Her fatuous comment about the funeral drew embarrassed silence from everyone in the room.","The talk show host asked a fatuous question that had no purpose and no answer.","Nobody laughed at his fatuous grin when he proudly announced the plan, which was pointless."]},"2023||encomium":{"def":"a speech or piece of writing that praises someone or something highly","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in"},{"part":"komos","type":"root","meaning":"celebration, revel"},{"part":"-ium","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Greek enkomion, 'praise song', from en + komos 'revel, festival'.","mnemonic":"En-CO-mium: a 'come to the party' toast full of praise.","sentences":["The president delivered a glowing encomium at the ceremony, praising the late scientist's decades of tireless discovery.","Reading the encomium aloud, the student fumbled with pride at the teacher's generous praise.","The reviewer's encomium, calling the novel a masterpiece, sent sales soaring within a week."]},"2023||blithely":{"def":"in a way that shows a casual and cheerful indifference considered to be callous or improper","pos":"adv","parts":[{"part":"blithe","type":"root","meaning":"cheerful, carefree"},{"part":"-ly","type":"suffix","meaning":"in a manner"}],"origin":"From Old English blithe, 'gentle, joyous', plus -ly.","mnemonic":"Blithely: whistling through a storm without a worry in the world.","sentences":["He blithely ignored the warning signs and walked straight onto the ice, whistling a cheerful tune.","She blithely promised to finish the project overnight, never considering how much work it required.","The tourists blithely snapped selfies beside the cliff edge while the guide pleaded with them to step back."]},"2023||prognosticate":{"def":"to predict or foretell a future event","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before"},{"part":"gnos","type":"root","meaning":"know"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Late Latin prognosticare, from Greek prognostikos 'foretelling', from pro + gignoskein 'to know'.","mnemonic":"Pro-GNOSTIC-ate: know beforehand and announce it.","sentences":["Economists often prognosticate about the coming recession, though their predictions rarely agree.","No one could prognosticate how the experiment would turn out, since nothing like it had been tried.","The old fisherman could prognosticate storms by watching the gulls and the color of the evening sky."]},"2023||redolent":{"def":"strongly reminiscent or suggestive of something","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back, again"},{"part":"ol","type":"root","meaning":"smell"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin redolere, 'to emit a scent', from re- + olere 'to smell'.","mnemonic":"Re-DOLE-nt: smells wafting back, calling up memories.","sentences":["The kitchen was redolent of garlic and fresh bread, and everyone's stomach began to rumble.","His tone, redolent of old arrogance, reminded the staff of the previous boss.","The antique shop was redolent of dust, leather, and a century of forgotten stories."]},"2023||philistine":{"def":"a person who is lacking in or hostile or smugly indifferent to cultural values, intellectual pursuits, aesthetic refinement, etc., or is contentedly commonplace in ideas and tastes","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"Philistine","type":"root","meaning":"ancient people of Canaan"}],"origin":"From the Biblical Philistines, adversaries of Israel; the sense of a person hostile to culture arose in 19th-century German student slang.","mnemonic":"A philistine yawns at the museum and asks where the burgers are.","sentences":["The critic called the mayor a philistine for cutting funding for the symphony and the public library.","Only a philistine would wander through the gallery chatting about football and ignoring the paintings.","Her father, a self-declared philistine, never opened a novel and thought poetry a waste of ink."]},"2023||demur":{"def":"raise doubts or objections or show reluctance","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, completely"},{"part":"mur","type":"root","meaning":"delay, linger"}],"origin":"From Old French demorer, 'to linger, delay', from Latin demorari.","mnemonic":"De-MUR: murmur an objection instead of agreeing.","sentences":["When asked to sign the contract on the spot, she chose to demur and asked for time to read it.","The board members rarely demur when the chairman proposes a plan, fearing his anger.","He did not demur at the price, though privately he thought it far too high."]},"2023||numismatic":{"def":"of, relating to, or consisting of coins, medals, paper money, etc.","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"numism","type":"root","meaning":"coin"},{"part":"-atic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin numisma, 'coin', from Greek nomisma 'current coin', from nomos 'custom'.","mnemonic":"Numis-matic: a 'new mis-mat' of old coins in a collector's case.","sentences":["The museum's numismatic collection includes Roman denarii, medieval silver pennies, and rare gold dollars.","He spent weekends at numismatic fairs, hunting for a mint-condition coin to complete his set.","A numismatic expert examined the coin under a lens and confirmed it was minted in 1792."]},"2023||disabuse":{"def":"to free (a person) from deception or error","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"remove, undo"},{"part":"abuse","type":"root","meaning":"misuse, deception"}],"origin":"From dis- + abuse, in the old sense 'deception', via French désabuser.","mnemonic":"Dis-ABUSE: take away the abuse of false ideas planted in your head.","sentences":["It took one visit to the factory to disabuse him of his romantic notions about manual work.","Let me disabuse you of the idea that the exam will be easy; most students fail.","The scientist tried to disabuse the public of the myth that we use only ten percent of our brains."]},"2023||officious":{"def":"assertive of authority in an annoyingly domineering way, especially with regard to petty or trivial matters","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"offic","type":"root","meaning":"duty, service"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin officiosus, 'dutiful', from officium 'duty'; the sense turned negative in English.","mnemonic":"Officious: an 'office' clerk bossing you around over a paper-clip.","sentences":["The officious security guard lectured us about the parking rules while our friends waited outside in the rain.","An officious clerk insisted on rechecking every form twice and scolded customers over tiny errors.","Nobody liked the officious neighbor who patrolled the street, noting down who left their bins out too long."]},"2023||sibilant":{"def":"characterized by a hissing sound; noting sounds like those spelled with 's'","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sibil","type":"root","meaning":"hiss"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin sibilare, 'to hiss, whistle', from sibilus 'hissing'.","mnemonic":"Sibilant: a snake saying 'sss', the hiss in the word.","sentences":["The sibilant whisper of the wind through the reeds sounded almost like someone hushing us.","Her sibilant s sounds whistled through the gap in her teeth whenever she spoke quickly.","He imitated the snake's sibilant hiss, sending the children shrieking with laughter around the campfire."]},"2023||inure":{"def":"to accustom to hardship, difficulty, pain, etc.; toughen or harden","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"into, in"},{"part":"ure","type":"root","meaning":"use, practice"}],"origin":"From Middle English enuren, 'to accustom', from en- + ure 'use, work' (Old French eure).","mnemonic":"In-URE: put you 'in use' until the hardship stops hurting.","sentences":["Years in the emergency room had inured the nurse to scenes that would horrify most people.","Soldiers are inured to hardship through relentless training in mud, cold, and hunger.","City dwellers become inured to constant traffic noise and barely notice it after a few months."]},"2023||confute":{"def":"to prove wrong","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, thoroughly"},{"part":"fut","type":"root","meaning":"beat down, check"}],"origin":"From Latin confutare, 'to check, silence, restrain'.","mnemonic":"Con-FUTE: bash an argument with facts until it collapses.","sentences":["Using data from three independent studies, the lawyer was able to confute the witness's claim.","No amount of clever talk could confute the simple evidence of the video recording.","The young scientist set out to confute the old theory with a single decisive experiment."]},"2023||impute":{"def":"represent (something, especially something undesirable) as being done, caused, or possessed by someone; attribute","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, upon"},{"part":"put","type":"root","meaning":"reckon, think"}],"origin":"From Latin imputare, 'to enter in an account, charge', from in- + putare 'to reckon'.","mnemonic":"Im-PUTE: put the blame on someone else's account.","sentences":["It is unfair to impute selfish motives to him when he donated his entire bonus to charity.","Critics would impute the failure to poor leadership, though supply shortages played a large role.","Don't impute malice to her mistake; she simply misread the schedule and arrived late."]},"2023||consonance":{"def":"agreement or compatibility between opinions or actions","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"son","type":"root","meaning":"sound"},{"part":"-ance","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin consonantia, 'harmony, agreement', from consonare 'to sound together'.","mnemonic":"Con-SONANCE: voices sounding together in agreement, like a chord.","sentences":["There was little consonance between the CEO's public promises and the company's actual hiring practices.","The choir's voices blended in perfect consonance, filling the old cathedral with harmony.","Their shared values brought a rare consonance to the otherwise divided committee, and the vote was unanimous."]},"2023||consecrate":{"def":"to make or declare sacred","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly, with"},{"part":"secr","type":"root","meaning":"sacred"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin consecrare, 'to make sacred', from con- + sacer 'holy'.","mnemonic":"Con-SECRET-ate: set a space aside as sacred and secret.","sentences":["The bishop will consecrate the new church on Sunday in front of the entire congregation.","They chose to consecrate the field as a memorial to the soldiers who died there.","She decided to consecrate her life to caring for orphans, giving up every personal ambition."]},"2023||aliquot":{"def":"a portion of a larger whole that constitutes a sample","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"aliquot","type":"root","meaning":"some, several"}],"origin":"From Latin aliquot, 'some, several', from alius 'other' + quot 'how many'.","mnemonic":"Ali-QUOT: a quote-sized slice, a small share of the whole.","sentences":["The technician drew a small aliquot of blood from the vial and placed it under the microscope.","She poured an aliquot of the solution into each test tube for the comparison.","Each lab student received a one-milliliter aliquot of the sample, taken from the same well-mixed flask."]},"2023||arrear":{"def":"money that is owed and should have been paid earlier","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ar-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"rear","type":"root","meaning":"behind"}],"origin":"From Old French arrere, 'behind', from Latin ad retro 'to the rear'.","mnemonic":"Arrear: payment stuck 'in the rear', behind where it should be.","sentences":["The tenant fell into arrear on his rent after losing his job, and the landlord sent a warning letter.","The company was months in arrear on its supplier invoices, and the suppliers stopped delivering.","Paying off the arrear on her taxes took her three years of careful saving."]},"2023||preponderate":{"def":"to be greater in number, influence, or importance","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before, above"},{"part":"ponder","type":"root","meaning":"weigh"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin praeponderare, 'to outweigh', from prae + ponderare 'to weigh'.","mnemonic":"Pre-PONDER-ate: the heavier side of the scale wins out.","sentences":["In the town's population, retirees began to preponderate, and the local school closed for lack of children.","Evidence for the first theory seemed to preponderate, though a few puzzling findings remained.","Women preponderate in the nursing field, and the hospital has launched a program to recruit more men."]},"2023||discarnate":{"def":"not having a physical body","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"without, apart"},{"part":"carn","type":"root","meaning":"flesh"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From dis- + Latin caro, carnis 'flesh'.","mnemonic":"Dis-CARN-ate: carne means flesh; a spirit with no meat on its bones.","sentences":["The medium claimed to speak with discarnate spirits who drifted through the house without any physical form.","In the novel, a discarnate voice guides the hero through the maze, though no speaker is ever visible.","Ghost stories often feature a discarnate presence that can see and hear, yet cannot touch a thing."]},"2023||disquiet":{"def":"make (someone) worried or uneasy","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not, away"},{"part":"quiet","type":"root","meaning":"calm"}],"origin":"From dis- + quiet, 'to deprive of calm', in English since the 1500s.","mnemonic":"Dis-QUIET: take away someone's quiet and calm.","sentences":["The sudden silence on the radio began to disquiet the sailors, who feared the storm was near.","News of layoffs did disquiet the staff, and rumors spread through the office all afternoon.","Nothing should disquiet you about the test; the questions will be similar to the practice ones."]},"2023||unexceptionable":{"def":"not open to objection; beyond criticism","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"cept","type":"root","meaning":"take"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to"}],"origin":"From un- + exception + -able, 'not open to taking exception'.","mnemonic":"Un-EXCEPTION-able: you can't take exception to it, it's flawless.","sentences":["Her conduct throughout the trial was unexceptionable, and even the opposing lawyers had no complaint.","The restaurant's service was unexceptionable, polite and prompt, though the food was merely average.","He had impeccable manners and an unexceptionable reputation, so the committee approved him without discussion."]},"2023||diatribe":{"def":"a bitter, sharply abusive denunciation, attack, or criticism","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"dia-","type":"prefix","meaning":"through"},{"part":"trib","type":"root","meaning":"rub, wear"}],"origin":"From Greek diatribe, 'a wearing away of time, discourse', from dia + tribein 'to rub'.","mnemonic":"Dia-TRIBE: a whole tribe's worth of angry shouting in one speech.","sentences":["The coach launched into a furious diatribe against the referees that lasted the entire bus ride home.","Her blog post, a long diatribe against social media, got thousands of angry comments.","Instead of answering the question, the senator delivered a diatribe about the opposition's failures."]},"2023||insoluble":{"def":"impossible to solve","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"solu","type":"root","meaning":"loosen, solve"},{"part":"-ble","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin insolubilis, 'that cannot be loosened', from in- + solvere 'to loosen'.","mnemonic":"In-SOLUBLE: you can't dissolve or solve it.","sentences":["The detectives faced an insoluble mystery, with no witnesses, no motive, and no physical evidence.","Oil is insoluble in water, so the two liquids separate into distinct layers.","Mathematicians once thought the problem insoluble until a young scholar found an elegant proof."]},"2022||artifice":{"def":"clever or cunning devices or expedients, especially as used to trick or deceive others.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"art","type":"root","meaning":"skill, craft"},{"part":"-i-","type":"suffix","meaning":"linking vowel"},{"part":"fic","type":"root","meaning":"make"}],"origin":"From Latin artificium, 'craft', from ars 'skill' + facere 'to make'.","mnemonic":"ARTI-fice: sneaky art, a craft used to fool you.","sentences":["The con man relied on artifice, with fake business cards and a rented office, to win his victims' trust.","Her charm was pure artifice; behind the smile she calculated how to get what she wanted.","The magician's artifice fooled the audience, who never noticed the hidden wire above the stage."]},"2022||duplicitous":{"def":"deceitful","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dupl","type":"root","meaning":"double"},{"part":"-ic-","type":"suffix","meaning":"linking element"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin duplex, 'twofold', via duplicity, 'double-dealing'.","mnemonic":"DUPLE-citous: two-faced, saying one thing and doing another.","sentences":["The duplicitous advisor smiled at the king while secretly sending letters to his enemies.","She felt betrayed on learning her business partner had been duplicitous, making deals behind her back.","The politician's duplicitous campaign promised lower taxes to voters and higher ones to donors."]},"2022||unfetter":{"def":"release from restraint or inhibition","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"undo"},{"part":"fetter","type":"root","meaning":"shackle for the feet"}],"origin":"From un- + fetter, from Old English feter, 'chain for the feet'.","mnemonic":"Un-FETTER: take the chains off the feet and run.","sentences":["The new law aims to unfetter small businesses from outdated rules that have stifled growth.","Therapy helped her unfetter her creativity after years of self-criticism had held her back.","The reformers hoped to unfetter the press, allowing journalists to report without fear of arrest."]},"2022||affront":{"def":"an action or remark that causes outrage or offense","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"af-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"front","type":"root","meaning":"forehead, face"}],"origin":"From Old French afronter, 'to strike on the forehead', from Latin ad frontem 'to the face'.","mnemonic":"A-FRONT: an insult thrown right in your face, at the front.","sentences":["Skipping the wedding without notice was a public affront to the bride's family, who felt deeply insulted.","The coach saw the player's refusal to shake hands as a personal affront.","To the proud chef, sending the dish back untouched was an affront to years of training."]},"2022||prescient":{"def":"having or showing knowledge of events before they take place","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before"},{"part":"sci","type":"root","meaning":"know"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin praescire, 'to know beforehand', from prae + scire 'to know'.","mnemonic":"Pre-SCIENT: has the science of tomorrow before it happens.","sentences":["Her prescient warning about the housing crash was dismissed at the time but proved right a year later.","The novelist's prescient vision of everyone staring at glowing screens seems uncanny today.","A prescient investor, he sold his shares just before the market collapsed, to everyone's astonishment."]},"2022||belie":{"def":"fail to give a true notion or impression of (something); disguise or contradict","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"be-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly, about"},{"part":"lie","type":"root","meaning":"deceive"}],"origin":"From Old English beleogan, 'to deceive by lying', from be- + leogan 'to lie'.","mnemonic":"Be-LIE: your face tells a lie that your actions give away.","sentences":["His calm voice could not belie the trembling of his hands as he opened the envelope.","The cheerful decor seemed to belie the grim history of the old hospital.","Her polished appearance would belie how little she had slept during the past week."]},"2022||attenuate":{"def":"reduce the force, effect, or value of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"tenu","type":"root","meaning":"thin"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin attenuare, 'to make thin', from tenuis 'thin'.","mnemonic":"At-TENUate: make it thin like a tennis string until it weakens.","sentences":["Thick walls attenuate the noise from the street, so the apartment stays peaceful even at rush hour.","Doctors use heat or chemicals to attenuate the virus before it is used in a vaccine.","Distance will attenuate the radio signal, and by the next valley reception will be very weak."]},"2022||vicissitude":{"def":"a change of circumstances or fortune, typically one that is unwelcome or unpleasant","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"vicis","type":"root","meaning":"change, turn"},{"part":"-itude","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin vicissitudo, 'change, alternation', from vicis 'turn, change'.","mnemonic":"Vicissitudes: the ups and downs, the vicious twists of life.","sentences":["Through all the vicissitudes of his career, from fame to bankruptcy, he kept a sense of humor.","The old sailor spoke of the vicissitudes of life at sea, where calm days gave way to sudden storms.","History is full of the vicissitudes of empires that rose in glory and fell into ruin."]},"2022||unheeded":{"def":"heard or noticed but disregarded","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"heed","type":"root","meaning":"pay attention"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"past participle"}],"origin":"From un- + heed, from Old English hedan, 'to take care of'.","mnemonic":"Un-HEEDED: you shouted, 'heed me!' and no one listened.","sentences":["Her warnings went unheeded, and the bridge collapsed a month later, just as she had predicted.","The unheeded advice of the old doctor could have saved the village from the epidemic.","Cries for help drifted unheeded over the noisy crowd, and no one turned around."]},"2022||recidivate":{"def":"to relapse to previous bad behavior","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back, again"},{"part":"cid","type":"root","meaning":"fall"},{"part":"-iv-","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin recidivus, 'falling back', from recidere 'to fall back'.","mnemonic":"Re-CID-ivate: fall back again into the same old rut.","sentences":["Studies show prisoners without job training are far more likely to recidivate within a year of release.","Without support, many addicts recidivate soon after leaving treatment, returning to old habits.","Programs that teach skills help ex-offenders avoid the temptation to recidivate and build stable lives."]},"2022||denouement":{"def":"the final part of a play, movie, or narrative in which the strands of the plot are drawn together and matters are explained or resolved","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"un-, away"},{"part":"nou","type":"root","meaning":"knot"},{"part":"-ment","type":"suffix","meaning":"result of"}],"origin":"From French dénouement, 'untying', from dénouer 'to untie a knot'.","mnemonic":"De-NOUE-ment: untie the plot knot and everyone exhales.","sentences":["The film's denouement tied up every loose thread, revealing the killer and reuniting the family.","Many readers found the novel's denouement disappointing, since the final chapter resolved nothing important.","After the thrilling chase, the denouement was quiet: a conversation at dawn explaining what had happened."]},"2022||unrequited":{"def":"not returned or reciprocated","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"requit","type":"root","meaning":"repay"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having been"}],"origin":"From un- plus requite, from re- 'back' plus quite (to pay, settle).","mnemonic":"Un-REQUITED: love sent out, nothing mailed back.","sentences":["He wrote poems about his unrequited love for the girl who never even noticed him.","The novel's tragedy rests on an unrequited passion that slowly consumes its young hero.","Her unrequited kindness toward the neighbors went unthanked for years, yet she never complained."]},"2022||unencumbered":{"def":"not having any burden or impediment","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"encumber","type":"root","meaning":"burden"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having been"}],"origin":"From un- plus encumber, from Old French encombrer 'to block, burden'.","mnemonic":"Un-ENCUMBERED: no heavy backpack of troubles dragging you down.","sentences":["After selling the house, she traveled light, unencumbered by debt or possessions.","The hikers, unencumbered by heavy packs, reached the summit well before noon.","He wanted a startup unencumbered by old rules, legacy software, or nervous investors."]},"2022||placid":{"def":"not easily upset or excited","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"plac","type":"root","meaning":"please, calm"},{"part":"-id","type":"suffix","meaning":"having quality"}],"origin":"From Latin placidus 'gentle, calm', from placere 'to please'.","mnemonic":"PLACID sounds like 'plastic lake': flat, smooth, undisturbed.","sentences":["The placid lake reflected the mountains without a single ripple disturbing its surface.","Despite the chaos in the kitchen, the chef stayed placid and kept giving calm orders.","Their placid cow chewed grass all afternoon, ignoring the barking dogs and honking cars."]},"2022||inexorable":{"def":"impossible to stop or prevent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"or","type":"root","meaning":"pray, plead"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin inexorabilis, from in- 'not' plus exorare 'to move by entreaty'.","mnemonic":"In-EX-ORABLE: you can't pray your way out of it; it keeps coming.","sentences":["The inexorable rise of the tide forced the campers to abandon the beach.","Scientists warned of the inexorable spread of the desert unless farming practices changed.","Facing an inexorable deadline, the team worked through the night with no hope of delay."]},"2022||nonplussed":{"def":"surprised and confused so much that they are unsure how to react","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"non-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"plus","type":"root","meaning":"more, further"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having been"}],"origin":"From Latin non plus 'no more', meaning at a loss for what to say or do.","mnemonic":"NON-PLUSSED: no more moves left, so you just stand there blinking.","sentences":["She was nonplussed when the waiter handed her a bill for a meal she never ordered.","The sudden silence left the speaker nonplussed, unsure whether the joke had offended anyone.","He looked nonplussed at the strange question, opened his mouth, and then closed it again."]},"2022||tautological":{"def":"the saying of the same thing twice in different words","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"taut","type":"root","meaning":"same"},{"part":"o","type":"root","meaning":"combining vowel"},{"part":"log","type":"root","meaning":"word, speech"},{"part":"-ical","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek tautologos, from tauto 'the same' plus logos 'word'.","mnemonic":"TAUTO-LOGICAL: the same LOGic repeated; 'free gift' says it twice.","sentences":["Saying a circle is perfectly round is tautological, since roundness is already what defines it.","The critic dismissed the argument as tautological: it simply restated its conclusion in different words.","Calling the sale 'a final, last chance' was tautological, repeating one idea three ways."]},"2022||begrudge":{"def":"to give reluctantly or resentfully; to envy one's possession or one's enjoyment of something","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"be-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"grudge","type":"root","meaning":"resent"}],"origin":"From be- plus grudge, from Old French grouchier 'to murmur, complain'.","mnemonic":"BE-GRUDGE: you hold a grudge over someone else's good luck.","sentences":["I don't begrudge her the promotion; she worked harder than anyone else in the office.","He begrudged every penny spent on repairs to a car he planned to sell.","Do not begrudge your sister her success; celebrate it as part of your family's good fortune."]},"2022||scruple":{"def":"a feeling of doubt or hesitation with regard to the morality or propriety of a course of action","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"scrupul","type":"root","meaning":"small sharp stone"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Latin scrupulus, a small sharp stone, hence a nagging worry.","mnemonic":"A SCRUPLE is a pebble in your shoe that keeps your conscience limping.","sentences":["He had no scruple about lying to customers if it meant closing the sale.","A lawyer with scruples may refuse a lucrative case that conflicts with her sense of right.","She felt a scruple before opening the letter, knowing it was addressed to someone else."]},"2022||faux":{"def":"made in imitation; artificial","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"faux","type":"root","meaning":"false"}],"origin":"From French faux 'false', from Latin falsus.","mnemonic":"FAUX rhymes with 'foe': a fake pretending to be real.","sentences":["She wore a faux fur coat that looked luxurious but cost a fraction of the real thing.","The restaurant's faux marble tables fooled nobody who actually touched the cold, plastic surface.","His faux apology, delivered with a smirk, only made everyone in the room angrier."]},"2022||acrimony":{"def":"bitterness or ill feeling","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"acri","type":"root","meaning":"sharp, bitter"},{"part":"-mony","type":"suffix","meaning":"resulting state"}],"origin":"From Latin acrimonia, from acer 'sharp'.","mnemonic":"ACRImony: ACRId bitterness, like a sour taste in a conversation.","sentences":["The divorce proceedings were marked by acrimony, with each side accusing the other of cruelty.","Years of acrimony between the two families made even a simple wedding invitation a risk.","Their meeting ended in acrimony, and neither manager spoke to the other for months."]},"2022||moribund":{"def":"at the point of death","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"mori","type":"root","meaning":"die"},{"part":"-bund","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin moribundus, from mori 'to die'.","mnemonic":"MORI-bund: MORtal, bound for the grave.","sentences":["The moribund steel town had lost its mill, its shops, and nearly half its residents.","Doctors feared the moribund patient would not survive the night despite every effort.","Once a bestseller, the moribund magazine now printed only a few thousand copies each month."]},"2022||serendipity":{"def":"the occurrence and development of events by chance in a happy or beneficial way","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"Serendip","type":"root","meaning":"old name of Sri Lanka"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"Coined 1754 by Horace Walpole from the Persian tale The Three Princes of Serendip.","mnemonic":"SERENDIPITY: Serene dip into a pond and you find a pearl.","sentences":["By pure serendipity, she met her future business partner while waiting out the rain.","Many scientific breakthroughs owe as much to serendipity as to careful planning.","It was serendipity that the lost letter surfaced just as the family needed proof."]},"2022||presage":{"def":"be a sign or warning that (something, typically something bad) will happen","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before"},{"part":"sag","type":"root","meaning":"perceive keenly"}],"origin":"From Latin praesagium 'a foreboding', from prae- 'before' plus sagire 'to perceive'.","mnemonic":"PRE-SAGE: a wise sage who senses trouble before it arrives.","sentences":["Dark clouds on the horizon seemed to presage a violent storm for the evening.","Economists said the falling factory orders could presage a long and painful recession.","The sudden silence of the birds may presage an earthquake, according to old village stories."]},"2022||surfeit":{"def":"cause (someone) to desire no more of something as a result of having consumed or done it to excess; an excessive amount","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"sur-","type":"prefix","meaning":"over"},{"part":"feit","type":"root","meaning":"make, do"}],"origin":"From Old French sorfait 'excess', from sur- 'over' plus faire 'to do'.","mnemonic":"SUR-FEIT: you did far too much, now you feel sick of it.","sentences":["A surfeit of sweets at the party left the children dizzy and cranky.","The market suffered from a surfeit of cheap phones, driving prices down for everyone.","After a surfeit of holiday meals, he longed for a plain bowl of soup."]},"2022||pugnacious":{"def":"eager or quick to argue, quarrel, or fight","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pugn","type":"root","meaning":"fist, fight"},{"part":"-acious","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"}],"origin":"From Latin pugnax, from pugnare 'to fight', related to pugnus 'fist'.","mnemonic":"PUG-nacious: a tiny pug dog barking for a fight.","sentences":["The pugnacious senator picked a fight with every reporter who questioned his record.","Even as a toddler he was pugnacious, squaring up to children twice his size.","A pugnacious little terrier charged at the huge mastiff, barking furiously and refusing to back down."]},"2022||eclectic":{"def":"deriving ideas, style, or taste from a broad and diverse range of sources","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ec-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"lect","type":"root","meaning":"choose"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek eklektikos 'selective', from eklegein 'to pick out'.","mnemonic":"ECLECTIC: electric mix of everything, hand-picked from all over.","sentences":["Her eclectic record collection ranged from opera and jazz to punk and Tuvan throat singing.","The eclectic menu borrowed dishes from Thailand, Mexico, Italy, and the American South.","He had eclectic tastes in reading, moving from physics textbooks to romance novels without embarrassment."]},"2022||precipitous":{"def":"dangerously high or steep; done suddenly and without careful consideration","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before"},{"part":"cipit","type":"root","meaning":"head"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin praeceps 'headlong', from prae- 'before' plus caput 'head'.","mnemonic":"PRECIPice-ous: a cliff edge, or falling head-first too fast.","sentences":["The hikers edged along a precipitous trail with a long drop just inches away.","Investors panicked at the precipitous fall in share prices, which lost half their value overnight.","It would be precipitous to quit your job without a plan or any savings."]},"2022||astute":{"def":"having or showing an ability to accurately assess situations or people and turn this to one's advantage","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"astut","type":"root","meaning":"crafty, shrewd"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin astutus 'crafty', from astus 'cunning'.","mnemonic":"A-STUTE: a sharp, acute mind that spots the angle.","sentences":["An astute investor, she noticed the warning signs months before the company collapsed.","The astute detective realized that the missing clock was the key to the whole case.","Teachers are often astute judges of which students need help before the students ask."]},"2022||ingratiate":{"def":"bring oneself into favor with someone by flattering or trying to please them","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"into"},{"part":"grat","type":"root","meaning":"pleasing, favor"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin in gratiam 'into favor', from gratia 'favor, grace'.","mnemonic":"IN-GRATE-iate: slip into someone's grateful good graces.","sentences":["He tried to ingratiate himself with the new boss by laughing at every joke.","Newcomers often ingratiate themselves with neighbors by bringing baked goods and offering small favors.","She would never ingratiate herself with the committee by flattering them, even to win funding."]},"2022||languish":{"def":"suffer from being forced to remain in an unpleasant place or situation","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"langu","type":"root","meaning":"be faint, weak"},{"part":"-ish","type":"suffix","meaning":"to become"}],"origin":"From Old French languir, from Latin languere 'to be faint or weak'.","mnemonic":"LANGUISH: a long-ish wait in a lonely room, wasting away.","sentences":["Prisoners languish in overcrowded cells for years while they wait for their trials.","Without funding, the promising project began to languish on a shelf in the basement.","The plants languished in the dark corner, growing pale and thin with every passing week."]},"2022||imprimatur":{"def":"a person's acceptance or guarantee that something is of a good standard","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, upon"},{"part":"prim","type":"root","meaning":"press"},{"part":"-atur","type":"suffix","meaning":"let it be"}],"origin":"From Latin imprimatur 'let it be printed', once an official license to print.","mnemonic":"IMPRIMATUR: an official stamp, as if IMPRINTed on the page.","sentences":["The new curriculum carries the imprimatur of the national education board, so schools trust it.","Without the imprimatur of a respected editor, the manuscript struggled to find a publisher.","A celebrity chef's imprimatur on the cookbook helped it sell out within a week."]},"2022||abscond":{"def":"leave hurriedly and secretly, typically to avoid detection of or arrest for an unlawful action such as theft.","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"abs-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away"},{"part":"cond","type":"root","meaning":"hide, put"}],"origin":"From Latin abscondere 'to hide away', from abs- plus condere 'to put'.","mnemonic":"AB-SCOND: scoot away with the cash and vanish.","sentences":["The cashier tried to abscond with the day's takings before the owner returned.","Police believe the accountant will abscond to another country if released on bail.","The thief managed to abscond through a back window before the alarm even sounded."]},"2022||quixotic":{"def":"exceedingly idealistic; unrealistic and impractical","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"Quixot","type":"root","meaning":"Don Quixote"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Don Quixote, the idealistic hero of Cervantes's novel who tilted at windmills.","mnemonic":"QUIXOTIC: Don Quixote charging at windmills thinking they are giants.","sentences":["His quixotic plan to end poverty by himself inspired many but accomplished very little.","Critics called the mayor's scheme to ban all cars downtown noble but quixotic.","She made a quixotic journey across the country to rescue every stray dog she could find."]},"2022||penchant":{"def":"a strong or habitual liking for something or tendency to do something","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pench","type":"root","meaning":"lean, incline"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"one that"}],"origin":"From French penchant 'leaning', from pencher 'to lean', from Latin pendere.","mnemonic":"PENCHANT: you PENCH-lean toward it again and again.","sentences":["She has a penchant for wearing bright yellow, even on the gloomiest of days.","His penchant for exaggeration meant that nobody quite believed his fishing stories anymore.","The professor's penchant for long digressions made every lecture run twenty minutes late."]},"2022||confer":{"def":"grant or bestow (a title, degree, benefit, or right)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"fer","type":"root","meaning":"bring, carry"}],"origin":"From Latin conferre 'to bring together', from con- plus ferre 'to carry'.","mnemonic":"CON-FER: bring together a gift or a meeting of minds.","sentences":["The university will confer an honorary degree on the retired nurse next spring.","Winning the election does not confer the right to ignore the courts or the constitution.","The king chose to confer knighthood on the explorer after his triumphant return."]},"2022||chagrin":{"def":"distress or embarrassment at having failed or been humiliated","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"chagrin","type":"root","meaning":"sorrow, vexation"}],"origin":"From French chagrin 'grief, vexation'; further origin uncertain.","mnemonic":"CHAGRIN: Shame crimsons your face and you grin weakly.","sentences":["To her chagrin, she realized she had been waving at a total stranger.","He read the review with chagrin, ashamed that the mistakes were so obvious.","Much to my chagrin, the mistake was printed on the front page for everyone to see."]},"2022||tendentious":{"def":"expressing or intending to promote a particular cause or point of view, especially a controversial one","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"tend","type":"root","meaning":"stretch, incline"},{"part":"-entious","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"}],"origin":"From Latin tendere 'to stretch, aim' via German tendenziös 'biased'.","mnemonic":"TENDENTIOUS: tends to push one side only, always.","sentences":["The documentary was tendentious, presenting only the facts that supported the director's political cause.","Historians criticized the textbook for its tendentious account of the war, which excluded opposing views.","He gave a tendentious speech, selecting each statistic to push the audience toward his side."]},"2022||defenestrate":{"def":"remove or dismiss (someone) from a position of power or authority","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out of"},{"part":"fenestr","type":"root","meaning":"window"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin de- plus fenestra 'window'; originally to throw out a window.","mnemonic":"DE-FENESTRATE: toss them out the window, like a fenster.","sentences":["After the scandal, the board moved to defenestrate the chairman and appoint a replacement.","Party rebels hoped to defenestrate the prime minister before the next general election.","The coach was defenestrated after a dismal season, replaced within days by his assistant."]},"2022||allay":{"def":"diminish or put at rest (fear, suspicion, or worry).","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"al-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"lay","type":"root","meaning":"put down, lay"}],"origin":"From Old English alecgan 'to lay down, put aside'.","mnemonic":"ALLAY: lay your fears down on the shelf.","sentences":["The doctor's calm explanation helped allay the family's fears about the surgery.","The company issued a statement to allay concerns that customer data had been stolen.","A cup of warm tea can allay anxiety before an important exam."]},"2022||distill":{"def":"extract the essential meaning or most important aspects of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down"},{"part":"still","type":"root","meaning":"drip"}],"origin":"From Latin destillare 'to drip down', from de- plus stilla 'a drop'.","mnemonic":"DISTILL: boil a long story until a few pure drops remain.","sentences":["The editor asked her to distill the five-hundred-page report into a single page of findings.","Brewers distill the fermented mash to produce a stronger spirit with more alcohol.","Great teachers distill complex ideas into simple lessons that students can remember for years."]},"2022||avaricious":{"def":"having or showing an extreme greed for wealth or material gain","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"avar","type":"root","meaning":"greedy"},{"part":"-icious","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"}],"origin":"From Latin avaritia 'greed', from avarus 'greedy', from avere 'to crave'.","mnemonic":"AVARICIOUS: Ava is vicious about hoarding gold.","sentences":["The avaricious landlord raised the rent every month and never fixed a single leak.","In the fable, an avaricious king wished everything he touched would turn to gold.","Critics described the avaricious executives as men who would sell their own parents for profit."]},"2022||impudent":{"def":"not showing due respect for another person; impertinent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"pud","type":"root","meaning":"feel shame"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin impudens 'shameless', from in- 'not' plus pudere 'to be ashamed'.","mnemonic":"IM-PUDENT: not PUDent, no shame, cheeky to your face.","sentences":["The impudent boy stuck out his tongue at the principal and strolled away.","She gave an impudent reply to the judge, earning a stern warning about her manners.","Servants were punished if they were impudent toward the noble family they served."]},"2022||obsequious":{"def":"obedient or attentive to an excessive or servile degree","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ob-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"sequ","type":"root","meaning":"follow"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin obsequiosus, from obsequi 'to comply', from ob- plus sequi 'to follow'.","mnemonic":"OB-SEQUIOUS: follows your sequence of orders like a puppy.","sentences":["The obsequious waiter bowed so often that the diners felt uncomfortable rather than pampered.","He was obsequious around the director, laughing at every joke and fetching coffee unasked.","Nobody trusted the obsequious assistant, whose constant flattery seemed too eager to be sincere."]},"2022||taciturn":{"def":"reserved or uncommunicative in speech; saying little","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"tacit","type":"root","meaning":"silent"},{"part":"-urn","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"}],"origin":"From Latin taciturnus 'silent', from tacitus, from tacere 'to be silent'.","mnemonic":"TACIT-urn: silent as a sealed urn.","sentences":["Her taciturn grandfather answered most questions with a single nod or a grunt.","The taciturn farmer rarely spoke at the market, preferring to let the produce do the talking.","A taciturn detective, he listened for hours and said almost nothing until the very end."]},"2022||bifurcation":{"def":"the division of something into two branches or parts","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"bi-","type":"prefix","meaning":"two"},{"part":"furc","type":"root","meaning":"fork"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin bifurcus 'two-pronged', from bi- 'two' plus furca 'fork'.","mnemonic":"BI-FURCATION: the road forks in two, like a furcated fork.","sentences":["The bifurcation of the river created two channels that flowed around a small island.","Political analysts noted the bifurcation of the party into moderate and radical wings.","A clear bifurcation in the road forced the travelers to choose between left and right."]},"2022||plutocrat":{"def":"a person whose power derives from their wealth","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pluto","type":"root","meaning":"wealth"},{"part":"-crat","type":"suffix","meaning":"ruler"}],"origin":"From Greek ploutos 'wealth' plus -kratia 'rule'.","mnemonic":"PLUTO-crat: rich like Pluto, god of wealth, and rules by it.","sentences":["The plutocrat bought three newspapers and used them to shape the national debate.","Reformers accused the plutocrat of using his fortune to buy laws that favored his factories.","In the novel, a ruthless plutocrat controls the city's banks, its mayor, and its police."]},"2022||paragon":{"def":"a person or thing regarded as a perfect example of a particular quality","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"para-","type":"prefix","meaning":"beside"},{"part":"gon","type":"root","meaning":"whetstone"}],"origin":"From Old Italian paragone 'touchstone', from Greek parakonan 'to sharpen against'; a model of excellence.","mnemonic":"PARAGON: a PAIR of GONe-perfect, the model all others copy.","sentences":["She was a paragon of patience, never raising her voice even with the most difficult students.","The coach held the veteran player up as a paragon of discipline and fair play.","No one is a paragon of virtue; even the kindest person has bad days."]},"2022||beguile":{"def":"charm or enchant (someone), sometimes in a deceptive way","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"be-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"guile","type":"root","meaning":"trickery"}],"origin":"From be- plus guile, from Old French guile 'deceit'.","mnemonic":"BE-GUILE: wrap them in guile and a charming smile.","sentences":["The singer's golden voice could beguile an entire stadium of restless fans into complete silence.","The con man tried to beguile the old woman with promises of easy riches.","Stories of faraway lands continued to beguile the young sailor long after he returned home."]},"2022||revile":{"def":"criticize in an abusive or angrily insulting manner","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"vile","type":"root","meaning":"cheap, base"}],"origin":"From Old French reviler 'to treat as vile', from re- plus vil 'vile'.","mnemonic":"RE-VILE: call someone vile again and again.","sentences":["The dictator was reviled by his people long after he was removed from power.","Critics continue to revile the film as a shallow, offensive waste of money.","Do not revile your opponents; answering insults with insults only makes the debate worse."]},"2022||cunning":{"def":"having or showing skill in achieving one's ends by deceit or evasion","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"cunn","type":"root","meaning":"know"},{"part":"-ing","type":"suffix","meaning":"having quality"}],"origin":"From Old English cunnan 'to know', from Old Norse kunning 'knowledge'.","mnemonic":"CUNNING: a fox knows a sly way in.","sentences":["The cunning fox tricked the farmer into leaving the henhouse door wide open.","She devised a cunning plan to win the game while appearing to lose.","His cunning excuse sounded perfectly reasonable, though the facts behind it were quite different."]},"2022||insidious":{"def":"proceeding in a gradual, subtle way, but with harmful effects; stealthily treacherous or deceitful","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"insidi","type":"root","meaning":"lie in wait"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin insidiosus 'deceitful', from insidiae 'ambush', from insidere 'to sit upon'.","mnemonic":"IN-SIDE-ious: the danger sits inside you before you notice.","sentences":["The insidious disease spread slowly through the body, showing no symptoms until too late.","Propaganda can be insidious, shaping a population's beliefs without anyone noticing the change.","An insidious rumor crept through the office and ruined her reputation before she knew it."]},"2022||adamant":{"def":"refusing to be persuaded or to change one's mind","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"dam","type":"root","meaning":"tame, conquer"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Greek adamas 'unconquerable', the name for the hardest metal or diamond.","mnemonic":"ADAMANT: Adam ain't moving, hard as a diamond.","sentences":["She remained adamant that she would not sell the family farm at any price.","The senator was adamant in refusing to compromise, despite pressure from his own party.","He was adamant about leaving early, ignoring every plea to stay for dessert."]},"2022||concatenation":{"def":"a series of interconnected things or events","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"catena","type":"root","meaning":"chain"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin concatenare 'to link together', from catena 'chain'.","mnemonic":"CON-CAT-ENATION: a chain of CATs linked tail to tail.","sentences":["The disaster was a concatenation of small errors, each one leading to the next.","A concatenation of lucky events, from missed trains to chance meetings, brought them together.","In programming, the concatenation of two strings joins them end to end into one."]},"2022||retrench":{"def":"reduce costs or spending in response to economic difficulty","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"trench","type":"root","meaning":"cut, ditch"}],"origin":"From French retrancher 'to cut back', from re- plus trancher 'to cut'.","mnemonic":"RE-TRENCH: dig back into a trench and spend less.","sentences":["Facing falling sales, the firm had to retrench and freeze all new hiring.","The university will retrench next year, cutting departments and closing two older libraries.","Governments often retrench during a recession, reducing public services to balance the budget."]},"2022||hermetic":{"def":"impervious to external influence; airtight","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"Hermes","type":"root","meaning":"Hermes Trismegistus"},{"part":"-etic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Hermes Trismegistus, legendary alchemist said to have invented an airtight seal.","mnemonic":"HERMETIC: a HERMIT sealed airtight in his cave.","sentences":["The hermetic container kept the sample free from air, moisture, and contamination for decades.","Living in a hermetic community, the villagers rarely met outsiders or heard outside ideas.","The lab's hermetic seal meant that no dust or outside influence could affect the experiment."]},"2022||despotism":{"def":"the exercise of absolute power, especially in a cruel and oppressive way.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"despot","type":"root","meaning":"master, lord"},{"part":"-ism","type":"suffix","meaning":"system of"}],"origin":"From Greek despotes 'master, lord' plus -ism.","mnemonic":"DESPOT-ism: a despot's one-man rule, all power, no rules.","sentences":["Under the despotism of the old king, citizens were jailed for criticizing the government.","Revolutionaries rose against the despotism that had silenced the press and abolished the courts.","History shows that despotism often begins with promises of stability and ends in fear."]},"2022||aloof":{"def":"not friendly or forthcoming; cool and distant","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"on"},{"part":"loof","type":"root","meaning":"windward, luff"}],"origin":"From a- plus loof, a Dutch nautical word 'to windward', meaning to keep clear.","mnemonic":"A-LOOF: a loof (roof) away, standing apart from the group.","sentences":["The new manager seemed aloof, eating lunch alone and rarely chatting with the staff.","She remained aloof at the party, watching the crowd from a quiet corner.","His aloof manner made strangers think he was unfriendly, though he was only shy."]},"2022||perfidious":{"def":"deceitful and untrustworthy","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"per-","type":"prefix","meaning":"through, against"},{"part":"fid","type":"root","meaning":"faith"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin perfidiosus, from perfidia 'treachery', from per fidem 'against faith'.","mnemonic":"PER-FID-ious: breaks FIDelity, a faithless betrayer.","sentences":["The perfidious ally secretly sold the army's battle plans to the enemy.","She felt hurt by her perfidious friend, who had spread her secrets across the school.","History remembers the perfidious general as a man who betrayed his king for money."]},"2022||impugn":{"def":"dispute the truth, validity, or honesty of (a statement or motive); call into question.","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"pugn","type":"root","meaning":"fight"}],"origin":"From Latin impugnare 'to attack', from in- 'against' plus pugnare 'to fight'.","mnemonic":"IM-PUGN: fight against someone's words with your fists of doubt.","sentences":["The defense lawyer tried to impugn the witness's honesty by revealing her past lies.","Do not impugn my motives when I have only ever tried to help the team.","Critics began to impugn the study's methods, claiming the data had been selectively chosen."]},"2022||placate":{"def":"make (someone) less angry or hostile","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"plac","type":"root","meaning":"please, calm"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin placare 'to calm', related to placere 'to please'.","mnemonic":"PLACATE: place a pacifier in an angry baby's mouth.","sentences":["The manager offered a full refund to placate the furious customer at the front desk.","Nothing the mayor said could placate the crowd, which kept shouting outside city hall.","She baked cookies to placate her neighbor after the loud party kept him awake."]},"2022||prosaic":{"def":"commonplace or dull; matter-of-fact or unimaginative","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pros","type":"root","meaning":"straightforward prose"},{"part":"-aic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin prosaicus, from prosa 'straightforward discourse, prose'.","mnemonic":"PROSAIC: plain PROSE with no poetry in it.","sentences":["His prosaic description of the sunset sounded more like a weather report than poetry.","After the excitement of travel, she returned to her prosaic routine of emails and meetings.","The prosaic office had gray walls, gray carpets, and no sign of creativity anywhere."]},"2022||disparage":{"def":"regard or represent as being of little worth","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart, away"},{"part":"par","type":"root","meaning":"equal, rank"},{"part":"-age","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Old French desparagier 'to marry someone of lower rank', from des- plus parage 'rank'.","mnemonic":"DIS-PARAGE: treat as unequal, put someone below your own rank.","sentences":["Rival politicians often disparage each other's records instead of discussing real issues.","It is unkind to disparage a friend's cooking when they worked so hard on dinner.","He would never disparage his competitors in public, preferring to let his products speak for themselves."]},"2022||progeny":{"def":"children or descendants; offspring","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth"},{"part":"gen","type":"root","meaning":"beget, produce"},{"part":"-y","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Latin progenies 'descendants', from progignere 'to beget'.","mnemonic":"PRO-GENY: the genes you project forward, your kids.","sentences":["The queen's progeny filled the palace, with grandchildren running through every hallway.","Salmon die after laying eggs, but their progeny swim back to the sea the next spring.","The farmer was proud that the progeny of his prize bull won ribbons at the fair."]},"2022||resplendent":{"def":"attractive and impressive through being richly colorful or sumptuous","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again, back"},{"part":"splend","type":"root","meaning":"shine"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin resplendere 'to shine brightly', from re- plus splendere 'to shine'.","mnemonic":"RE-SPLENDENT: splendid, shining again and again.","sentences":["The bride looked resplendent in a gown embroidered with gold thread and tiny pearls.","The resplendent peacock spread its tail, showing off shimmering blues and greens to the crowd.","A resplendent sunrise lit the valley, turning every window and rooftop to bright gold."]},"2022||vehement":{"def":"showing strong feeling; forceful, passionate, or intense","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"veh","type":"root","meaning":"carry, drive"},{"part":"-ement","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"}],"origin":"From Latin vehemens 'impetuous, violent', perhaps from vehere 'to carry' or vemens 'mindless'; origin uncertain.","mnemonic":"VEHEMENT: a VEHicle at full speed of emotion.","sentences":["The mayor issued a vehement denial, banging the table and demanding an immediate apology.","Her vehement protest against the new law drew thousands of supporters to the square.","He gave a vehement defense of his friend, refusing to hear any criticism at all."]},"2022||pariah":{"def":"an outcast","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pariah","type":"root","meaning":"drummer, outcast"}],"origin":"From Tamil paraiyar 'drummer', a name for a low caste, later used for any outcast.","mnemonic":"PARIAH: PARTY? Not for you; everyone avoids you.","sentences":["After the scandal, the former star was treated like a pariah in his own hometown.","Refusing to join the strike made her a pariah among her coworkers for months.","The small nation became a pariah state, shunned by trading partners and excluded from summits."]},"2022||insolence":{"def":"rude and disrespectful behavior","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"sol","type":"root","meaning":"accustomed"},{"part":"-ence","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin insolentia 'unusualness, arrogance', from in- plus solere 'to be accustomed'.","mnemonic":"IN-SOLENCE: no soul for manners, just rude sass.","sentences":["The teacher sent the student to the office for his insolence in front of the class.","She was shocked by the insolence of the clerk, who rolled his eyes and walked away.","The general would not tolerate insolence from junior officers, however brilliant they might be."]},"2022||decrepit":{"def":"elderly and infirm","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down, away"},{"part":"crepit","type":"root","meaning":"creak, rattle"}],"origin":"From Latin decrepitus 'worn out', from de- plus crepare 'to creak'.","mnemonic":"DE-CREPIT: creaky, crumbling, and falling apart.","sentences":["The decrepit old barn leaned so badly that it seemed ready to collapse in the next storm.","Their decrepit dog could barely climb stairs, but still wagged his tail at every visitor.","A decrepit bridge, rusted and sagging, was closed to traffic until it could be replaced."]},"2022||equable":{"def":"not easily disturbed or angered; calm and even-tempered","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"equ","type":"root","meaning":"equal, level"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin aequabilis 'that can be made equal', from aequare 'to make level'.","mnemonic":"EQU-able: EQUAL moods, level as a calm EQUATOR.","sentences":["Her equable temperament made her the perfect person to lead during a crisis.","The island enjoys an equable climate, with mild summers and winters that rarely turn harsh.","Even after hours of angry shouting, the equable judge never lost his calm."]},"2022||co-opt":{"def":"divert to or use in a role different from the original or usual one","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"co-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"opt","type":"root","meaning":"choose"}],"origin":"From Latin cooptare 'to choose together', from co- plus optare 'to choose'.","mnemonic":"CO-OPT: choose to take someone else's idea, as if teamed up.","sentences":["Big companies often co-opt protest slogans to sell shoes and soft drinks.","The ruling party tried to co-opt the movement's leaders by offering them government jobs.","She worried that the committee would co-opt her research and use it for its own agenda."]},"2022||surreptitiously":{"def":"in a way that attempts to avoid notice or attention; secretly","pos":"adv","parts":[{"part":"sur-","type":"prefix","meaning":"under"},{"part":"rept","type":"root","meaning":"seize, creep"},{"part":"-itious","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"},{"part":"-ly","type":"suffix","meaning":"in a manner"}],"origin":"From Latin surreptus 'taken secretly', from sub- 'under' plus rapere 'to seize'.","mnemonic":"SURREPTITIOUSLY: sneaking like a reptile, unseen.","sentences":["She surreptitiously slipped the note into his pocket while the teacher wasn't looking.","The guard surreptitiously checked his phone every few minutes during the long, quiet night shift.","Surreptitiously, the children fed vegetables to the dog under the dinner table."]},"2022||inimitable":{"def":"so good or unusual as to be impossible to copy; unique","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"imit","type":"root","meaning":"copy"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin inimitabilis, from in- 'not' plus imitari 'to copy'.","mnemonic":"IN-IMIT-able: can't be imitated, one of a kind.","sentences":["The comedian's inimitable style, full of odd pauses and sudden shouts, made her instantly recognizable.","Nobody could copy the chef's inimitable sauce, though dozens of rivals tried for years.","Her inimitable handwriting, looping and slanted, made every letter a small piece of art."]},"2022||replete":{"def":"filled or well-supplied with something","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again, fully"},{"part":"plet","type":"root","meaning":"fill"}],"origin":"From Latin repletus 'filled up', from replere 'to fill', from re- plus plere.","mnemonic":"RE-PLETE: completely refilled, like a plate piled full.","sentences":["The old library was replete with rare maps, first editions, and handwritten letters.","The speech was replete with jokes, so the audience laughed from start to finish.","His diary, replete with names and dates, became crucial evidence at the trial."]},"2022||temerity":{"def":"excessive confidence or boldness; audacity","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"temer","type":"root","meaning":"rashness, blind"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin temeritas 'rashness', from temere 'blindly, at random'.","mnemonic":"TEMERITY: a TEMpER-ity to boldly challenge the boss.","sentences":["He had the temerity to ask the king for a raise in front of the whole court.","Few had the temerity to question the director's decision, given his famously hot temper.","She showed great temerity by walking into the lion's enclosure just to get a closer photo."]},"2022||denizen":{"def":"an inhabitant, resident; one who frequents a place","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"deinz","type":"root","meaning":"from within"},{"part":"-en","type":"suffix","meaning":"dweller"}],"origin":"From Anglo-French deinzein 'one within', from deinz 'within', from Latin de intus.","mnemonic":"DEN-IZEN: a creature who lives in the den.","sentences":["The cave's denizen, a lone bat, flapped out into the dusk as visitors approached.","Every denizen of the small town knew the bakery owner by name and favorite pastry.","Late at night, the diner fills with its usual denizen crowd of truckers, students, and insomniacs."]},"2022||sacrosanct":{"def":"regarded as too important or valuable to be interfered with","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sacro","type":"root","meaning":"sacred"},{"part":"sanct","type":"root","meaning":"holy, made inviolable"}],"origin":"From Latin sacrosanctus 'made holy by a sacred rite', from sacer plus sanctus.","mnemonic":"SACRO-SANCT: sacred AND holy, so don't touch it.","sentences":["In their family, Sunday dinner was sacrosanct, and no one was allowed to miss it.","The journalist argued that the privacy of sources is sacrosanct and cannot be violated by the courts.","For the ancient Romans, the person of a tribune was sacrosanct and could not be harmed."]},"2022||attest":{"def":"provide or serve as clear evidence of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"at-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"test","type":"root","meaning":"witness"}],"origin":"From Latin attestari, from ad- 'to' plus testari 'to bear witness'.","mnemonic":"AT-TEST: you TEST the facts and stand witness to them.","sentences":["The signed documents attest that the painting was created in the seventeenth century.","Several neighbors can attest to his good character, having known him for decades.","The ruins attest to a once-powerful empire that ruled the region for centuries."]},"2022||recalcitrant":{"def":"having an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"calc","type":"root","meaning":"heel, kick"},{"part":"-itrant","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"}],"origin":"From Latin recalcitrare 'to kick back', from re- plus calx 'heel'.","mnemonic":"RE-CALCI-TRANT: a mule that kicks back with its heel.","sentences":["The recalcitrant student refused to follow instructions, no matter how often the teacher asked.","A recalcitrant mule stood in the road and would not move for any amount of pulling.","The committee struggled to get anything done with a recalcitrant chairman blocking every proposal."]},"2022||tacit":{"def":"understood or implied without being stated","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"tac","type":"root","meaning":"silent"},{"part":"-it","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective ending"}],"origin":"From Latin tacitus 'silent', from tacere 'to be silent'.","mnemonic":"TACIT: sounds like 'tacky quiet'; the unspoken message.","sentences":["The two nations reached a tacit agreement to avoid fighting, though nothing was ever signed.","Her silence was taken as tacit approval, so the plan went ahead without a vote.","Employees understood the tacit rule that no one leaves before the boss does."]},"2022||ergogenic":{"def":"intended to enhance physical performance, stamina, or recovery","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"erg","type":"root","meaning":"work"},{"part":"o","type":"root","meaning":"combining vowel"},{"part":"gen","type":"root","meaning":"produce"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek ergon 'work' plus -genes 'producing'.","mnemonic":"ERGO-GENIC: generates more ERGs of work, boosting your muscles.","sentences":["Many athletes use caffeine as an ergogenic aid to improve endurance during long races.","The study tested whether creatine works as an ergogenic supplement for sprinters and weight lifters.","Coaches debated whether the new energy gel was truly ergogenic or merely tasted nice."]},"2022||excoriate":{"def":"censure or criticize severely","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"off, out"},{"part":"cori","type":"root","meaning":"skin, hide"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin excoriare, 'to strip the skin off', from corium 'hide'.","mnemonic":"Imagine a critic peeling the skin off your essay with every harsh word.","sentences":["The senator chose to excoriate her opponent in a televised speech, accusing him of lying about the budget.","Reviewers did not hesitate to excoriate the film, calling it lazy, bloated, and insulting to its audience.","My coach will excoriate us if we skip practice again, so nobody dares to be late."]},"2022||dearth":{"def":"a lack, scarcity","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"dearth","type":"root","meaning":"scarcity, costliness"}],"origin":"From Middle English derthe, 'costliness', from dere 'dear, costly'.","mnemonic":"A dearth is when dear (costly) things are hard to find: shelves are bare.","sentences":["A dearth of affordable housing has pushed many young families out of the city center entirely.","Despite the dearth of rain this summer, the farmers managed to save a small portion of their crops.","There was a dearth of volunteers at the cleanup, so only five of us showed up to do the work."]},"2022||cataclysm":{"def":"a sudden, violent, or devastating upheaval","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"cata-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down"},{"part":"clysm","type":"root","meaning":"wash, flood"}],"origin":"From Greek kataklysmos, 'flood', from kata 'down' + klyzein 'to wash'.","mnemonic":"A cat clasps 'em in a flood: a cataclysm sweeps everything away.","sentences":["The earthquake was a cataclysm that leveled whole neighborhoods and left thousands of people homeless within minutes.","Historians debate whether the eruption was the cataclysm that ended the ancient civilization on the island.","For the small shop owner, the sudden flood was a cataclysm that wiped out years of careful work."]},"2022||unbridled":{"def":"uncontrolled; unconstrained","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"bridle","type":"root","meaning":"restraining harness"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having been"}],"origin":"From un- + bridled, from bridle, the horse's head harness used for control.","mnemonic":"A horse with no bridle gallops wherever it wants, wild and free.","sentences":["The crowd erupted with unbridled joy when the home team scored in the final seconds of the match.","His unbridled spending on luxury cars eventually forced the company into serious financial trouble.","She spoke about the project with unbridled enthusiasm, hardly pausing to breathe between ideas."]},"2022||errant":{"def":"straying from the right course or from accepted standards","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"err","type":"root","meaning":"wander, stray"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"doing, being"}],"origin":"From Old French errant, 'wandering', from Latin errare 'to wander'; also influenced by iterare.","mnemonic":"Errant sounds like 'error': someone who wanders off the right path.","sentences":["An errant pass sailed over the receiver's head and landed in the crowd behind the end zone.","The headmaster called in the errant student and asked why he had skipped class for a whole week.","In the old tale, an errant knight wandered the countryside seeking adventures and righting wrongs."]},"2022||aplomb":{"def":"self-confidence or assurance, especially when in a demanding situation","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to, according to"},{"part":"plomb","type":"root","meaning":"lead weight, plumb line"}],"origin":"From French aplomb, 'perpendicularity, balance', from a plomb 'according to the plumb line'.","mnemonic":"A plumb line hangs perfectly straight; a person with aplomb stands just as steady.","sentences":["She handled the hostile questions from reporters with remarkable aplomb, never losing her smile or composure.","The young pianist walked on stage with aplomb, as if the huge audience were just friends.","He dealt with the flat tire and the missed flight with aplomb, calmly rebooking everything from his phone."]},"2022||idyllic":{"def":"extremely happy, peaceful, or picturesque","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"idyll","type":"root","meaning":"short pastoral poem"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From idyll, from Greek eidyllion, 'little picture', a short poem about rural life.","mnemonic":"An idyll is a picture-perfect poem; idyllic scenes look like postcards.","sentences":["They spent an idyllic week in a lakeside cabin, swimming at dawn and watching the stars at night.","The village, with its stone cottages and quiet meadows, looked almost too idyllic to be real.","Her childhood summers on the farm were idyllic, full of warm sunshine, fresh peaches, and endless play."]},"2022||laden":{"def":"heavily loaded or weighed down","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"lad","type":"root","meaning":"to load"},{"part":"-en","type":"suffix","meaning":"past participle"}],"origin":"From Old English hladan, 'to load', past participle of lade.","mnemonic":"A ladder-load: a donkey laden with so much cargo it can barely walk.","sentences":["The mules climbed slowly up the mountain path, laden with sacks of grain and heavy tools.","The air was laden with the smell of rain just before the storm finally broke over the town.","She returned from the market laden with bags of fruit, bread, and flowers for the weekend dinner."]},"2022||pontificate":{"def":"express one's opinions in a way considered annoyingly pompous and dogmatic","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pontifex","type":"root","meaning":"Roman high priest"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin pontificari, from pontifex 'high priest' (literally 'bridge-builder'); to speak like a pope.","mnemonic":"Imagine a pontiff lecturing from his balcony: he pontificates, you yawn.","sentences":["My uncle loves to pontificate about politics at dinner, never letting anyone else get a word in.","Critics accused the columnist of arrogance, since he would pontificate on every topic imaginable without ever listening.","Rather than pontificate about leadership, the manager simply rolled up her sleeves and helped the team finish."]},"2022||trite":{"def":"overused and consequently of little import; lacking originality or freshness","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"trit","type":"root","meaning":"rubbed, worn"}],"origin":"From Latin tritus, 'worn, rubbed away', past participle of terere 'to rub'.","mnemonic":"Trite phrases are worn thin, like a coin rubbed smooth by too many hands.","sentences":["Saying 'everything happens for a reason' felt trite to her after the loss of her home.","The movie's dialogue was so trite that the audience could finish each line before the actors spoke.","He worried his wedding toast sounded trite, packed with the same old jokes everyone had heard before."]},"2022||vitriol":{"def":"cruel and bitter criticism","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"vitriol","type":"root","meaning":"glassy sulfate, sulfuric acid"}],"origin":"From Medieval Latin vitriolum, 'sulfate', from Latin vitreus 'glassy'; acid became a metaphor for harsh speech.","mnemonic":"Vitriol was once sulfuric acid; harsh words burn like it.","sentences":["The online comments were filled with vitriol, with strangers attacking the author's looks instead of her ideas.","The debate began politely but soon dissolved into vitriol, as both candidates hurled personal insults at each other.","She was shocked by the vitriol in his email, which went on for pages without a single kind word."]},"2022||mollify":{"def":"appease the anger or anxiety of (someone)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"moll","type":"root","meaning":"soft"},{"part":"-ify","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin mollificare, from mollis 'soft' + facere 'to make'.","mnemonic":"Mollify means to make mellow: turn an angry person soft like a mole's fur.","sentences":["The airline offered free vouchers to mollify passengers whose flights had been canceled for the third time.","Nothing I said could mollify my sister after I forgot her birthday dinner entirely.","The mayor tried to mollify angry residents by promising to repair the road before the winter began."]},"2022||promulgate":{"def":"promote or make widely known (an idea or cause)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth, forward"},{"part":"mulg","type":"root","meaning":"make known, publish"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin promulgare, 'to publish, make known'; exact origin of the root is uncertain.","mnemonic":"Pro-mulgate: push the news forth so everyone hears it, like a town crier.","sentences":["The government will promulgate the new safety regulations next month, and companies will have a year to comply.","Missionaries traveled widely to promulgate their faith across the region during the early centuries.","Activists used social media to promulgate their message, reaching millions of people within a single week."]},"2022||didactic":{"def":"intended to teach, particularly in having moral instruction as an ulterior motive","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"didac","type":"root","meaning":"teach"},{"part":"-tic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek didaktikos, from didaskein 'to teach'.","mnemonic":"A didactic book is a dad-tic: always lecturing you with a lesson.","sentences":["The novel felt too didactic, as every chapter ended with a heavy-handed lesson about honesty and hard work.","Her didactic tone made the dinner party feel like a lecture, and guests quietly stopped asking questions.","Medieval fables were often didactic, designed to teach children right from wrong through simple animal stories."]},"2022||reprisal":{"def":"an action or act of retaliation","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"prisal","type":"root","meaning":"taking, seizing"}],"origin":"From Old French reprisaille, from Italian ripresaglia, from Latin reprehendere 'to take back'.","mnemonic":"Re-prise-al: you take something back from someone who hurt you.","sentences":["Workers feared reprisal from their employer if they spoke to journalists about the unsafe conditions in the plant.","The attack was carried out in reprisal for the earlier bombing, and tensions rose along the border.","She refused to report the bullying, afraid that any reprisal would only make school life worse."]},"2022||fraught":{"def":"(of a situation or course of action) filled with or likely to result in (something undesirable)","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"fraught","type":"root","meaning":"loaded, freight"}],"origin":"From Middle Dutch vracht, 'cargo, freight'; originally 'loaded as a ship with cargo'.","mnemonic":"Fraught rhymes with caught: caught up and loaded with trouble, like freight.","sentences":["The negotiations were fraught with tension, and each side suspected the other of hiding critical information.","Crossing the icy mountain pass in winter is a journey fraught with danger for even skilled drivers.","Their conversation about money was fraught from the start, since both of them were already anxious and tired."]},"2022||munificent":{"def":"larger or more generous than is usual or necessary","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"muni","type":"root","meaning":"gift, duty"},{"part":"fic","type":"root","meaning":"make, do"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being, having"}],"origin":"From Latin munificus, 'generous', from munus 'gift' + facere 'to make'.","mnemonic":"Muni-fice-nt: a person who makes lots of money-gifts, very generous.","sentences":["The munificent donor gave ten million dollars to the library, far more than anyone had asked for.","Her munificent tips amazed the waiters, who had never been left such large sums by a single customer.","The king was famously munificent, feeding the poor each winter and rewarding even his smallest servants."]},"2022||ardent":{"def":"enthusiastic or passionate","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ard","type":"root","meaning":"burn, glow"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being, having"}],"origin":"From Latin ardens, 'burning', from ardere 'to burn'.","mnemonic":"Ardent fans burn like a fire: think 'arson' for passion.","sentences":["He was an ardent supporter of the local team and never missed a home game in twenty years.","The ardent young poet wrote letters every day, pouring his deepest feelings onto the page.","Her ardent belief in clean energy led her to start a company that installs solar panels for schools."]},"2022||copacetic":{"def":"in excellent order","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"copacetic","type":"root","meaning":"excellent (origin uncertain)"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; American slang from the early 1900s, possibly from African American English.","mnemonic":"Co-pa-set-ic: everything is set, in place, and perfectly fine.","sentences":["After a rough week, the mechanic assured us that the engine was running copacetic and the trip could go ahead.","Don't worry about the schedule; everything is copacetic, and the guests will arrive to a ready house.","My landlord inspected the apartment and said it was all copacetic, so I would get my deposit back."]},"2022||rapport":{"def":"a close and harmonious relationship in which the people or groups concerned understand each other's feelings or ideas and communicate well.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"port","type":"root","meaning":"carry"}],"origin":"From French rapport, 'a bringing back, return', from rapporter 'to bring back'.","mnemonic":"Rapport rhymes with 'report': you report back the same feelings, sharing them.","sentences":["The new teacher quickly built a warm rapport with her students, who felt comfortable sharing their worries.","Good rapport between the doctor and patient can make an anxious visit feel much easier for everyone.","The two negotiators had little rapport at first, but shared jokes over coffee soon eased the mood."]},"2022||advent":{"def":"the arrival of a notable person, thing, or event","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"vent","type":"root","meaning":"come"}],"origin":"From Latin adventus, 'arrival', from advenire 'to come to'.","mnemonic":"Ad + vent: something that 'comes toward' us. Think Advent calendar counting down to arrival.","sentences":["The advent of the smartphone changed how people shop, travel, and talk to each other in everyday life.","Farmers welcomed the advent of spring, since warmer weather meant planting could finally begin in the fields.","With the advent of cheap air travel, families spread across continents could visit one another more often."]},"2022||diaphanous":{"def":"(especially of fabric) light, delicate, and translucent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dia-","type":"prefix","meaning":"through"},{"part":"phan","type":"root","meaning":"show, appear"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Greek diaphanes, 'transparent', from dia 'through' + phainein 'to show'.","mnemonic":"Dia-phan: light shines through, like a fan of sheer silk.","sentences":["She wore a diaphanous scarf that floated behind her in the breeze and let the sunlight shine through.","Diaphanous curtains softened the harsh afternoon light, filling the quiet bedroom with a gentle glow.","The dancer's diaphanous costume shimmered under the stage lights, almost disappearing with each turn."]},"2022||juncture":{"def":"a point of time, especially one made critical or important by a concurrence of circumstances","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"junct","type":"root","meaning":"join"},{"part":"-ure","type":"suffix","meaning":"result of an action"}],"origin":"From Latin junctura, 'a joining', from jungere 'to join'.","mnemonic":"A juncture is where roads join; life has junctures where you must choose.","sentences":["At this critical juncture, the company must decide whether to expand overseas or protect its home market.","The talks reached a delicate juncture when both sides refused to give up their central demands.","Looking back, she saw that quitting her job was the juncture where her whole life changed direction."]},"2022||boon":{"def":"a thing that is helpful or beneficial","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"boon","type":"root","meaning":"favor, benefit"}],"origin":"From Old Norse bón, 'a request, prayer'; sense shifted to 'favor' then 'blessing'.","mnemonic":"A boon is a bonus: a good gift that lands in your lap.","sentences":["The new bike lane proved a boon to commuters, cutting their travel time and keeping them healthier.","For the struggling restaurant, the sudden rush of tourists was an unexpected boon that saved the season.","Cheap solar panels have been a boon to rural villages that never had reliable electricity before."]},"2022||pedigree":{"def":"the recorded ancestry, especially upper-class ancestry, of a person or family","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pedigree","type":"root","meaning":"crane's foot"}],"origin":"From Middle French pie de grue, 'crane's foot', from the three-branched mark used in family trees.","mnemonic":"A family tree's branches look like a crane's foot: pedigree charts your ancestors.","sentences":["The champion racehorse had an impressive pedigree, with several famous winners among its parents and grandparents.","The young designer's pedigree included study at the best schools and an apprenticeship under a famous master.","Breeders showed the buyer the dog's papers, proving a pedigree that went back nine generations."]},"2022||conveyance":{"def":"the action or process of transporting someone or something from one place to another.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, with"},{"part":"vey","type":"root","meaning":"way, road"},{"part":"-ance","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or state"}],"origin":"From Old French conveier, 'to escort', from Latin con- + via 'way'.","mnemonic":"Con + via: going along the way together, so conveyance carries you.","sentences":["The old horse cart was the only conveyance available for reaching the remote mountain village in that era.","Lawyers handled the conveyance of the house, passing the title from the seller to the new owners.","The tunnel is designed for the conveyance of thousands of passengers each hour between the two cities."]},"2022||effulge":{"def":"to shine forth; to radiate","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"fulg","type":"root","meaning":"shine"}],"origin":"From Latin effulgere, 'to shine forth', from ex- + fulgere 'to shine'; rare back-formation from effulgent.","mnemonic":"Ef-fulge: a bulb that fulges, shining its glow outward.","sentences":["At dawn the golden sun began to effulge over the hills, lighting the valley with warm color.","Her smile seemed to effulge in the dim room, making everyone feel welcome and at ease.","The lighthouse lamp effulged across the dark water, guiding the weary fishing boats safely back to shore."]},"2022||inimical":{"def":"tending to obstruct or harm; unfriendly; hostile","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"amic","type":"root","meaning":"friend"},{"part":"-al","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Late Latin inimicalis, from inimicus 'enemy', from in- 'not' + amicus 'friend'.","mnemonic":"In-imical: not a friend, so an enemy to your goals.","sentences":["Heavy smoke is inimical to health, especially for children and people who already have lung problems.","The dictator viewed free newspapers as inimical to his rule and shut them down one by one.","Loud music is inimical to careful study, so the library enforces strict silence on the top floor."]},"2022||demarcation":{"def":"the marking of boundaries or categories","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"off, from"},{"part":"marc","type":"root","meaning":"boundary, mark"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or result"}],"origin":"From Spanish demarcación, from demarcar 'to mark out boundaries', ultimately of Germanic origin (mark).","mnemonic":"De-mark-ation: you mark a line to show where something stops.","sentences":["A clear line of demarcation separates the two districts, marked by a river and a long stone wall.","The treaty required careful demarcation of the border so that farmers knew whose land they were working.","There is little demarcation between her work life and home life, since she answers emails at all hours."]},"2022||morass":{"def":"an area of muddy or boggy ground; a complicated or confused situation","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"morass","type":"root","meaning":"marsh, swamp"}],"origin":"From Dutch moeras, 'marsh', from Old French marais, of Germanic origin.","mnemonic":"A morass is a moist mess: you get stuck like boots in a swamp.","sentences":["The hikers sank to their knees in the morass and spent hours pulling their boots free from the mud.","Lawmakers have been trapped in a bureaucratic morass of rules and appeals that stalls every reform proposal.","I tried to sort out my tax records, but the morass of forms and missing receipts defeated me."]},"2022||catalytic":{"def":"relating to or involving the action of a catalyst.","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"cata-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down, wholly"},{"part":"ly","type":"root","meaning":"loosen, break"},{"part":"-tic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From catalyst, from Greek katalysis 'dissolution', from kata- + lyein 'to loosen'.","mnemonic":"Catalytic things trigger change: a cat sets off a chain reaction by knocking one glass.","sentences":["The new law played a catalytic role in reform, setting off a chain of changes across the whole industry.","In the factory, a catalytic converter speeds up reactions that turn harmful exhaust gases into safer ones.","Her speech had a catalytic effect on the community, inspiring dozens of people to volunteer almost overnight."]},"2022||furor":{"def":"an outbreak of public anger or excitement","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"furor","type":"root","meaning":"rage, madness"}],"origin":"From Latin furor, 'rage, madness', from furere 'to rage'; related to fury.","mnemonic":"Furor is fury in public: everybody is furious at once.","sentences":["The senator's comments caused a furor, with thousands of angry messages flooding the office within a day.","A furor erupted over the new school dress code, and parents packed the meeting room to protest.","The leaked documents set off a furor in the press that lasted for the entire election season."]},"2022||consign":{"def":"deliver (something) to a person's custody, typically in order for it to be sold.","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, with"},{"part":"sign","type":"root","meaning":"mark, seal"}],"origin":"From Latin consignare, 'to seal with a seal, register', from con- + signare 'to mark'.","mnemonic":"Con + sign: sign the papers and hand your goods over.","sentences":["She decided to consign her late mother's furniture to an auction house rather than keep it in storage.","The editor chose to consign the dull manuscript to the trash pile without reading past the first page.","Merchants often consign goods to a shopkeeper, who pays them only after the items have actually sold."]},"2022||demagogue":{"def":"a leader who exploits popular prejudices and false claims and promises in order to gain power","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"demos","type":"root","meaning":"people"},{"part":"agogos","type":"root","meaning":"leader"}],"origin":"From Greek demagogos, 'leader of the people', from demos 'people' + agein 'to lead'.","mnemonic":"Demo + gogue: a leader of the crowd who leads it astray with angry lies.","sentences":["The demagogue promised easy answers to complicated problems and blamed immigrants for every economic hardship.","Ancient Athenians feared the demagogue who flattered the crowd to win power and then ignored their interests.","Historians warn that a demagogue thrives when people are afraid and ready to trust anyone with simple slogans."]},"2022||pillary":{"def":"attack or ridicule publicly","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pillary","type":"root","meaning":"variant of pillory (origin uncertain)"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; apparently a variant spelling of 'pillory', the wooden frame used to publicly shame offenders.","mnemonic":"Picture a criminal locked in a wooden frame in the square: public shame.","sentences":["Tabloid editors love to pillary celebrities for small mistakes, turning private errors into weeks of public ridicule.","Please do not pillary your colleagues in the group chat for one honest error in a spreadsheet.","Critics threatened to pillary the mayor in the press unless he explained where the missing funds had gone."]},"2022||typify":{"def":"be characteristic or a representative example of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"typ","type":"root","meaning":"type, example"},{"part":"-ify","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin typus, 'figure, type' (from Greek typos) + -ify, 'to make'.","mnemonic":"Typify: be the type, the perfect model of a kind of thing.","sentences":["Her calm manner and dry humor typify the people of the region, who rarely get rattled by anything.","The glass towers and busy streets typify the modern financial district found in cities around the world.","Critics say the film's lazy plot will typify the weak sequels that studios release each summer."]},"2022||milieu":{"def":"a person's social environment","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"mi-","type":"prefix","meaning":"middle"},{"part":"lieu","type":"root","meaning":"place"}],"origin":"From French milieu, 'middle, surroundings', from mi 'middle' + lieu 'place'.","mnemonic":"Mi-lieu: the place in the middle of which you live, your surroundings.","sentences":["The author grew up in a working-class milieu, and her stories reflect the struggles of her neighbors.","In the artistic milieu of 1920s Paris, young writers met in cafes to argue about literature and politics.","He felt out of place in the corporate milieu, where everyone wore suits and talked only about quarterly numbers."]},"2022||gumption":{"def":"shrewd or spirited initiative and resourcefulness","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"gumption","type":"root","meaning":"initiative, common sense"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; Scottish dialect word from the early 1700s meaning 'common sense'.","mnemonic":"Gumption is guts plus action: the get-up-and-go in your gut.","sentences":["It took real gumption for the teenager to start his own business and pitch it to local investors.","She had the gumption to walk into the director's office and ask for the promotion she had earned.","With a little gumption and a borrowed ladder, he fixed the leaking roof himself before the storm arrived."]},"2022||scourge":{"def":"a person or thing that causes great trouble or misfortune","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"scourge","type":"root","meaning":"whip"}],"origin":"From Old French escorge, from Latin excoriare 'to flay'; originally a whip, later any affliction.","mnemonic":"A scourge is a whip: a plague that lashes a whole land.","sentences":["Malaria remains a scourge in many tropical regions, killing hundreds of thousands of children every single year.","The king saw the pirates as a scourge upon his coast and sent his entire fleet to destroy them.","For small towns near the border, drug addiction has become a scourge that tears families apart."]},"2022||recant":{"def":"say that one no longer holds an opinion or belief, especially one considered heretical","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back, again"},{"part":"cant","type":"root","meaning":"sing, chant"}],"origin":"From Latin recantare, 'to revoke, sing again', from re- + cantare 'to sing'.","mnemonic":"Re-chant: sing the opposite song and take back what you said.","sentences":["Under pressure from the court, the scientist refused to recant his claim that the earth moved around the sun.","The witness chose to recant her earlier statement, admitting she had not actually seen the accident happen.","The newspaper forced the columnist to recant his remarks and publish a public apology the next morning."]},"2022||incantation":{"def":"a series of words said as a magic spell or charm","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, upon"},{"part":"cant","type":"root","meaning":"sing, chant"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or result"}],"origin":"From Latin incantatio, 'enchanting', from incantare 'to sing a spell over', from in- + cantare.","mnemonic":"In-chant-ation: a chant that casts magic on someone.","sentences":["The witch murmured an incantation over the bubbling pot, and a strange green smoke rose into the air.","Children chanted a silly incantation before blowing out their candles, believing it would make wishes come true.","The old monk's low incantation echoed through the stone hall, its strange words unknown to the visitors."]},"2022||decrement":{"def":"a reduction or diminution","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down, away"},{"part":"cre","type":"root","meaning":"grow"},{"part":"-ment","type":"suffix","meaning":"result, state"}],"origin":"From Latin decrementum, 'a decrease', from decrescere 'to grow less'.","mnemonic":"The opposite of an increment: de-crement, a small step down.","sentences":["Each year brought a small decrement in the village's population as young people left for city jobs.","The program applies a decrement of one point to the score for every wrong answer given.","Even a tiny decrement in battery life can matter when the device is used for long hikes."]},"2022||detente":{"def":"the easing of hostility or strained relations, especially between countries.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, undo"},{"part":"tente","type":"root","meaning":"tension, stretching"}],"origin":"From French détente, 'relaxation, loosening', from détendre 'to slacken'.","mnemonic":"De-tense: release the tension between rivals.","sentences":["After years of hostility, the two nations entered a period of detente, resuming trade and cultural exchange.","The sudden detente between the rival coaches surprised fans, who had expected the feud to continue all season.","A fragile detente settled over the family dinner once Grandpa and my aunt stopped arguing about politics."]},"2022||antipathy":{"def":"a deep-seated feeling of dislike; aversion","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"anti-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"path","type":"root","meaning":"feeling"},{"part":"-y","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From Greek antipatheia, from anti 'against' + pathos 'feeling'.","mnemonic":"Anti + path(feeling): a feeling against someone, like pathos in reverse.","sentences":["She felt a deep antipathy toward her boss, who constantly criticized her work in front of colleagues.","The two brothers' mutual antipathy was obvious, and relatives never seated them side by side at family gatherings.","Public antipathy to the tax grew quickly, and protests spread across the country within a few weeks."]},"2022||furlough":{"def":"suspension or discharge of a worker or workers on account of economic conditions or shortage of work, especially when temporary","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"furlough","type":"root","meaning":"leave of absence"}],"origin":"From Dutch verlof, 'permission, leave', via 17th-century English; related to Old English leave.","mnemonic":"Fur-low: you go low on work and take a break without pay.","sentences":["The company announced a temporary furlough for half its staff while sales recovered during the winter months.","The soldier returned home on furlough, spending two weeks with his family before heading back to the base.","Many airline workers faced a long furlough when travel dropped sharply, and some had to find other jobs."]},"2022||kleptocracy":{"def":"a society or system ruled by people who user their power to steal from their country's resources","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"klept","type":"root","meaning":"steal"},{"part":"-o-","type":"suffix","meaning":"connecting vowel"},{"part":"-cracy","type":"suffix","meaning":"rule, government"}],"origin":"From Greek kleptes 'thief' + -kratia 'rule'; coined in the 20th century.","mnemonic":"Klepto-cracy: a government run by kleptomaniacs who steal everything.","sentences":["Critics described the country as a kleptocracy, where officials grew rich while hospitals and schools went unfunded.","Journalists exposed how the kleptocracy hid billions in foreign bank accounts and luxury properties abroad.","Living in a kleptocracy meant that every permit required a bribe, and honest businesses struggled to survive."]},"2022||modicum":{"def":"a small quantity of a particular thing, especially something considered desirable or valuable","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"mod","type":"root","meaning":"measure, moderate"},{"part":"-icum","type":"suffix","meaning":"small thing"}],"origin":"From Latin modicum, 'a small amount', neuter of modicus 'moderate', from modus 'measure'.","mnemonic":"A modicum is a modest amount: just a tiny measure.","sentences":["With even a modicum of effort, he could have passed the exam, but he never opened the book.","The negotiators showed a modicum of flexibility, and finally the talks moved a little bit forward.","She kept a modicum of hope alive, even after the search party returned with no news at all."]},"2022||potentate":{"def":"a monarch or ruler, especially an autocratic one","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"potent","type":"root","meaning":"powerful"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who is"}],"origin":"From Latin potentatus, 'dominion', from potens 'powerful'.","mnemonic":"Potent + ate: a ruler who is very potent, with power over everyone.","sentences":["The oil-rich potentate lived in a golden palace while his people struggled to afford basic food.","Travelers brought gifts to the potentate, hoping to earn his favor and safe passage across his lands.","No potentate, however powerful, can rule forever without the quiet cooperation of generals and tax collectors."]},"2022||pall":{"def":"become less appealing or interesting through familiarity","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pall","type":"root","meaning":"become vapid (from appall/pale)"}],"origin":"From Middle English pallen, 'to grow faint', probably shortened from appall; sense developed to 'lose appeal'.","mnemonic":"Even your favorite song will pall if it plays on repeat all day.","sentences":["Even the sweetest candy can pall if you eat it every day for a month.","The thrill of the new video game began to pall after a week of playing the same level.","Luxury hotels may pall for frequent travelers, who eventually miss simple meals and a quiet bed at home."]},"2022||scrupulous":{"def":"diligent, thorough, and extremely attentive to details","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"scrup","type":"root","meaning":"sharp stone"},{"part":"-ul","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin scrupulosus, from scrupulus 'small sharp stone', a worry in one's shoe; hence conscientious.","mnemonic":"A tiny stone in your shoe nags you; a scrupulous person feels every detail.","sentences":["The accountant was scrupulous, checking each number twice before signing the final report for the board.","A scrupulous editor will catch every typo, even the tiny ones hiding in the footnotes.","She was scrupulous about returning borrowed items, always bringing them back clean and a day early."]},"2022||purveyor":{"def":"a person who sells or deals in particular goods","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pur-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth, before"},{"part":"vey","type":"root","meaning":"see, provide"},{"part":"-or","type":"suffix","meaning":"one who"}],"origin":"From Anglo-French purveour, from purveier 'to provide', from Latin providere 'to foresee'.","mnemonic":"A purveyor provides: he pursues your order and delivers the goods.","sentences":["The shop was a purveyor of fine cheeses, importing dozens of varieties from small farms across Europe.","As the town's main purveyor of grain, he knew every farmer and every baker in the region.","Some websites act as purveyors of false stories, spreading rumors faster than any fact-checker can respond."]},"2022||defray":{"def":"provide money to pay (a cost or expense)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away, off"},{"part":"fray","type":"root","meaning":"cost, expense"}],"origin":"From French défrayer, 'to pay the costs', from de- + frais 'expenses'.","mnemonic":"De-fray: take the fray (cost) off someone's back by paying.","sentences":["The grant will defray the cost of travel for students who cannot afford the trip to the conference.","Ticket sales helped defray the expenses of the school play, covering costumes, lighting, and the stage.","The company offered to defray moving costs for any employee willing to relocate to the new office."]},"2022||pejorative":{"def":"having a disparaging, derogatory, or belittling effect or force","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pejor","type":"root","meaning":"worse"},{"part":"-ative","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Late Latin pejorativus, from pejorare 'to make worse', from pejor 'worse'.","mnemonic":"Pejorative sounds like 'peeved' and 'worse': a word that makes things worse.","sentences":["The word was once neutral, but it has since taken on a pejorative meaning that most people now avoid.","He used the term in a pejorative way, clearly meaning it as an insult rather than a description.","Linguists note that many pejorative labels begin as plain descriptions and gradually pick up an insulting tone."]},"2022||espouse":{"def":"adopt or support (a cause, belief, or way of life)","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out, thoroughly"},{"part":"spous","type":"root","meaning":"betroth, promise"}],"origin":"From Old French espouser, 'to marry', from Latin sponsare 'to betroth'; sense extended from marrying to embracing a cause.","mnemonic":"Espouse sounds like spouse: you marry an idea and stay devoted.","sentences":["The young senator began to espouse the cause of clean energy and spoke about it at every rally.","Philosophers who espouse extreme views often attract devoted followers, along with fierce critics.","She espoused a simple lifestyle, selling her car and moving into a tiny apartment near the market."]},"2022||bellicose":{"def":"demonstrating aggression or willingness to fight","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"bell","type":"root","meaning":"war"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"},{"part":"-ose","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin bellicosus, 'warlike', from bellum 'war'.","mnemonic":"Bell-icose: ring the war bell; he's always ready to fight.","sentences":["The bellicose general demanded an immediate attack, ignoring his advisers who urged patience and negotiation.","His bellicose speech alarmed neighboring countries, who began to strengthen their own borders.","The two bellicose rivals could not share a room without one of them starting an argument."]},"2022||demurrage":{"def":"a charge payable to the owner of a chartered ship in respect of failure to load or discharge the ship within the time agreed","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"demur","type":"root","meaning":"delay, linger"},{"part":"-age","type":"suffix","meaning":"charge, process"}],"origin":"From Old French demorage, from demorer 'to linger, delay', from Latin demorari.","mnemonic":"Demur + age: pay when your ship lingers and stays too long.","sentences":["The shipping firm faced heavy demurrage after the cargo sat in the port for two weeks without being unloaded.","Importers worry about demurrage charges, which pile up each day a container stays on the dock.","Customs delays forced the merchant to pay demurrage on top of the freight, wiping out most of his profit."]},"2022||countervail":{"def":"offset the effect of [something] by countering it with something of equal force","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"counter-","type":"prefix","meaning":"against"},{"part":"vail","type":"root","meaning":"be strong, avail"}],"origin":"From Latin contra valere, 'to be strong against', via Anglo-French countrevaloir.","mnemonic":"Counter + avail: set something of equal strength against a force.","sentences":["The central bank tried to countervail the rising prices by raising interest rates over several months.","Strong unions can countervail the power of large corporations, giving ordinary workers a real voice in negotiations.","Her cheerful nature seemed to countervail her brother's gloom, balancing the mood at every family dinner."]},"2022||internecine":{"def":"destructive to both sides in a conflict","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"inter-","type":"prefix","meaning":"between"},{"part":"nec","type":"root","meaning":"killing, death"},{"part":"-ine","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin internecinus, 'murderous', from internecare 'to kill off', from inter + necare 'to kill'.","mnemonic":"Inter-necine: death between them, so both sides lose.","sentences":["The internecine war between the rival gangs left both groups weakened and nearly destroyed.","Internecine disputes within the party cost it the election, as members fought each other rather than the opposition.","The company was torn apart by internecine rivalries, with each department trying to ruin the others."]},"2022||bastion":{"def":"an institution, place, or person strongly defending or upholding particular principles, attitudes, or activities","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"bast","type":"root","meaning":"build, fortify"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"result, thing"}],"origin":"From French bastion, from Italian bastione, from bastire 'to build'; a projecting part of a fortress.","mnemonic":"A bastion is a bastard-strong fort: a stronghold that holds firm.","sentences":["The old library remained a bastion of quiet learning in a city increasingly filled with noise and screens.","Soldiers held the northern bastion for three days, defending the fortress walls against wave after wave of attackers.","The small college was a bastion of free debate, welcoming students with every kind of opinion."]},"2022||zeitgeist":{"def":"the spirit of the time; the general trend of thought, feeling, or tastes characteristic of a particular period of time","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"zeit","type":"root","meaning":"time"},{"part":"geist","type":"root","meaning":"spirit, ghost"}],"origin":"From German Zeitgeist, 'spirit of the time', from Zeit 'time' + Geist 'spirit'.","mnemonic":"Zeit + geist: the ghost of the times haunting everything popular.","sentences":["The film captured the zeitgeist of the 1960s, reflecting the hopes and anxieties of a restless generation.","Marketers study the zeitgeist closely, hoping to guess which ideas and styles will become popular next season.","Her novel perfectly caught the zeitgeist of the digital age, with its endless scrolling and constant worry."]},"2022||apparatus":{"def":"any system or systematic organization of activities, functions, processes, etc., directed toward a specific goal","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ad-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"par","type":"root","meaning":"prepare"},{"part":"-atus","type":"suffix","meaning":"result, thing"}],"origin":"From Latin apparatus, 'preparation, equipment', from apparare 'to prepare', from ad- + parare.","mnemonic":"Apparatus: the stuff you prepare, a set of equipment or machinery.","sentences":["The chemistry teacher set up an apparatus of glass tubes and burners to demonstrate how water is purified.","The state's security apparatus monitored thousands of citizens, relying on informers, cameras, and a network of offices.","Firefighters carried a heavy breathing apparatus up the stairs, ready to enter the smoke-filled building."]},"2022||shill":{"def":"a person who publicizes or praises something or someone for reasons of self-interest, personal profit, or friendship or loyalty","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"shill","type":"root","meaning":"decoy, hired promoter"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; American slang from the early 1900s for a carnival decoy who feigns being a customer.","mnemonic":"A shill is a fake fan in the crowd, cheering for the seller.","sentences":["The auctioneer used a shill to raise bids, planting a fake bidder in the crowd to drive up prices.","Commenters accused the influencer of being a paid shill for the supplement company after he praised it daily.","Not every reviewer is a shill, but suspiciously glowing posts sometimes come from friends of the owner."]},"2022||stochastic":{"def":"randomly determined; having a random probability distribution or pattern that may be analyzed statistically but may not be predicted precisely","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"stochast","type":"root","meaning":"guesser, aimer"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek stochastikos, 'skilled at aiming or guessing', from stochazesthai 'to aim at, guess'.","mnemonic":"Stochastic: stock prices flicker at random; you can only guess the pattern.","sentences":["Weather is a stochastic process, so forecasters speak in probabilities rather than promising exactly what will occur.","The researchers built a stochastic model of the stock market, with random shocks that cannot be predicted precisely.","Because customer arrivals are stochastic, the store manager schedules extra staff just in case a rush appears."]},"2022||insipid":{"def":"lacking flavor","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"sip","type":"root","meaning":"taste, savor"}],"origin":"From Latin insipidus, 'tasteless', from in- 'not' + sapidus 'savory', from sapere 'to taste'.","mnemonic":"In-sip-id: you wouldn't want a second sip, because it's bland.","sentences":["The soup was thin and insipid, and no amount of salt could give it any real flavor.","Critics found the film insipid, with flat characters and a plot as bland as unseasoned rice.","He gave an insipid speech full of safe clichés, and the audience quickly forgot every word."]},"2021||seminal":{"def":"highly original and influencing the development of future events","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"semin","type":"root","meaning":"seed"},{"part":"-al","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin seminalis, 'of seed', from semen 'seed'.","mnemonic":"Seminal works are seeds from which a whole field grows.","sentences":["Darwin's seminal book on evolution shaped biology for generations and still guides researchers today.","The band's seminal debut album influenced countless musicians who formed their own groups in the decades that followed.","She published a seminal paper that opened a new area of study and was cited thousands of times."]},"2021||syndicate":{"def":"a group of individuals or organizations combined or making a joint effort to undertake some specific duty or carry out specific transactions or negotiations","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"syn-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"dic","type":"root","meaning":"justice, speak"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"group, office"}],"origin":"From French syndicat, from Late Latin syndicus 'representative', from Greek syndikos 'advocate', from syn- + dike 'justice'.","mnemonic":"Syn + dic: people who join together under one banner to do business.","sentences":["A syndicate of wealthy investors bought the failing newspaper, planning to turn it around within two years.","Police broke up a crime syndicate that had smuggled goods across three countries for nearly a decade.","Her cartoon strip ran in a syndicate, so papers across the country printed it each morning."]},"2021||disparate":{"def":"distinct in kind; essentially different; dissimilar","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart"},{"part":"par","type":"root","meaning":"equal, prepare"}],"origin":"From Latin disparatus, 'separated', past participle of disparare 'to separate'; influenced by dispar 'unequal'.","mnemonic":"Dis + pair: they don't make a pair, so they're totally different.","sentences":["The committee included disparate voices, from union leaders to bankers, who rarely agreed on anything.","The two cities, though only an hour apart, have disparate cultures, foods, and ways of speaking.","Researchers combined disparate sources of data, from satellite images to local surveys, to understand the drought."]},"2021||engender":{"def":"cause or give rise to","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"cause to be"},{"part":"gender","type":"root","meaning":"beget, produce"}],"origin":"From Old French engendrer, from Latin ingenerare, 'to beget', from in- + generare 'to produce'.","mnemonic":"En-gender: give birth to a feeling, like generating it.","sentences":["Honest leadership tends to engender trust, while secrecy and spin quickly engender doubt among employees.","The unfair ruling did engender widespread anger, and protesters filled the streets the very next afternoon.","Good teachers engender curiosity in children by asking questions rather than simply handing out answers."]},"2021||specious":{"def":"superficially plausible, but actually wrong","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"spec","type":"root","meaning":"look, appearance"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin speciosus, 'fair-looking, plausible', from species 'appearance', from specere 'to look'.","mnemonic":"Specious: spec-ious looks special on the surface, but it's fake.","sentences":["His specious argument sounded convincing at first, but it fell apart as soon as someone checked the numbers.","The lawyer offered a specious excuse for the delay, hoping the judge would not notice the missing dates.","Advertisements often make specious claims, using clever wording that hides how little the product actually does."]},"2021||accost":{"def":"to approach and speak to first; to confront in a challenging or aggressive way","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ac-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to, toward"},{"part":"cost","type":"root","meaning":"rib, side"}],"origin":"From Middle French accoster, from Late Latin accostare, 'to be side by side', from ad- + costa 'rib, side'.","mnemonic":"Ac-cost: someone sidles up to your side and demands attention.","sentences":["A stranger tried to accost her on the dark street, so she crossed quickly and entered a shop.","Reporters will accost the mayor as he leaves the building, shouting questions about the missing funds.","Salespeople in the market would accost every tourist, pushing bracelets and scarves into their hands."]},"2021||derision":{"def":"contemptuous ridicule or mockery","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"down"},{"part":"ris","type":"root","meaning":"laugh"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin deridere, to laugh at, from de- + ridere 'to laugh'.","mnemonic":"Deriding crowd laughs DOWN at you: de-ris-ion, a scornful riot of laughter.","sentences":["The crowd greeted the mayor's absurd promise with open derision, jeering until he left the stage.","She endured years of derision from classmates before her invention finally won the national science prize.","His derision was obvious when he snorted and called the amateur painting a child's scribble."]},"2021||exigency":{"def":"an urgent need or demand","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"ig","type":"root","meaning":"drive (agere)"},{"part":"-ency","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin exigentia, from exigere 'to demand, drive out'.","mnemonic":"An EXIT-gency: the building's on fire and you must leave NOW.","sentences":["The exigency of the flood forced the town to evacuate hundreds of residents within hours.","Faced with the exigency of a failing engine, the pilot had no time to consult the manual.","Budget cuts are rarely popular, but the exigency of the deficit left the council no choice."]},"2021||congenial":{"def":"pleasant because of a personality, qualities, or interests that are similar to one's own","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"genial","type":"root","meaning":"pleasant, of genius/spirit"}],"origin":"From Latin con- 'together' + English genial, from Latin genialis 'festive, pleasant'.","mnemonic":"CON-genial: con-genes together; kindred spirits who gel like old friends.","sentences":["Her congenial manner made the nervous new hires feel at home from the first minute.","We found a congenial group of hikers who shared our love of long, quiet trails.","The small cafe offered a congenial atmosphere, with soft music and neighbors chatting over coffee."]},"2021||haphazard":{"def":"lacking any obvious principle of organization","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"hap","type":"root","meaning":"chance, luck"},{"part":"hazard","type":"root","meaning":"risk, dice game"}],"origin":"From Old Norse happ 'chance' + English hazard (from Old French hasard, a dice game).","mnemonic":"Hap-hazard: happy-go-lucky, as if tossed by a hazard of dice.","sentences":["The files were stored in a haphazard pile, so finding last year's invoice took an entire afternoon.","His haphazard approach to studying, with no schedule or notes, led to a disappointing exam result.","Houses sprang up in haphazard fashion along the hillside, with no roads or plan connecting them."]},"2021||deft":{"def":"neatly skillful and quick in one's movements; demonstrating skill and cleverness","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"deft","type":"root","meaning":"neat, fitting"}],"origin":"From Middle English deft, a variant of daft, originally meaning 'gentle, fitting'.","mnemonic":"Think 'daft' made neat: a DEFT thief's nimble fingers pick the lock in a flash.","sentences":["The surgeon's deft hands stitched the wound in seconds, leaving barely a scar.","With a deft flick of her wrist, the chef flipped the pancake perfectly.","He made a deft reply to the hostile question, calming the room without losing his point."]},"2021||buttress":{"def":"increase the strength of or justification for; reinforce","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"buttress","type":"root","meaning":"supporting wall/prop"}],"origin":"From Old French bouterez, from bouter 'to push, thrust'; the noun denotes a masonry support.","mnemonic":"A BUTT-ress props up a wall like a big stone rear end holding it steady.","sentences":["The lawyer buttressed her argument with three eyewitness statements and a security video.","Engineers buttressed the aging dam with massive concrete walls before the spring floods arrived.","New data buttress the researchers' claim that sleep improves memory in teenagers."]},"2021||collate":{"def":"to compare critically in order to note differences, similarities, etc.; to arrange in order for some specific purpose","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"col-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"lat","type":"root","meaning":"carried"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin collatus, past participle of conferre 'to bring together'.","mnemonic":"CO-LATE: bring pages together, even if you're late, and put them in order.","sentences":["The assistant had to collate the survey responses into one spreadsheet before the meeting began.","Before printing the booklet, please collate the pages so each copy is in the proper order.","Scholars collated the three surviving manuscripts to spot differences between the medieval versions of the poem."]},"2021||impregnable":{"def":"unable to be captured or broken into; unable to be defeated or destroyed","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"pregn","type":"root","meaning":"take, seize (prehendere)"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Old French imprenable, 'not able to be taken', from Latin prehendere 'to seize'.","mnemonic":"IM-PREG-nable: cannot be taken; a fortress no army can grab.","sentences":["The medieval castle, perched on a cliff behind thick walls, seemed impregnable to any attacking army.","Despite its reputation as an impregnable system, the bank's network was breached in minutes.","The champion's defense looked impregnable all season, conceding only two goals in thirty matches."]},"2021||paucity":{"def":"the presence of something only in small or insufficient quantities or amounts; scarcity","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pauc","type":"root","meaning":"few, little"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin paucitas, from paucus 'few'.","mnemonic":"POOR-city: a city with a paucity of resources is poor in everything.","sentences":["A paucity of rainfall left the reservoirs nearly empty by the end of August.","The report noted a paucity of evidence, since only two small studies had examined the drug.","Critics lamented the paucity of good restaurants in a town of fifty thousand people."]},"2021||quaint":{"def":"attractively unusual or old-fashioned","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"quaint","type":"root","meaning":"cunning, odd, charming"}],"origin":"From Old French cointe 'skilled, pretty', from Latin cognitus 'known'; sense shifted to 'unusual, charming'.","mnemonic":"Quaint sounds like 'QUAINT-ly' tiny cottage with a thatched roof: cute and old.","sentences":["We strolled through the quaint village, admiring its thatched cottages and cobbled lanes.","The hotel's quaint charm included creaky floors, floral wallpaper, and a coal fireplace in every room.","Her grandmother's quaint habit of writing letters by hand delighted the whole family."]},"2021||reify":{"def":"make (something abstract) more concrete or real","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"re","type":"root","meaning":"thing (res)"},{"part":"-ify","type":"suffix","meaning":"make"}],"origin":"From Latin res 'thing' + -ify, 'to make into a thing'.","mnemonic":"RE-I-FY: turn an idea into a real THING you can poke.","sentences":["Critics warn that economists often reify the market, speaking of it as if it were a living creature.","The artist hoped to reify the idea of grief by sculpting it in heavy, dark stone.","Managers sometimes reify abstract goals, treating a number on a dashboard as if it were the real outcome."]},"2021||depredation":{"def":"the act of preying upon or plundering","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"pred","type":"root","meaning":"plunder, prey"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin depraedatio, from depraedari 'to plunder', from praeda 'prey, booty'.","mnemonic":"DE-PREY-dation: predators stripping a place bare.","sentences":["Coastal villages suffered years of depredation from raiders who burned boats and stole grain.","The farmers blamed wolves for the depredation of their sheep every winter.","Historians recorded the depredations of the invading army, which looted temples and emptied the royal treasury."]},"2021||salutary":{"def":"beneficial, helpful; healthful, wholesome","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"salut","type":"root","meaning":"health"},{"part":"-ary","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin salutaris, from salus 'health, safety'.","mnemonic":"SALUTE your health: a salutary walk makes you healthier.","sentences":["The failure proved a salutary lesson, teaching the young founder to double-check her financial projections.","Doctors recommend a salutary routine of regular exercise, fresh vegetables, and eight hours of sleep.","The scandal had a salutary effect on the company, forcing it to finally clean up its practices."]},"2021||abate":{"def":"to lessen in violence or intensity","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to, down"},{"part":"bat","type":"root","meaning":"beat"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Old French abatre 'to beat down', from Latin battuere 'to beat'.","mnemonic":"A-BATE: bait the storm until it beats itself down and dies off.","sentences":["Residents waited in the shelter until the hurricane's fury began to abate around dawn.","His anger did not abate until he had slept on the problem and read the apology twice.","The pain abated slowly once the medicine took effect, and she finally drifted off to sleep."]},"2021||lurid":{"def":"presented in vividly shocking or sensational terms","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"lurid","type":"root","meaning":"pale, yellowish"}],"origin":"From Latin luridus 'pale yellow, ghastly', later extended to 'shocking, glaring'.","mnemonic":"LURID sounds like 'lure': tabloids lure readers with shocking, gory headlines.","sentences":["The tabloid ran a lurid account of the crime, complete with gruesome details and blood-red headlines.","She wore a lurid orange jacket that practically glowed against the gray winter street.","Critics said the film's lurid violence was designed to shock rather than to tell a story."]},"2021||provenance":{"def":"place or source of origin","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth"},{"part":"ven","type":"root","meaning":"come"},{"part":"-ance","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From French provenir, from Latin provenire 'to come forth'.","mnemonic":"PRO-VEN-ance: where a thing PROVEd to have COME from.","sentences":["The museum checked the painting's provenance carefully, tracing every owner back to the artist's studio.","Buyers of vintage wine often demand proof of provenance to be sure the bottle is genuine.","Researchers questioned the provenance of the manuscript, unsure which monastery had originally produced it."]},"2021||consummate":{"def":"complete or perfect in the highest degree","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, fully"},{"part":"summ","type":"root","meaning":"sum, highest"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin consummare 'to sum up, complete', from summus 'highest'.","mnemonic":"CON-SUM-mate: the SUM total of skill, completely finished.","sentences":["She was a consummate professional, handling the crisis with calm precision and perfect manners.","The pianist's consummate skill left the critics searching for any flaw and finding none.","He was a consummate liar, so smooth that even his closest friends never suspected him."]},"2021||quintessential":{"def":"representing the most perfect or typical example of a quality or class","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"quint","type":"root","meaning":"fifth"},{"part":"essent","type":"root","meaning":"being, essence"},{"part":"-ial","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From medieval Latin quinta essentia, the 'fifth essence' beyond earth, air, fire and water.","mnemonic":"QUINT-essential: the fifth element, the purest essence of a thing.","sentences":["With its red buses and rain, London is the quintessential English city for many tourists.","The diner, with its chrome stools and jukebox, was the quintessential American roadside stop.","He was the quintessential absent-minded professor, forever losing his glasses while they sat on his head."]},"2021||exculpate":{"def":"show or declare that (someone) is not guilty of wrongdoing","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out of"},{"part":"culp","type":"root","meaning":"blame, fault"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin ex- + culpa 'fault, blame'.","mnemonic":"EX-CULP-ate: take the CULPrit OUT of trouble; clear him.","sentences":["New DNA evidence helped exculpate the man who had spent ten years in prison.","The investigator's report did exculpate the captain, showing the crash was caused by faulty equipment.","Lawyers hoped the surveillance footage would exculpate their client by placing him miles from the scene."]},"2021||stultify":{"def":"cause to lose enthusiasm and initiative, especially as a result of a tedious or restrictive routine","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"stult","type":"root","meaning":"foolish"},{"part":"-ify","type":"suffix","meaning":"make"}],"origin":"From Latin stultus 'foolish' + facere 'to make'; 'to make foolish or useless'.","mnemonic":"STULTIFY: a dull routine makes you a stuffed, stiff dummy.","sentences":["Endless paperwork can stultify even the most energetic employee, draining all creativity from the job.","Critics argued that rigid testing would stultify classrooms and leave no room for curiosity.","A boring, repetitive routine was stultifying her, and she began to dream of quitting."]},"2021||posterity":{"def":"all future generations of people","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"post","type":"root","meaning":"after"},{"part":"-erity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state, quality"}],"origin":"From Latin posteritas, from posterus 'coming after', from post 'after'.","mnemonic":"POST-erity: those who come POST (after) us, the future crowd reading our posts.","sentences":["The old general wrote his memoirs for posterity, hoping future generations would understand his choices.","Photographs were buried in a time capsule so that posterity could see how the town looked in 2020.","Scientists argue we must protect the forests not just for ourselves but for posterity."]},"2021||vitiate":{"def":"spoil or impair the quality or efficiency of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"viti","type":"root","meaning":"fault, vice"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin vitiare 'to spoil, corrupt', from vitium 'fault, vice'.","mnemonic":"VITIATE: VICE gets in and spoils the whole thing.","sentences":["Hidden bias in the survey questions could vitiate the entire study and make its results worthless.","Corruption threatened to vitiate the election, leaving voters doubting every ballot count.","A single careless mistake can vitiate months of careful lab work and waste the whole team's effort."]},"2021||purport":{"def":"appear or claim to be or do something, especially falsely; profess.","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pur-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth"},{"part":"port","type":"root","meaning":"carry"}],"origin":"From Anglo-French purporter, from Latin pro- 'forth' + portare 'to carry'.","mnemonic":"PURport: the email purports to be from your bank, but carries a fraud.","sentences":["The leaflet purports to offer medical advice, though it was written by a salesman with no training.","Many websites purport to reveal celebrity secrets, but most simply invent their stories.","The letter purported to come from the king, though scholars later proved it was a forgery."]},"2021||hubris":{"def":"excessive pride or self-confidence","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"hubris","type":"root","meaning":"insolence, arrogance"}],"origin":"From Ancient Greek hybris 'insolence, wanton violence'.","mnemonic":"HUBris: a hub of pride, so puffed up that a fall is certain.","sentences":["The CEO's hubris led him to ignore every warning, and the company collapsed within a year.","In Greek tragedies, a hero's hubris usually angers the gods and brings about his downfall.","Their hubris was clear when they boasted that no rival could ever beat them, right before losing."]},"2021||deign":{"def":"do something that one considers to be below one's dignity","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"deign","type":"root","meaning":"think worthy"}],"origin":"From Old French deigner, from Latin dignari 'to think worthy', from dignus 'worthy'.","mnemonic":"DEIGN: a snobby DIGNitary barely deigns to wave at you.","sentences":["The famous actor would not deign to speak with reporters, brushing past them without a glance.","She refused to deign to answer such a rude and baseless accusation.","Few critics would deign to review the comic book, though it sold millions of copies."]},"2021||visage":{"def":"a face or facial expression","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"vis","type":"root","meaning":"see, face"},{"part":"-age","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun suffix"}],"origin":"From Old French visage, from Latin visus 'sight, appearance', from videre 'to see'.","mnemonic":"VISage: what you VIS-ibly see, a face.","sentences":["A stern visage greeted the students as the headmaster stepped into the hall.","The old portrait showed a pale visage, with deep eyes and a thin, worried mouth.","Her cheerful visage lit up the room, and everyone relaxed as soon as she smiled."]},"2021||amalgamate":{"def":"to combine into a unified whole","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"malgam","type":"root","meaning":"soft mass (malagma)"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From medieval Latin amalgamare, from amalgama, a mixture of mercury and another metal.","mnemonic":"A-MAL-GAM-ate: a gum of metals melded into one.","sentences":["The two small banks decided to amalgamate, forming a single larger company with shared branches.","Chefs amalgamate flavors from several countries to create dishes that feel entirely new.","Several local clubs amalgamated into one league, ending decades of petty rivalry."]},"2021||respite":{"def":"a short period of rest or relief from something difficult or unpleasant","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back"},{"part":"spect","type":"root","meaning":"look"},{"part":"-ite","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun suffix"}],"origin":"From Old French respit, from Latin respectus 'looking back, regard'.","mnemonic":"RE-SPITE: take a break from spite and rest.","sentences":["After weeks of stormy weather, the sunny afternoon offered a welcome respite for the weary farmers.","Nurses on the busy ward rarely got a respite, working twelve hours with barely a pause.","The quiet garden provided a brief respite from the noise and stress of the city."]},"2021||injunction":{"def":"an authoritative warning or order","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"upon"},{"part":"junct","type":"root","meaning":"join, command (jungere)"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin injunctio, from injungere 'to impose, enjoin', from jungere 'to join'.","mnemonic":"IN-JUNCTION: a court order you cannot dodge at the junction.","sentences":["The judge issued an injunction forbidding the factory from dumping waste into the river.","Her doctor's injunction to rest for a week was clear and could not be ignored.","The company obtained an injunction to stop its former employee from sharing trade secrets."]},"2021||eminent":{"def":"famous and respected within a particular sphere or profession","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"min","type":"root","meaning":"jut, project"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"}],"origin":"From Latin eminens, 'standing out', from eminere 'to project, stand out'.","mnemonic":"E-MINENT: stands out like a mountain EMERGING above the crowd.","sentences":["An eminent surgeon from the capital was invited to lead the hospital's new research center.","She became an eminent historian, cited in textbooks and invited to lecture around the world.","The conference featured several eminent scientists, including two Nobel laureates and a famous astronomer."]},"2021||triage":{"def":"assign degrees of urgency to","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"triage","type":"root","meaning":"sorting (trier)"}],"origin":"From French trier 'to sort, pick out'; used first for sorting wool and later wounded soldiers.","mnemonic":"TRI-age: sort the hurt into three piles, urgent first.","sentences":["Nurses had to triage the arriving patients, treating the most seriously wounded before anyone else.","After the storm, volunteers triaged the repair requests, fixing broken water lines before leaking roofs.","When you triage your inbox, answer the urgent messages first and leave the rest for later."]},"2021||enmity":{"def":"hatred, ill-will","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not (in-)"},{"part":"mit","type":"root","meaning":"friend (amicus)"},{"part":"-y","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Old French enemistie, from Latin inimicus 'enemy', from in- 'not' + amicus 'friend'.","mnemonic":"ENMITY: ENEMY-ty, the state of being enemies.","sentences":["The two families had nursed a bitter enmity for generations, ever since the land dispute.","He felt no enmity toward his rival and even shook his hand after the loss.","Years of enmity between the neighboring countries made any peace talk difficult."]},"2021||incisive":{"def":"intelligently analytical and clear-thinking","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"into"},{"part":"cis","type":"root","meaning":"cut"},{"part":"-ive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin incisivus, from incidere 'to cut into'.","mnemonic":"IN-CISE-ive: a mind that cuts straight in, like scissors.","sentences":["Her incisive questions exposed the flaws in the proposal within just a few minutes of the meeting.","The critic's incisive review cut through the hype and explained exactly why the film failed.","He gave an incisive analysis of the crisis, ignoring the noise and naming its true cause."]},"2021||iniquity":{"def":"immoral or grossly unfair behavior","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"iqu","type":"root","meaning":"equal, fair (aequus)"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin iniquitas, from iniquus 'unequal, unfair', from in- + aequus 'equal'.","mnemonic":"IN-EQUITY: the opposite of equity, wicked and unjust.","sentences":["The preacher spoke of the iniquity of a city where the rich grew fat and the poor starved.","Reformers fought the iniquity of child labor in the mills and mines.","Out of sheer iniquity, the officials rigged the trial and punished an innocent man."]},"2021||licentious":{"def":"promiscuous and unprincipled in sexual matters","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"lic","type":"root","meaning":"be allowed"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin licentiosus, from licentia 'freedom, license', from licere 'to be allowed'.","mnemonic":"LICENTIOUS: a LICENSE to break every rule of decency.","sentences":["Moralists condemned the king's licentious court, where feasts and affairs went on every night.","The novel was banned for its licentious scenes and its disregard for ordinary morals.","The preacher denounced the licentious behavior of the city's wealthiest families from the pulpit every Sunday."]},"2021||disconcert":{"def":"disturb the composure of; unsettle","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart, undo"},{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together"},{"part":"cert","type":"root","meaning":"agree, harmony"}],"origin":"From obsolete French disconcerter, 'to upset the harmony', from dis- + concerter 'to bring into agreement'.","mnemonic":"DIS-CONCERT: ruin the concert's harmony and the singer freezes.","sentences":["The sudden question did disconcert the speaker, who lost his place and fumbled with his notes.","Her calm stare seemed to disconcert the interviewer, who began to stammer.","It can disconcert a young actor to see the audience whispering during a quiet scene."]},"2021||sanctimonious":{"def":"making a show of being morally superior to other people","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sanct","type":"root","meaning":"holy"},{"part":"-imon","type":"suffix","meaning":"quality"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin sanctimonia 'holiness', from sanctus 'holy'; now used for fake piety.","mnemonic":"SANCTI-monious: a saint-y moan, looking down from a holy high horse.","sentences":["He gave a sanctimonious lecture on honesty, then lied to everyone about his own expenses.","Nobody enjoyed dinner with her sanctimonious cousin, who judged every dish and every guest.","The politician's sanctimonious speech about family values sounded hollow after the scandal broke."]},"2021||stolid":{"def":"calm, dependable, and showing little emotion or animation","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"stolid","type":"root","meaning":"dull, stupid (stolidus)"}],"origin":"From Latin stolidus 'insensible, dull'; possibly related to stultus 'foolish'.","mnemonic":"STOLID: stoic and solid, a rock that doesn't wobble.","sentences":["The stolid guard stood at the gate, showing no reaction as the crowd shouted insults.","Her stolid face gave nothing away, even when the verdict was read aloud.","He was a stolid, dependable farmer who met good and bad harvests with the same calm."]},"2021||intractable":{"def":"hard to control or deal with","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"tract","type":"root","meaning":"drag, handle"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin intractabilis, from in- + tractare 'to handle, manage'.","mnemonic":"IN-TRACTOR-able: can't be hauled or handled, even by a tractor.","sentences":["The intractable dispute dragged on for years, with neither side willing to compromise.","She had an intractable cough that no medicine, rest, or doctor seemed able to cure.","The mule proved intractable, refusing to move no matter how much the farmer pulled and pleaded."]},"2021||beleaguered":{"def":"in a very difficult situation","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"be-","type":"prefix","meaning":"around, thoroughly"},{"part":"leaguer","type":"root","meaning":"siege camp"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"past participle"}],"origin":"From Dutch belegeren 'to besiege', from leger 'camp'.","mnemonic":"BE-LEAGUE-red: surrounded by an entire league of enemies.","sentences":["The beleaguered mayor faced angry crowds, a budget crisis, and calls for her resignation all in one week.","Beleaguered by debts and lawsuits, the little shop finally closed its doors.","Support poured in for the beleaguered team, which had lost ten straight games."]},"2021||quid pro quo":{"def":"something for something (latin); a favor or advantage granted or expected in return for something","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"quid","type":"root","meaning":"something"},{"part":"pro","type":"root","meaning":"for"},{"part":"quo","type":"root","meaning":"something"}],"origin":"From Latin quid pro quo, literally 'something for something'.","mnemonic":"QUID for QUO: pay a quid, get a quo, a fair swap.","sentences":["The senator denied that the donation was a quid pro quo, insisting he never promised anything in return.","I'll help you move this weekend, but only as a quid pro quo for borrowing your car.","Investigators looked for a quid pro quo between the contractor's gifts and the generous city contract."]},"2021||laudable":{"def":"deserving praise and commendation","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"laud","type":"root","meaning":"praise"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"worthy of"}],"origin":"From Latin laudabilis, from laudare 'to praise'.","mnemonic":"LAUD-able: LOUD applause for a worthy act.","sentences":["Her laudable effort to teach free classes earned praise from the whole neighborhood.","The company's laudable goal of recycling every package was welcomed by environmental groups.","It is laudable that he admitted his mistake publicly instead of blaming anyone else."]},"2021||introjection":{"def":"an unconscious adoption of the ideas or beliefs of of others","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"intro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"inward"},{"part":"ject","type":"root","meaning":"throw"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin intro- 'inward' + jacere 'to throw'; coined in psychology in the early 1900s.","mnemonic":"INTRO-JECTION: throwing others' beliefs INTO your own head.","sentences":["Therapists described his harsh self-criticism as an introjection of his father's stern voice.","In psychology class, introjection was explained as absorbing a parent's values until they feel like your own.","Through introjection, the child adopted her teachers' opinions without ever questioning where they came from."]},"2021||disrepute":{"def":"the state of being held in low esteem by the public","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again"},{"part":"put","type":"root","meaning":"think, reckon"},{"part":"-e","type":"suffix","meaning":"noun ending"}],"origin":"From dis- + repute, from Latin reputare 'to think over, reckon'.","mnemonic":"DIS-REPUTE: your reputation DISsolves.","sentences":["The bribery scandal brought the once-proud institution into disrepute across the entire country.","Several players' repeated misconduct on and off the field threatened to bring the sport into disrepute.","The doctor's name fell into disrepute after patients complained about his false promises."]},"2021||consternation":{"def":"feelings of anxiety or dismay, typically at something unexpected","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"con-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"stern","type":"root","meaning":"strew, throw down"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin consternatio, from consternare 'to throw into confusion', related to sternere 'to strew'.","mnemonic":"CON-STERN-ation: a stern shock that knocks you flat.","sentences":["To her consternation, the train pulled away just as she reached the platform.","The sudden resignation caused consternation in the office, where nobody had seen it coming.","Parents expressed consternation when the school announced it would close without any warning."]},"2021||apotheosis":{"def":"the highest point in the development of something; culmination or climax","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"apo-","type":"prefix","meaning":"from, away"},{"part":"theo","type":"root","meaning":"god"},{"part":"-sis","type":"suffix","meaning":"process"}],"origin":"From Greek apotheosis 'deification', from apotheoun 'to make a god'.","mnemonic":"APO-THEO-sis: THEO is God; raised to godlike height.","sentences":["The symphony's final movement was the apotheosis of the composer's lifelong search for beauty.","Critics called the film the apotheosis of the superhero genre, the perfect peak of the form.","For many fans, the championship game was the apotheosis of the star's brilliant career."]},"2021||recursive":{"def":"characterized by recurrence or repetition","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back, again"},{"part":"curs","type":"root","meaning":"run"},{"part":"-ive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin recurrere 'to run back', via recursion in mathematics.","mnemonic":"RE-CURS-ive: running back to itself, endlessly, like mirrors facing mirrors.","sentences":["A recursive function in programming calls itself again and again until it reaches a simple base case.","The artwork showed a recursive scene: a painting of a room containing the same painting, forever.","Her recursive definition of a tree described a trunk with smaller trees growing from each branch."]},"2021||pithy":{"def":"concise and forcefully expressive","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pith","type":"root","meaning":"core of a plant, essence"},{"part":"-y","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Old English pitha 'the core of plant stems', so 'full of essence'.","mnemonic":"PITHY: has the PITH, the juicy core, with no peel.","sentences":["His pithy reply, just four words long, silenced the entire committee and ended the argument.","The editor praised her pithy writing, which said in one line what others took a page to say.","Mark Twain was famous for pithy remarks that captured a big idea in a single sentence."]},"2021||brash":{"def":"self-assertive in a rude, noisy, or overbearing way","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"brash","type":"root","meaning":"hasty, rash"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; possibly related to rash, first recorded in the 1800s.","mnemonic":"BRASH: a BRASS band blaring loudly with no manners.","sentences":["The brash young lawyer interrupted the judge twice and bragged about never losing a case.","His brash comments at dinner embarrassed his hosts, who had never met such a loud guest.","A brash rookie, he told the veterans exactly how they should play."]},"2021||putative":{"def":"generally considered or reputed to be","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"put","type":"root","meaning":"think, reckon"},{"part":"-ative","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"}],"origin":"From Latin putativus, from putare 'to think, reckon'.","mnemonic":"PUT-ative: the one PUT forward as the father, but not proven.","sentences":["The putative author of the poem was later found to have lived two centuries after it appeared.","Police interviewed the putative leader of the gang, though no one had proven his role.","The putative benefits of the diet were widely advertised, yet no study confirmed them."]},"2021||surmise":{"def":"suppose that something is true without having evidence to confirm it","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"sur-","type":"prefix","meaning":"upon"},{"part":"mis","type":"root","meaning":"send, put (mittere)"}],"origin":"From Old French surmettre 'to accuse', from sur- 'upon' + mettre 'to put'.","mnemonic":"SUR-MISE: a SURe-ish MISE-ry guess without proof.","sentences":["From the wet footprints, we could surmise that someone had entered the house just minutes earlier.","I can only surmise why she left so suddenly, since she never explained her reasons.","Historians surmised that the village was abandoned after a plague, though no records confirmed it."]},"2021||denigrate":{"def":"criticize unfairly; disparage","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly, down"},{"part":"nigr","type":"root","meaning":"black"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"verb ending"}],"origin":"From Latin denigrare 'to blacken', from niger 'black'.","mnemonic":"DE-NIGRATE: paint someone's name black.","sentences":["It is unfair to denigrate a rival's work simply because you envy his success.","Critics tried to denigrate the young inventor, calling her designs worthless before testing them.","Good leaders encourage their staff and never denigrate people in front of their colleagues."]},"2021||reckoning":{"def":"the avenging or punishing of past mistakes or misdeeds","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"reckon","type":"root","meaning":"count, calculate"},{"part":"-ing","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Old English gerecenian 'to explain, count'; the sense 'day of reckoning' means a settling of accounts.","mnemonic":"RECKON-ing: the day the bill comes due and you finally pay.","sentences":["The tyrant believed he would never face a reckoning for his crimes, but the day finally came.","After years of ignoring the warnings, the company faced a harsh reckoning when the lawsuits arrived.","Preachers warned villagers that a day of reckoning awaited those who cheated the poor."]},"2021||reticent":{"def":"not revealing one's thoughts or feelings readily","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"back, again"},{"part":"tic","type":"root","meaning":"be silent (tacere)"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"}],"origin":"From Latin reticentem, 'keeping silent', from re- + tacere 'to be silent'.","mnemonic":"RE-TICENT: stays TIGHT and silent, keeping thoughts back.","sentences":["Normally outgoing, she was strangely reticent about her trip and changed the subject whenever it came up.","The witness was reticent on the stand, answering with one word and avoiding eye contact.","My reticent uncle rarely shared his feelings, preferring to show his love by fixing things."]},"2021||machination":{"def":"plot or scheme","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"mach","type":"root","meaning":"device, contrivance"},{"part":"-ination","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin machinatio, from machinari 'to contrive', from machina 'machine'.","mnemonic":"MACHINE-ation: a plot with gears turning behind the curtain.","sentences":["The queen's courtiers spent their days in secret machinations to win the king's favor.","Behind the company's collapse lay the machinations of a rival who had bribed two directors.","The detective unraveled the machination step by step, exposing who had arranged the fake robbery."]},"2021||eloquent":{"def":"fluent or persuasive in speaking or writing","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"e-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"loqu","type":"root","meaning":"speak"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"}],"origin":"From Latin eloquens, from eloqui 'to speak out'.","mnemonic":"E-LOQUENT: words flow OUT like a lot of quiet-song.","sentences":["The senator gave an eloquent speech that moved even her fiercest opponents to applaud.","Her eloquent letter described the hardships of war so clearly that readers wept.","Though shy, he was eloquent on paper, writing essays that were praised for their power and clarity."]},"2021||propitious":{"def":"giving or indicating a good chance of success; favorable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forward"},{"part":"pit","type":"root","meaning":"rush, seek (petere)"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin propitius 'favorable', perhaps from pro- + petere 'to seek, fly toward'.","mnemonic":"PRO-PITCH-ious: a pro pitch hits just right on a lucky day.","sentences":["The sunny weather seemed propitious for the picnic, so we packed our bags and set off.","The start-up launched at a propitious moment, just as demand for its product exploded.","Astrologers declared the date propitious for weddings, and bookings filled up for the entire month."]},"2021||latent":{"def":"existing but not yet developed or manifest; hidden; concealed","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"lat","type":"root","meaning":"lie hidden"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"adjective suffix"}],"origin":"From Latin latens, from latere 'to lie hidden'.","mnemonic":"LATENT sounds like 'late and hidden': the talent has not shown up yet.","sentences":["The virus can remain latent in the body for years before symptoms finally appear.","A teacher noticed her latent talent for music long before she ever picked up an instrument.","Latent tensions between the two groups erupted when the council cut funding for one of them."]},"2021||thrall":{"def":"the state of being in someone's power or having great power over someone","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"thrall","type":"root","meaning":"slave, servant"}],"origin":"From Old English thræl, from Old Norse thræll 'slave'.","mnemonic":"THRALL: a THRILL so strong it holds you captive.","sentences":["The young prince fell under the thrall of a cunning advisor who controlled his every decision.","Millions of viewers were held in thrall by the final episode of the drama.","For years she lived in thrall to her addiction, unable to break free."]},"2021||untrammeled":{"def":"not deprived of freedom of action or expression; not restricted or hampered","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"trammel","type":"root","meaning":"net, fetter"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"having been"}],"origin":"From un- + trammel, from Old French tramail 'net', from Latin tres 'three' + macula 'mesh'.","mnemonic":"UN-TRAM-MELD: not stuck in the TRAM tracks; completely free.","sentences":["The explorers enjoyed untrammeled freedom in the vast wilderness, with no rules or fences in sight.","He believed in untrammeled free speech, even for ideas he found offensive.","The artist painted with untrammeled imagination, ignoring every rule of the academy."]},"2021||incorrigible":{"def":"not able to be corrected, improved, or reformed","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"corrig","type":"root","meaning":"correct, set right (corrigere)"},{"part":"-ible","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin incorrigibilis, from in- + corrigere 'to correct'.","mnemonic":"IN-CORRECT-ible: cannot be corrected, a hopeless prankster.","sentences":["The incorrigible schoolboy was punished again for pulling the same prank he had been warned about.","My incorrigible uncle still tells the same terrible joke at every family dinner.","The judge called the thief an incorrigible repeat offender who had ignored every chance to change."]},"2021||inveterate":{"def":"having a particular habit, activity, or interest that is long-established and unlikely to change","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in"},{"part":"veter","type":"root","meaning":"old"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin inveteratus, 'grown old', from vetus 'old'.","mnemonic":"IN-VET-erate: old as a veteran, set in his habits.","sentences":["He was an inveterate gambler who could not walk past a casino without stopping to play.","The inveterate traveler had visited ninety countries and was already planning her next trip.","As an inveterate procrastinator, she waited until midnight to start the project due that morning."]},"2021||ignominy":{"def":"public shame or disgrace","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"i-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not (in-)"},{"part":"nomin","type":"root","meaning":"name"},{"part":"-y","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin ignominia, from in- 'not' + nomen 'name', i.e. loss of good name.","mnemonic":"IGNO-MINY: with no NAME left, you're ignored in shame.","sentences":["The disgraced official left office in ignominy, his name now a symbol of corruption.","Losing to the weakest team in the league was an ignominy the proud club could not forget.","He faced the ignominy of being fired in front of his colleagues."]},"2020||profligate":{"def":"recklessly extravagant or wasteful in the use of resources","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forward, down"},{"part":"flig","type":"root","meaning":"strike down"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin profligatus 'ruined, dissolute', from profligare 'to strike down, ruin'.","mnemonic":"PRO-FLIGHT-gate: flying through money on private flights.","sentences":["The profligate prince burned through his inheritance in two years on parties, horses, and gold carriages.","Critics attacked the government's profligate spending, which doubled the deficit in a single budget.","Her profligate habits, such as leaving every light on and wasting food, appalled her thrifty aunt."]},"2020||aspersion":{"def":"(n.) a damaging or derogatory statement; the act of slandering or defaming","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to"},{"part":"spers","type":"root","meaning":"sprinkle"},{"part":"-ion","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin aspergere 'to sprinkle on', from ad- + spargere 'to scatter'.","mnemonic":"A-SPERSION: sprinkle dirt on someone's name.","sentences":["The rival campaign cast aspersions on the senator's honesty without offering a shred of evidence.","She resented the aspersion that she had cheated, since she had studied for months.","Reporters were careful not to cast aspersions on the witness before the court reached its verdict."]},"2020||daft":{"def":"senseless, stupid, or foolish","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"daft","type":"root","meaning":"foolish, awkward"}],"origin":"From Old English gedæfte 'gentle, meek', which later shifted to 'foolish'.","mnemonic":"DAFT sounds like 'deaf-t': acting deaf to common sense.","sentences":["It was a daft idea to go swimming in the freezing sea in January, and everyone said so.","He gave a daft grin and wandered off, forgetting why he had come into the room.","Don't be daft, you cannot carry that heavy piano up the stairs by yourself."]},"2020||faculty":{"def":"a natural power or ability (of the mind)","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"facult","type":"root","meaning":"ability, power (facultas)"}],"origin":"From Latin facultas 'ability, means', from facilis 'easy, doable'.","mnemonic":"FACULTY: your mind's FACILe powers, like sight or memory.","sentences":["Even at ninety, he kept his mental faculty sharp by solving puzzles every morning.","The faculty of hearing is often the first to weaken in elderly people.","She had a remarkable faculty for remembering names after meeting people only once."]},"2020||axiom":{"def":"a universal truth; an established rule","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"axiom","type":"root","meaning":"worthy thing (axios)"}],"origin":"From Greek axioma 'that which is thought worthy', from axios 'worthy'.","mnemonic":"AXIOM: an AXE through doubt, a truth you don't question.","sentences":["It is an axiom of geometry that two points determine exactly one straight line.","The old axiom that practice makes perfect guided the coach's training plan.","Economists treat it as an axiom that people respond to incentives, and build their models on it."]},"2020||dogmatism":{"def":"the tendency to lay down principles as incontrovertibly true, without consideration of evidence or the opinions of others.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"dogma","type":"root","meaning":"opinion, belief"},{"part":"-tism","type":"suffix","meaning":"practice of"}],"origin":"From Greek dogma 'opinion, decree' + -ism, via French dogmatisme.","mnemonic":"DOG-MA-tism: a dog barking one rule at everyone, never listening.","sentences":["His dogmatism made debate impossible, since he dismissed every opposing view without listening.","Scientists must avoid dogmatism and revise their theories when new evidence appears.","The committee's dogmatism blocked reform for years, as members insisted their rules were beyond question."]},"2020||subvert":{"def":"overturn or overthrow from the foundation; undermine","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"sub-","type":"prefix","meaning":"from below"},{"part":"vert","type":"root","meaning":"turn"}],"origin":"From Latin subvertere 'to overthrow', from sub- + vertere 'to turn'.","mnemonic":"SUB-VERT: turn things from below, like a submarine tipping a ship.","sentences":["Rebels tried to subvert the government by spreading rumors and bribing a few generals.","The comedian likes to subvert expectations, setting up a familiar joke and then turning it upside down.","Cheating would subvert the whole purpose of the exam, which is to measure honest learning."]},"2020||heuristic":{"def":"serving to indicate or point out; stimulating interest as a means of furthering investigation.","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"heur","type":"root","meaning":"find, discover"},{"part":"-istic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek heuriskein, 'to find', via German heuristisch.","mnemonic":"A heuristic is a hint that helps you find the answer yourself: Eureka!","sentences":["The teacher used a heuristic approach, posing riddles that pushed students to discover the underlying rule themselves.","Her heuristic questions sparked fresh curiosity and led the team toward several new lines of investigation.","Rather than lecturing, the guide offered a heuristic nudge and let the visitors work out the puzzle."]},"2020||aberration":{"def":"a departure from what is normal, usual, or expected, typically one that is unwelcome","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ab-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away from"},{"part":"err","type":"root","meaning":"wander"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act or state of"}],"origin":"From Latin aberrare, 'to wander away'.","mnemonic":"An aberration is a mistake that wanders away from the path.","sentences":["The warm January day was a weather aberration in a city that usually freezes through the winter.","Her one failed exam was an aberration in an otherwise flawless academic record.","Scientists treated the odd reading as an aberration until repeated tests showed the instrument was working fine."]},"2020||tenuous":{"def":"thin, slender, not dense; lacking clarity or sharpness; of slight importance or significance; lacking a sound basis, poorly supported","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"tenu","type":"root","meaning":"thin"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin tenuis, 'thin, slender'.","mnemonic":"Tenuous sounds like 'tenuis': thin as a spider thread holding up a hammock.","sentences":["The detective admitted that the link between the two crimes was tenuous and hardly worth presenting to a jury.","A tenuous strand of smoke rose from the chimney before vanishing into the pale morning air.","His claim to the inheritance rested on a tenuous connection to a distant, long-dead cousin."]},"2020||fickle":{"def":"liable to change very rapidly, erratic; marked by a lack of constancy or steadiness, inconsistent","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"fickle","type":"root","meaning":"changeable, deceitful"}],"origin":"From Old English ficol, 'deceitful, treacherous'.","mnemonic":"Fickle sounds like 'pickle': one day you love them, next day you don't.","sentences":["The fickle weather swung from sunshine to hail and back again within a single afternoon.","Fans can be fickle, cheering a striker one week and booing him the next.","She grew tired of her fickle friend, who changed plans and opinions every few hours."]},"2020||ineffable":{"def":"indescribable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"eff","type":"root","meaning":"speak out"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin ineffabilis, 'unutterable', from in- + effari 'to speak out'.","mnemonic":"In-effable: cannot be effed, meaning spoken, out loud; words fail.","sentences":["Standing at the canyon rim at dawn, she felt an ineffable joy that no poem could capture.","The musician tried to explain the ineffable beauty of the melody but finally gave up and simply played it.","Grief of that kind is ineffable, and clumsy words from friends only seemed to shrink it."]},"2020||pretext":{"def":"a false reason, deceptive excuse","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before"},{"part":"text","type":"root","meaning":"woven, written"}],"origin":"From Latin praetextum, 'outward display', from prae- + texere 'to weave'.","mnemonic":"A pre-text is a story woven before the real reason shows.","sentences":["He used a fake dentist appointment as a pretext to leave work early and watch the match.","The government invaded on the pretext of protecting its citizens, though few believed that was the real reason.","She called under the pretext of returning a book, but really wanted to ask about the job."]},"2020||assiduous":{"def":"showing great care and perseverance","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"as-","type":"prefix","meaning":"to, toward"},{"part":"sid","type":"root","meaning":"sit"},{"part":"-uous","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"}],"origin":"From Latin assiduus, 'constantly sitting', from assidere 'to sit beside'.","mnemonic":"Assiduous students sit at the desk so long they could almost be glued.","sentences":["The assiduous librarian checked every shelf twice, determined that no book would ever be misplaced.","Through assiduous practice, the young pianist mastered the difficult sonata in just three months.","Her assiduous attention to detail meant the final report contained virtually no errors."]},"2020||desultory":{"def":"lacking in consistency, constancy, or visible order, disconnected; fitful","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"from, down"},{"part":"sult","type":"root","meaning":"leap"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin desultorius, 'of a leaper', from desultor, a circus rider who leapt between horses.","mnemonic":"Desultory: de-sultry leaping, hopping from topic to topic without a plan.","sentences":["The meeting dragged on with desultory chatter that jumped from budgets to holiday plans and back again.","He made a few desultory attempts to tidy the garage before giving up and watching television.","Their desultory conversation drifted aimlessly from the weather to politics to nothing in particular."]},"2020||trepidation":{"def":"a feeling of fear or agitation about something that may happen","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"trepid","type":"root","meaning":"tremble, alarmed"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin trepidare, 'to tremble', from trepidus 'alarmed'.","mnemonic":"Trepidation: your trembling legs tap-dance before the big stage.","sentences":["She approached the principal's office with trepidation, unsure what she had been summoned to discuss.","The climber looked down with trepidation, his hands shaking on the narrow ledge.","Voters entered the polling station with trepidation, fearing the results might upend their lives."]},"2020||ostensible":{"def":"stated or appearing to be true, but not necessarily so","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"os-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"tens","type":"root","meaning":"stretch, show"},{"part":"-ible","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin ostendere, 'to show', from obs- + tendere 'to stretch'.","mnemonic":"Ostensible: on the OSTEN-sign, it SHOWS one thing, but who knows.","sentences":["The ostensible purpose of the visit was a courtesy call, though everyone suspected a negotiation.","His ostensible reason for quitting was family, but rumors pointed to a dispute with the boss.","The company's ostensible goal is charity, yet its books show most money funds executive perks."]},"2020||austere":{"def":"severe or stern in manner; without adornment or luxury, simple, plain; harsh or sour in flavor","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"auster","type":"root","meaning":"harsh, dry"}],"origin":"From Greek austeros, 'harsh, dry', via Latin austerus.","mnemonic":"Austere sounds like 'a stare': a stern, bare room with a cold glare.","sentences":["The monastery's austere cells held only a narrow bed and a wooden stool.","Her austere manner discouraged students from joking or chatting in her classroom.","The government adopted an austere budget, cutting luxuries and holding spending to the bare essentials."]},"2020||perverse":{"def":"willfully determined or disposed to go counter to what is expected or desired; contrary","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"per-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly, away"},{"part":"vers","type":"root","meaning":"turn"}],"origin":"From Latin perversus, 'turned the wrong way', from pervertere.","mnemonic":"Perverse people turn the wrong way on purpose, like a stubborn toddler.","sentences":["Out of perverse stubbornness, he ordered the dish the waiter had specifically warned him against.","It seemed perverse that the harder she studied, the worse her scores became.","The child's perverse streak showed whenever she was told to wear a coat and instantly refused."]},"2020||zeal":{"def":"great energy or enthusiasm in pursuit of a cause or an objective","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"zeal","type":"root","meaning":"ardor, jealousy"}],"origin":"From Greek zelos, 'ardor, eager rivalry', via Latin zelus.","mnemonic":"Zeal sounds like 'Zeal-ous Zealot': burning energy for the cause.","sentences":["The new volunteers attacked the cleanup with such zeal that the beach was spotless by noon.","Her zeal for reform inspired colleagues who had long since grown tired of the old routines.","He pursued the championship with religious zeal, training before dawn every single day."]},"2020||impetuous":{"def":"acting or done quickly and without thought or care","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"in-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward, against"},{"part":"pet","type":"root","meaning":"rush at, seek"},{"part":"-uous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin impetuosus, 'violent, rushing', from impetus 'attack'.","mnemonic":"Impetuous: an impetus of energy, dashing off before thinking.","sentences":["The impetuous young manager quit on the spot, then regretted it before lunch.","Her impetuous decision to buy the car without a test drive cost her dearly.","Teenagers can be impetuous, leaping into plans with more enthusiasm than forethought."]},"2020||frivolous":{"def":"not having any serious purpose or value","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"frivol","type":"root","meaning":"silly, worthless"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin frivolus, 'silly, trifling'.","mnemonic":"Frivolous sounds like 'fry-volume': a lot of fluff, nothing serious.","sentences":["The judge dismissed the frivolous lawsuit, which claimed a restaurant had caused emotional distress by serving cold soup.","She spent her bonus on frivolous trinkets rather than saving anything for emergencies.","The committee refused to waste time on frivolous proposals that had no practical purpose."]},"2020||equanimity":{"def":"calmness, composure, refusal to panic","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"equ","type":"root","meaning":"equal, even"},{"part":"anim","type":"root","meaning":"mind, spirit"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin aequanimitas, from aequus 'even' + animus 'mind'.","mnemonic":"Equanimity: an equal, even mind that stays level in storms.","sentences":["The pilot met the sudden turbulence with equanimity, calmly reassuring the nervous passengers.","She accepted the bad news with equanimity, showing no sign of panic or anger.","Years of meditation had given him an equanimity that criticism could hardly disturb."]},"2020||reprise":{"def":"a recurrence, renewal, or resumption of an action","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"re-","type":"prefix","meaning":"again"},{"part":"prise","type":"root","meaning":"taken, seized"}],"origin":"From Old French reprendre, 'to take up again', from Latin reprehendere.","mnemonic":"A reprise: re-prize, grabbing the song again for an encore.","sentences":["The band closed the show with a reprise of their opening song, delighting the crowd.","The film's final scene offered a quiet reprise of the theme heard in its first minutes.","Critics noted the sequel was merely a reprise of the original, with little new to say."]},"2020||portent":{"def":"a sign or forewarning of something momentous or calamitous to come","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"por-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth"},{"part":"tent","type":"root","meaning":"stretch, hold"}],"origin":"From Latin portendere, 'to foretell', from por- + tendere 'to stretch'.","mnemonic":"A portent is a warning port-ent: a sign sent before the storm.","sentences":["Farmers regarded the unusual comet as a portent of famine and war.","The sudden silence of the birds was an eerie portent of the earthquake that followed.","Many analysts saw the bank's collapse as a portent of wider economic trouble ahead."]},"2020||pedantic":{"def":"overly concerned with minute details or formalisms, especially in teaching","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pedant","type":"root","meaning":"schoolmaster, teacher"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Italian pedante, 'schoolmaster', possibly from Greek paideuein 'to teach'.","mnemonic":"Pedantic: ped-ant picking at every tiny detail like a fussy teacher.","sentences":["His pedantic habit of correcting everyone's grammar made dinner conversations exhausting for his friends.","The professor grew pedantic about footnotes, rejecting papers over a misplaced comma.","Some readers find the author's pedantic attention to tiny historical details tedious rather than impressive."]},"2020||ostentatious":{"def":"marked by conspicuous or pretentious display to impress others","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"os-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"tent","type":"root","meaning":"stretch, show"},{"part":"-atious","type":"suffix","meaning":"characterized by"}],"origin":"From Latin ostentare, 'to display', related to ostendere 'to show'.","mnemonic":"Ostentatious: tents of showiness stretched out so everyone notices.","sentences":["The millionaire's ostentatious gold-plated car drew stares on every street he drove down.","She avoided ostentatious jewelry, preferring a single quiet pendant to a cascade of diamonds.","The ostentatious wedding, complete with fireworks and a live orchestra, was mostly meant to impress the neighbors."]},"2020||pretense":{"def":"an attempt to make something that is not the case appear true","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"pre-","type":"prefix","meaning":"before, forward"},{"part":"tense","type":"root","meaning":"stretch, hold out"}],"origin":"From Latin praetendere, 'to stretch in front', via Anglo-Norman.","mnemonic":"Pretense: stretching a pretty mask in front of the truth.","sentences":["He kept up the pretense of being wealthy, though he secretly struggled to pay rent.","Under the pretense of checking the pipes, the stranger scanned the whole house.","She dropped all pretense and admitted she had never read the book."]},"2020||disport":{"def":"enjoy oneself unrestrainedly; frolic","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away"},{"part":"port","type":"root","meaning":"carry"}],"origin":"From Old French desporter, 'to carry away, amuse', from des- + porter.","mnemonic":"Dis-port: carry yourself away from work to play in the harbor.","sentences":["Children disport themselves in the shallow waves while their parents doze on the sand.","Dolphins disported alongside the ferry, leaping and twisting for the delighted tourists.","After the exams ended, the students disported on the lawn until the sun went down."]},"2020||numinous":{"def":"having a strong religious or spiritual quality; indicating or suggesting the presence of a divinity","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"numin","type":"root","meaning":"divine power, nod of a god"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin numen, 'divine power', popularized by Rudolf Otto in 1917.","mnemonic":"Numinous sounds like 'luminous': a glowing, sacred presence in the cathedral.","sentences":["The vast empty cathedral had a numinous hush that made even tourists lower their voices.","Mountain dawns felt numinous to the pilgrims, as if a god were quietly present.","The painter tried to capture the numinous light streaming through the temple's ancient doorway."]},"2020||propagate":{"def":"to multiply, spread out","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"pro-","type":"prefix","meaning":"forth"},{"part":"pag","type":"root","meaning":"fasten, plant"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin propagare, 'to set cuttings, multiply', from propages 'a layer of a plant'.","mnemonic":"Propagate: pro-pagate, plant cuttings that spread like gossip.","sentences":["Gardeners propagate roses by planting cuttings in damp soil until new roots appear.","Rumors propagate quickly online, often reaching millions before anyone checks the facts.","The missionaries worked to propagate their faith across the remote island villages."]},"2020||commensurate":{"def":"corresponding in size, degree, or amount; porportionate","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"com-","type":"prefix","meaning":"together, with"},{"part":"mens","type":"root","meaning":"measure"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"having"}],"origin":"From Latin commensuratus, from com- + mensurare 'to measure'.","mnemonic":"Commensurate: measured together, so reward fits the effort.","sentences":["The job offers a salary commensurate with experience, so veterans earn more than newcomers.","Punishment should be commensurate with the offense, not wildly harsher than the crime.","She expected rewards commensurate with her long hours, but received only a thank-you note."]},"2020||abdicate":{"def":"to renounce or relinquish a throne, right, power, claim, responsibility, or the like, especially in a formal manner","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ab-","type":"prefix","meaning":"away"},{"part":"dic","type":"root","meaning":"proclaim, declare"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin abdicare, 'to renounce', from ab- + dicare 'to proclaim'.","mnemonic":"Ab-dic-ate: declare yourself away from the throne.","sentences":["The aging king chose to abdicate in favor of his daughter rather than rule from a sickbed.","Parents who abdicate their responsibilities at home often leave their children to raise themselves.","Edward VIII shocked the nation when he decided to abdicate the throne in 1936."]},"2020||esoteric":{"def":"intended for or likely to be understood by only a small number of people with a specialized knowledge or interest","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"eso","type":"root","meaning":"within"},{"part":"ter","type":"root","meaning":"comparative"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek esoterikos, 'inner', from eso 'within'.","mnemonic":"Esoteric: an inside secret shared in an exclusive club.","sentences":["The professor's lecture on esoteric medieval manuscripts left most of the class baffled.","Collecting rare coins is an esoteric hobby that only a handful of enthusiasts truly understand.","The software's esoteric commands were known to only a few specialist engineers."]},"2020||nascent":{"def":"starting to develop; coming into existence","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"nasc","type":"root","meaning":"be born"},{"part":"-ent","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Latin nascens, 'being born', from nasci 'to be born'.","mnemonic":"Nascent shares a root with 'natal': newborn and just emerging.","sentences":["The nascent startup had only three employees and a borrowed garage for an office.","Scientists are studying the nascent signs of life in the newly formed lake.","A nascent friendship between the two rivals began to bloom after the trip."]},"2020||valence":{"def":"the value an outcome holds for the person contemplating it","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"val","type":"root","meaning":"be strong, worth"},{"part":"-ence","type":"suffix","meaning":"state or quality"}],"origin":"From Latin valentia, 'strength, capacity', from valere 'to be strong'.","mnemonic":"Valence: the 'value' something has, positive or negative, pulling you in.","sentences":["Psychologists measured the valence of each image, noting how positive or negative it felt to participants.","Because the prize had high valence for him, he worked tirelessly to win it.","The campaign gave the policy a negative valence, so voters recoiled from it instinctively."]},"2020||exhortation":{"def":"an address or communication emphatically urging someone to do something","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"thoroughly"},{"part":"hort","type":"root","meaning":"urge, encourage"},{"part":"-ation","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin exhortari, 'to encourage strongly', from ex- + hortari 'to urge'.","mnemonic":"Exhortation: an ex-HORT-ation, a coach shouting hortatory urges.","sentences":["The coach's passionate exhortation to play harder lifted the exhausted team in the final minutes.","Her exhortation to vote in the election echoed across the crowded campus.","Despite repeated exhortations to save money, he spent his entire paycheck within the week."]},"2020||vestigial":{"def":"forming a very small remnant of something that was once much larger or more noticeable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"vestig","type":"root","meaning":"footprint, trace"},{"part":"-ial","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin vestigium, 'footprint, trace'.","mnemonic":"Vestigial: a vestige is a footprint, a faint trace left behind.","sentences":["The human tailbone is a vestigial structure, a tiny remnant of the long tails our distant ancestors once had.","The old rail station kept only a vestigial platform, long since overgrown with weeds.","Whales still carry vestigial hip bones buried in their bodies, evidence of their land-walking ancestors."]},"2020||menial":{"def":"(adj.) lowly, humble, lacking importance or dignity; (n.) a person","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"men","type":"root","meaning":"stay, household"},{"part":"-ial","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Anglo-Norman meinie, 'household', from Latin mansio 'dwelling'.","mnemonic":"Menial: a mean-ial job, like scrubbing the household floor.","sentences":["He took a menial job washing dishes just to cover his rent while he searched for better work.","The company refused to assign menial tasks like filing to its senior engineers.","She started in menial roles, sweeping floors and carrying boxes, before rising to manager."]},"2020||cursory":{"def":"going rapidly over something, without noticing details; hasty; superficial","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"curs","type":"root","meaning":"run"},{"part":"-ory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin cursorius, 'of a runner', from currere 'to run'.","mnemonic":"Cursory: you just run through it like a cursor skimming a page.","sentences":["The inspector gave the building only a cursory glance and missed the cracks in the foundation.","A cursory reading of the contract hid the clause that later caused the dispute.","He made a cursory check of the doors before dashing off to catch his flight."]},"2020||ubiquitous":{"def":"present, appearing, or found everywhere","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ubi","type":"root","meaning":"where, everywhere"},{"part":"-quit","type":"root","meaning":"quite"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin ubique, 'everywhere', from ubi 'where'.","mnemonic":"Ubiquitous sounds like 'you be quite' everywhere: it's all over.","sentences":["Smartphones are now ubiquitous, found in nearly every pocket from big cities to remote villages.","The ubiquitous scent of coffee filled every corner of the Seattle neighborhood.","Chain stores became ubiquitous, so every town center started to look exactly alike."]},"2020||delineate":{"def":"to portray, sketch, or describe in accurate and vivid detail; to represent pictorially","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"completely"},{"part":"lin","type":"root","meaning":"line"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin delineare, 'to sketch out', from de- + linea 'line'.","mnemonic":"De-LINE-ate: draw the lines of it clearly.","sentences":["The map will delineate the border between the two countries with a thick red line.","In her essay, she delineated the causes of the war in careful and vivid detail.","The contract must delineate each partner's duties so no one argues later about who does what."]},"2020||delimit":{"def":"determine the limits or boundaries of","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"de-","type":"prefix","meaning":"completely"},{"part":"limit","type":"root","meaning":"boundary"}],"origin":"From Latin delimitare, 'to mark the boundaries', from de- + limitare.","mnemonic":"De-LIMIT: put the limit on it, fence in its borders.","sentences":["Surveyors were hired to delimit the property so the neighbors would stop arguing over the fence.","The treaty seeks to delimit the territory each nation may fish in.","Researchers must delimit their study to one age group, or the data becomes unmanageable."]},"2020||credulity":{"def":"readiness to believe","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"cred","type":"root","meaning":"believe"},{"part":"-ul","type":"suffix","meaning":"inclined to"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin credulitas, from credere 'to believe'.","mnemonic":"Credulity: you give too much credit, believing everything.","sentences":["The con artist counted on the credulity of tourists who would believe any story about a lost fortune.","His credulity was so great that he accepted every chain email as absolute truth.","Skeptics mocked the crowd's credulity when thousands lined up for the miracle cure."]},"2020||extricate":{"def":"to free from entanglements or difficulties; to remove with effort","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out of"},{"part":"tric","type":"root","meaning":"hindrances, tangles"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin extricare, 'to disentangle', from ex- + tricae 'hindrances'.","mnemonic":"Extricate: pull yourself out of the tricky tangle.","sentences":["Rescuers worked for hours to extricate the driver from the crumpled car.","She struggled to extricate herself from the awkward conversation and slip away.","The lawyer hoped to extricate his client from the legal mess without a trial."]},"2020||envisage":{"def":"contemplate or conceive of as a possibility or a desirable future event","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, make"},{"part":"vis","type":"root","meaning":"see, face"},{"part":"-age","type":"suffix","meaning":"action, result"}],"origin":"From French envisager, 'to look in the face', from en- + visage.","mnemonic":"Envisage: put a future face on it in your mind's eye.","sentences":["Few experts could envisage a world where cars drive themselves through busy streets.","The architect envisaged a gleaming glass tower rising high above the old harbor and its crowded market.","Can you really envisage yourself living in another country for the rest of your adult life?"]},"2020||amenable":{"def":"open to or willing to follow advice or suggestion; malleable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"men","type":"root","meaning":"lead, threaten"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Old French amener, 'to bring to', from a- + mener 'to lead'.","mnemonic":"Amenable: easy to lead, like a friendly 'amen' to any idea.","sentences":["The usually stubborn client proved amenable to changes once we explained the cost savings.","She was amenable to suggestions and quickly adjusted her plan after hearing his advice.","Young puppies are amenable to training when rewarded with treats and gentle encouragement."]},"2020||affinity":{"def":"a spontaneous or natural liking or sympathy for someone or something","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"af-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"fin","type":"root","meaning":"border, end"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin affinis, 'bordering on, related', from ad- + finis.","mnemonic":"Affinity: a natural link, as if you share a border.","sentences":["She felt a strong affinity for the quiet coastal town the moment she arrived.","Twins often share a deep affinity that seems to go well beyond ordinary friendship or family loyalty.","The chemist noted the metal's affinity for oxygen, which makes it rust quickly."]},"2020||volition":{"def":"the act of willing","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"vol","type":"root","meaning":"wish, will"},{"part":"-ition","type":"suffix","meaning":"act of"}],"origin":"From Latin volo, 'I wish, I will'.","mnemonic":"Volition: your volume knob of free will.","sentences":["He signed the papers of his own volition, without any pressure from his family.","The prisoner insisted he had confessed against his volition after hours of questioning.","Philosophers debate whether volition truly exists or merely feels real to the brain."]},"2020||minatory":{"def":"threatening; menacing","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"min","type":"root","meaning":"threaten, jut out"},{"part":"-atory","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin minari, 'to threaten', from minae 'threats'.","mnemonic":"Minatory: a MINAce, a menacing threat.","sentences":["The dog gave a low, minatory growl as the stranger approached the gate.","Dark clouds gathered in a minatory mass above the village, and the farmers hurried home before the storm.","His minatory tone left the employees in no doubt that someone would be fired."]},"2020||untenable":{"def":"(adj.) not capable of being held or defended; impossible to maintain","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"un-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"ten","type":"root","meaning":"hold"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From un- + Latin tenere, 'to hold'.","mnemonic":"Untenable: cannot be held, like a tent in a hurricane.","sentences":["After the evidence emerged, the minister's position became untenable and he resigned within days.","Critics called the theory untenable, since it contradicted nearly every known experiment performed over the last century.","Living in the flooded house had become untenable after the third week, so the family moved in with relatives."]},"2020||stipulate":{"def":"to arrange specifically; to require as a condition of agreement","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"stipul","type":"root","meaning":"a stalk, agreement"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin stipulari, 'to demand a formal promise'.","mnemonic":"Stipulate: a strict pledge, like swearing on a stalk of straw.","sentences":["The contract stipulates that rent must be paid by the first of every month.","She stipulated that her donation be used only for children's education, and nothing else, or she would withdraw it.","Before agreeing to the deal, he stipulated that his name be kept off the building."]},"2020||iterate":{"def":"to perform or utter repeatedly","pos":"verb","parts":[{"part":"iter","type":"root","meaning":"again"},{"part":"-ate","type":"suffix","meaning":"to make"}],"origin":"From Latin iterare, 'to repeat', from iterum 'again'.","mnemonic":"Iterate: it-er-ate it again and again.","sentences":["Designers iterate on a prototype, improving it a little with each new round of testing.","The program will iterate through every name in the list until it finds a match.","The teacher asked him to iterate the instructions slowly so no one would miss a step."]},"2020||multifarious":{"def":"having many different parts, elements, forms, etc.","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"multi-","type":"prefix","meaning":"many"},{"part":"fari","type":"root","meaning":"speak, kinds"},{"part":"-ous","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin multifarius, 'manifold', from multi- + -farius.","mnemonic":"Multifarious: multi-many, a thousand kinds in one basket.","sentences":["The market sold multifarious goods, from spices and silk to antique clocks and live chickens.","Her multifarious duties included budgeting, hiring, training, and fixing the office printer.","The city's multifarious cultures made every neighborhood feel like a different country."]},"2020||discursive":{"def":"passing aimlessly from one place or subject to another, rambling, roving, nomadic","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"dis-","type":"prefix","meaning":"apart, in different directions"},{"part":"curs","type":"root","meaning":"run"},{"part":"-ive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin discursus, 'running about', from dis- + currere 'to run'.","mnemonic":"Discursive: his words run in different directions like unleashed dogs.","sentences":["The author's discursive style wandered from recipes to politics to childhood memories within a single page.","He gave a discursive speech that touched on dozens of topics but never reached a conclusion.","Her discursive letters rambled pleasantly from the weather to her cat to the price of bread."]},"2020||entrenched":{"def":"firmly established","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"en-","type":"prefix","meaning":"in, put into"},{"part":"trench","type":"root","meaning":"ditch"},{"part":"-ed","type":"suffix","meaning":"past participle"}],"origin":"From en- + Old French trenche, 'ditch', via Latin truncare.","mnemonic":"Entrenched: dug in a deep trench and not budging.","sentences":["Corruption in the department was so entrenched that reformers despaired of ever rooting it out.","His entrenched habits made it nearly impossible for him to adopt the new software.","The soldiers remained entrenched on the hillside for weeks, refusing to give ground."]},"2020||auspicious":{"def":"conducive to success; favorable","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"auspic","type":"root","meaning":"bird watching, omen"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin auspicium, 'divination by birds', from avis 'bird' + specere 'to look'.","mnemonic":"Auspicious: watching the birds fly and finding good omens.","sentences":["The sunny weather made for an auspicious start to the festival, and organizers predicted record crowds all weekend.","Her auspicious debut, a sold-out concert, hinted at a bright musical career.","They waited for an auspicious day, according to the calendar, before opening the new shop."]},"2020||poignant":{"def":"evoking a keen sense of sadness or regret","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"poign","type":"root","meaning":"pierce, prick"},{"part":"-ant","type":"suffix","meaning":"being"}],"origin":"From Old French poignant, 'pricking', from Latin pungere 'to prick'.","mnemonic":"Poignant: a point that pricks the heart.","sentences":["The old photograph of the smiling soldiers was a poignant reminder of the friends who never returned.","The film ended with a poignant scene of a father waving goodbye at the train station.","She delivered a poignant farewell speech that left even the toughest colleagues wiping their eyes."]},"2020||expendable":{"def":"not worth keeping; not essential","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"ex-","type":"prefix","meaning":"out"},{"part":"pend","type":"root","meaning":"weigh, hang"},{"part":"-able","type":"suffix","meaning":"able to be"}],"origin":"From Latin expendere, 'to weigh out, pay', from ex- + pendere.","mnemonic":"Expendable: you can spend it and not miss it.","sentences":["The captain decided the extra supplies were expendable and tossed them overboard to lighten the boat.","Workers feared they were expendable, to be replaced the moment a cheaper option appeared.","Early prototypes are often expendable, built to be tested to destruction and then discarded."]},"2020||sanguine":{"def":"optimistic or positive, especially in an apparently bad or difficult situation","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"sangu","type":"root","meaning":"blood"},{"part":"-ine","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Latin sanguineus, 'of blood', from sanguis; the 'blood' humor was linked to cheerfulness.","mnemonic":"Sanguine: sang-GWIN, blood in the cheeks and a rosy outlook.","sentences":["Despite the dismal polls, the candidate remained sanguine about her chances of winning.","He was sanguine about the surgery, convinced that everything would turn out fine.","Investors stayed sanguine even as the market dipped, expecting a quick recovery."]},"2020||propriety":{"def":"the state or quality of conforming to conventionally accepted standards of behavior or morals","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"propri","type":"root","meaning":"one's own, proper"},{"part":"-ety","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin proprietas, 'ownership, peculiarity', from proprius 'one's own'.","mnemonic":"Propriety: acting proper, as the rules require.","sentences":["The headmaster insisted on propriety, so students had to address teachers as sir or ma'am.","She questioned the propriety of accepting an expensive gift from a client.","Victorian society placed great importance on propriety, especially in matters of dress and speech."]},"2020||quandary":{"def":"a state of perplexity or uncertainty over what to do in a difficult situation","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"quandary","type":"root","meaning":"state of doubt"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; first recorded in the late 16th century.","mnemonic":"Quandary: 'quan-dairy', stuck choosing between milk and cream.","sentences":["She was in a quandary over whether to accept the new job or stay with her friends.","The council faced a quandary: raise taxes or close the beloved public library.","His quandary deepened when both of his best friends asked him to be their best man."]},"2020||pragmatic":{"def":"dealing with things sensibly and realistically in a way that is based on practical rather than theoretical considerations","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"pragmat","type":"root","meaning":"deed, business"},{"part":"-ic","type":"suffix","meaning":"relating to"}],"origin":"From Greek pragmatikos, 'fit for action', from pragma 'deed'.","mnemonic":"Pragmatic: practical, a person who does the work, not the dream.","sentences":["Rather than chase an ideal solution, she took a pragmatic approach and fixed the most urgent problem first.","The pragmatic mayor focused on repairing roads instead of building grand monuments.","He gave pragmatic advice: forget the dream house and buy something you can actually afford."]},"2020||veracity":{"def":"conformity to facts; accuracy","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"ver","type":"root","meaning":"true"},{"part":"-acity","type":"suffix","meaning":"quality of"}],"origin":"From Latin veracitas, from verax 'truthful', from verus 'true'.","mnemonic":"Veracity: VERy true, like 'verify'.","sentences":["The journalist checked every source to confirm the veracity of the shocking story.","Jurors doubted the witness's veracity after hearing three different versions of his account.","Nobody questioned the veracity of her statement, since she had never been caught in a lie."]},"2020||abeyance":{"def":"a state of temporary disuse or suspension","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"a-","type":"prefix","meaning":"toward"},{"part":"bey","type":"root","meaning":"gape, wait"}],"origin":"From Old French abeance, 'a gaping at', from a- + bayer 'to gape'.","mnemonic":"Abeyance: the plan is on hold, waiting to say 'ah-bey'.","sentences":["The project was held in abeyance until the funding questions were finally resolved.","Their rivalry remained in abeyance during the crisis, as both sides worked together to survive.","The law has fallen into abeyance, though it has never been formally repealed."]},"2020||qualm":{"def":"an uneasy feeling of doubt, worry, or fear, especially about one's own conduct; a misgiving.","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"qualm","type":"root","meaning":"sick feeling, doubt"}],"origin":"Origin uncertain; possibly from Old English cwealm 'death, plague'.","mnemonic":"Qualm: your calm is gone and your conscience squirms.","sentences":["He felt no qualm about returning the extra change the cashier had accidentally handed him.","She had a sudden qualm about signing the contract without reading the fine print.","The lawyer admitted a qualm or two about defending a client he knew was guilty."]},"2020||rash":{"def":"displaying or proceeding from a lack of careful consideration of the possible consequences of an action","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"rash","type":"root","meaning":"hasty, quick"}],"origin":"From Middle Dutch or Low German rasch, 'quick, hasty'.","mnemonic":"A rash decision breaks out like an itchy skin rash: fast and regrettable.","sentences":["It would be rash to quit your job before you have found another one.","His rash promise to finish the project overnight left the whole team scrambling.","Making rash purchases when you are angry usually leads to regret by morning."]},"2020||fastidious":{"def":"very attentive to and concerned about accuracy and detail","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"fastid","type":"root","meaning":"disgust, pride"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin fastidiosus, 'disdainful, squeamish', from fastidium 'loathing'.","mnemonic":"Fastidious: so fussy that fast, tidy ideas are all that please.","sentences":["The fastidious chef inspected every plate for stray crumbs before it left the kitchen.","She was fastidious about her handwriting, rewriting entire pages over one crooked letter.","Fastidious accountants double-check every figure so that not a single decimal point is wrong."]},"2020||captious":{"def":"excessively ready to find fault; given to petty criticism; intended to trap, confuse, or show up","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"capt","type":"root","meaning":"take, seize"},{"part":"-ious","type":"suffix","meaning":"full of"}],"origin":"From Latin captiosus, 'sophistical, fallacious', from capere 'to take'.","mnemonic":"Captious: captures every little flaw to catch you out.","sentences":["The captious critic seized on a single typo and ignored the book's many strengths.","His captious remarks about everyone's work made the team reluctant to share ideas.","She asked a captious question designed to trap the speaker into contradicting himself."]},"2020||furtive":{"def":"attempting to avoid notice or attention, typically because of guilt or a belief that discovery would lead to trouble; secretive","pos":"adj","parts":[{"part":"furt","type":"root","meaning":"theft, thief"},{"part":"-ive","type":"suffix","meaning":"tending to"}],"origin":"From Latin furtivus, 'stolen, hidden', from furtum 'theft'.","mnemonic":"Furtive: a thief's sneaky glance over the shoulder.","sentences":["The boy cast a furtive glance around the kitchen before sneaking a cookie from the jar.","They exchanged furtive whispers in the back row, hoping the teacher would not notice.","A furtive figure slipped out of the alley and vanished into the crowd."]},"2020||impunity":{"def":"exemption from punishment or harm","pos":"noun","parts":[{"part":"im-","type":"prefix","meaning":"not"},{"part":"pun","type":"root","meaning":"punish"},{"part":"-ity","type":"suffix","meaning":"state of"}],"origin":"From Latin impunitas, 'freedom from punishment', from poena 'penalty'.","mnemonic":"Impunity: not punished, so you walk off free like a rogue with a pun.","sentences":["Smugglers operated with impunity because the border guards had been bribed to look away.","The dictator ruled with impunity, knowing no court in his country would dare charge him.","Critics argue that corporations pollute with impunity when fines are smaller than the profits."]}};
